ophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 34}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔστω (ἤτω {{LXX+5th c.BC+|LXX.Psa.103.31, and late Inscrr., “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2664, +others; but in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 361c to be read ἴτω), _Doric dialect_ εἴτω, ἤτω, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1411.21, Elean ἤστω “Schwyzer” 424}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔστωσαν, but ἔστων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 502a, ὄντων [prev. author] “Lg.” 879b}}, and early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12.22, etc. (ἔστωσαν first in 2nd c.BC, [prev. work]22.1328), _Doric dialect_ ἐόντων [prev. work]1126:}}_subjunctive_ ὦ, ᾖς, ᾖ, _Epic dialect_ ἔω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.18}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἔῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.300, +others (also ἔῃσι 2.366, +others, ᾖσι (ν) 19.202, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 294)}}, also _Boeotian dialect_ ἔνθω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3172.165, μετείω 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.47 and perhaps εἴῃ 9.245, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὦντι {{Refs|“SIG” 940.3 (from Crete), ἔωντι “GDI” 5040.14 (from Hierapytna)}}, _Boeotian dialect_ ἴωνθι {{Refs 6th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.46 (3rd c.BC): _optative_ εἴην, -ης (εἴησθα 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 715)}}, -η, also ἔοις, ἔοι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.284, 142, +others, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.6}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἴοισαν Ἀρχ. Ἐφ. {{Refs|1911.133 (from Gonni)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ εἴτην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 149e, “Sph.” 243e}}; _1st pers._ _plural_ εἶμεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 921 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ εἶτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.195}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ εἶεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.372, etc., εἴησαν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.2, etc.}}; Elean ἔα, ={εἴη}, [{{Refs 6th c.BC+|“SIG” 9 (6th c.BC), and σύν-εαν, ={συνεῖεν}, “GDI” 1149 (6th c.BC):}}]_infinitive_ εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus ἦναι “SIG” 306.9 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}; _Epic dialect_ ἔμμεναι (also _Aeolic dialect_ ἔμμεν᾽ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 34), ἔμμεν (also 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.42, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 623 (Lyric poetry)), ἔμεναι, ἔμεν, also ἔμειν “SIG” 1166 (from Dodona)}}; _Doric dialect_ εἶμεν Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77, 79, “IG” 7.1.7 (from Megara), ἦμεν “Test.Epict.” 5.16, “Tab.Heracl.” 1.75, Cretan dialect ἦμεν or ἤμην “Leg.Gort.” 1.15, +others, “GDI” 4998i 2, +others, Megarian dialect εἴμεναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 775, εἴμειν “IG” 12(1).155.100 (from Rhodes), 14.952 (from Agrigentum); εἶν [prev. work] 12(9).211.10 (from Eretria), “SIG” 135.4 (from Olynthus), etc.:}}_participle_ ὤν, _Epic dialect_ ἐών, ἐοῦσα, ἐόν, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Cypria ἰών “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.23 H.}}; _Boeotian dialect_ _feminine_ ἰῶσα {{Refs|“IG” 7.3172.116 (Orchom.)}}, _Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ _feminine_ ἔσσα {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 75.4, “IG” 4.952.2 (Epid.), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 28.16, ἐοῖσα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.265, ἔασσα “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 9, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, εὖσα Erinna Lyrica 5.5}} (also _Ionic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 5.16, εὔντων 2.85), ἐᾶσα 5th-4th c.BC: Timaeus Locrus Philosophus 96d, “IG” 5(1).1470.8 (from Messene), ἴαττα “Leg.Gort.” 8.47; _accusative_ _sing._ εὖντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.3}}; _nominative_ _singular_ εἴς in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1756.13, _plural_ ἔντες “Tab.Heracl.” 1.117; _dative_ _plural_ ἔντασσι [prev. work]104; _genitive_ _plural_ παρ-έντων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 64}}: _imperfect_ ἦν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.77, etc., _Epic dialect_ ἔον}} (also _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 127, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in 1787 “fragment” 3 ii 21), in _Attic dialect_ ἦ}} (uncertain in _Aeolic dialect_, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 14.9), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 77, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 61b, etc., but usually altered to ἦν in codices (and ἦν}} is required by metre in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 280), _contraction_ from _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ ἦα (8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.808, +others, “IG” 12(8).449.2 (from Thasos)}}, whence Hom.and later _Ionic dialect_ ἔᾱ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.321, +others, ἔας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, ἔατε [prev. author] 4.119)}}; _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦεν, always with ν in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; ἔην as _1st pers._ _singular_, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.762 (assuming variant, +others ἔον), frequently as _3rd.pers._ _sing._ (generally before a consonant, so that ἔεν is possible), sometimes also ἤην}}; _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσθα, later ἦς (which is variant in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 1.26), sometimes in LXX (LXX.Judg.11.35, LXX.Ruth.3.2, +others), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Axiochus” 365e, Erinna Lyrica 4.4, NT.Matt.25.21, +others, ἦσθας 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 156, _Epic dialect_ ἔησθα}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦν, _Epic dialect_ ἔην, ἤην, ἦεν (see. above), _Doric dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ ἦς {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 30.1, 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 102, 5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 59, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 2.90, “SIG” 241.145 (Delph.)}}; _3rd.pers._ _dual_ ἤστην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.10, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 387, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 982, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a, +others}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ἦμες {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lycurgus” 21}}; _2nd pers._ _plural_ ἦτε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 276c, ἦστε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 821, “Ec.” 1086}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἦσαν, _Ionic dialect_ and poetical ἔσαν (in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321, 825, ἦν is not _plural_ for ἦσαν}}, but is rather a peculiarity of syntax, see below see, but is _3rd.pers._ _plural_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, +others, “SIG” 560.15 (Epidamnus, 3rd c.BC))}}; _Aeolic dialect_ ἔον {{NT+5th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 644.12; later ἤμην “PSI” 4.362.21 (3rd c.BC), “SIG” 527.46 (Crete, 3rd c.BC), “IGRom.” 4.1740 (from Cyme), always in LXX as LXX.Bar.1.19, cf. NT.Matt.23.30, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.174a, etc., and sometimes in codices of earlier writers, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.34, “Tragica Adespota” 124 (cited from 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 931 by 4th-5th c.AD: Choeroboscus Grammaticus and from [prev. author] “Tr.” 474 by Aps.), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.9, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 26}}, _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἦσο {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 379 (from Aezani)}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ἦστο {{Refs|“Supp.epigram” 1.455.7 (from Phrygia)}}, _1st pers._ _plural_ ἤμεθα {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PPetr.” 2p.11 (3rd c.BC), LXX.Bar.1.19, LXX.1Ki.25.16, NT.Eph.2.3}}; _subjunctive_ ὦμαι {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PBaden” 48.12 (2nd c.BC), ἦται “GDI” , ἦνται probably in “IG” 5(1).1390.83 (from Andania)}}; _Ionic dialect_ and _Epic dialect_ also ἔσκον, used by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 656 (Lyric poetry):}}_future_ ἔσομαι, ἔσται, _Epic dialect_ and _Aeolic dialect_ also ἔσσομαι, ἔσεται, ἔσσετα; _Aeolic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ ἔσσῃ probably in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 67, 87; _Doric dialect_ 2 and _3rd.pers._ _sing._ ἐσσῇ, ἐσσεῖται, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.393, 13.317, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 10.5, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐσσοῦνται Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.77 codices (but ἔσσονται “Tab.Heracl.” 1.113)}}, _infinitive_ ἐσσεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophron Comicus 57.}} —All forms of the _present_ _indicative_ are enclitic (except _2nd pers._ _singular_ εἶ and _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔασι); but _3rd.pers._ _singular_ is written ἔστι when it begins a sentence or verse, or when it immediately follows οὐκ, καί, εἰ, ὡς, ἀλλά, or τοῦτ᾽, Hdn.Gr.{{Refs|1.553 (also μή _accusative_ to “Etymologicum Magnum” 301.3)}}; later Grammars wrote ἔστι as _substantive_ Verb, {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 880.22.}}
**A** as the _substantive_ Verb,
**A.I** of persons, **exist**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ οὗτος ἀνήρ, οὐδ᾽ ἔσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.437}}; ἔτ᾽ εἰσί they **are** still **in being**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.433, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 445, etc.; τεθνηῶτος.. μηδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐόντος 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.289}}; οὐκέτ᾽ ἐστί he **is** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 1162}}; οὐδὲ δὴν ἦν he **was** not long-**lived**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.131}}; ὁ οὐκ ὤν, οἱ οὐκ ὄντες, of **those who are** no more, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.45, 44}}; οἱ ὄντες the **living**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.29.2}}; ὁ ὤν the {{LXX+8th c.BC+|“Eternal”, LXX.Exo.3.14, +others, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.289; θεοὶ αἰὲν ἐόντες 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.290}}; ἐσσόμενοι **posterity**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|2.119; κἀγὼ γὰρ ἦ ποτ᾽, ἀλλὰ νῦν οὐκ εἴμ᾽ ἔτι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 284}}; ὡς ἂν εἶεν ἅνθρωποι **might continue in being**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 190c; ζώντων καὶ ὄντων Ἀθηναίων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.72, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 318b25}}; of things, εἰ ἔστι ἀληθέως [ἡ τράπεζα] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.17, etc.}}; of cities, ὄλωλεν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ἐστὶ Τροία {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 1292, compare “Heracl.” 491}}; δοκεῖ μοι Καρχηδόνα μὴ εἶναι **censeo Carthaginem esse delendam**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 27}}; ἂν ᾖ τὸ στράτευμα **be in existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.17}}; of money, **to be in hand**, τῶν ὄντων χρημάτων καὶ τῶν προσιόντων {{Refs|“IG” 12.91.25}}; τὰ ὄντα **property**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 511a, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Antonius” 24, etc.}}; τὸ ἐσόμενον ἐκ.. **future revenue** from.., {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BCH” 46.420 (Olymos, 1st c.BC)}}; of place, τὴν οὖσαν ἐκκλησίαν the **local** church, {{NT|NT.Act.13.1}}; of time, τοῦ ὄντος μηνός in the **current** month, {{Refs|“BGU” 146.4, etc.}}; **in office**, ἱερέων τῶν ὄντων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PPar.” 5.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; αἱ οὖσαι [ἐξουσίαι] the powers **that be, Ep. Rom.**{{Refs|13.1.}}
**A.II** of the **real** world, **be**, opposed to become, γίγνεται πάντα ἃ δή φαμεν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 152d, etc.}}; τὸ ὄν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Being”, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.35, 5th c.BC: Protagoras Philosophus 2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 27d, etc.}}; opposed to τὸ μὴ ὄν, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “fragment” 3 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator, etc.; οὐδὲν γίνεται ἐκ τοῦ μὴ ὄντος 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.5U.; ἐξ οὐκ ὄντων ἐποίησεν αὐτὰ ὁ θεός LXX.2Mac.7.28}}; τὰ ὄντα the **world of things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 7, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 129.5, etc.}}; ὄν_indeclinable_, τῶν ὂν εἰδῶν species **of Being**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.2.10.}}
**A.II.2** of circumstances, events, etc., **to happen**, τά τ᾽ ἐόντα, τά τ᾽ ἐσσόμενα, πρό τ᾽ ἐόντα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.70; ἡ ἐσβολὴ ἔμελλεν ἔσεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, etc.}}; τῆς προδοσίας οὔσης since treachery **was there**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.103}}; ἕως ἂν ὁ πόλεμος ᾖ so long as it **last**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.58; αἱ σπονδαὶ ἐνιαυτὸν ἔσονται [prev. author] 4.118}}; τί ἐστι; what **is** it? what's the matter? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 193}}; τί οὖν ἦν τοῦτ; how **came** it **to pass**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58a: repeated with a _relative_ to avoid a positive assertion, ἔστι δ᾽ ὅπῃ νῦν ἔ.}} things **are** as they **are**, i.e. are ill, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 67.}}
**A.III** **be the fact** or **the case**, διπλασίαν ἂν τὴν δύναμιν εἰκάζεσθαι ἤ ἐστιν twice as large as it **really is**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.10}}; αὐτὸ ὅ ἐστι καλόν beauty **in its essence**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 211c, compare “Phd.” 74b}}; frequently in _participle_, τὸν ἐόντα λόγον λέγειν or φαίνειν the **true** story, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.95, 116}}; τῷ ἐόντι χρήσασθαι tell the **truth**,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 30; τὰ ὄντα ἀπαγγέλλειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.8}}; σκῆψιν οὐκ οὖσαν, λόγον οὐκ ὄντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 584, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1052}}; τῷ ὄντι **in reality, in fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328d, etc.}}; to apply a quotation to a case in point, τῷ ὄντι κλαυσίγελως **real** 'smiles through tears' (with allusion to {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.2.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196d}}; κατὰ τὸ ἐόν according to the **fact, right**ly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.97}}; πᾶν τὸ ἐόν the whole **truth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.11; τοῦ ἐόντος ἀποτεύξεται 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 2.}}
**A.IV** followed by the _relative_, οὐκ ἔστιν ὅς or ὅστις **no one**, οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὃς.. ἀπαλάλκοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.348; οὐκ ἔ. οὐδεὶς ὅς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 903}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅτῳ, ={οὐδενί}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 293 (_anapaest meter_), compare 989: frequently in _plural_, εἰσὶν οἵ}}, = Latin _sunt qui_, used exactly like{ἔνιοι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 7.44, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77d, “Grg.” 503a, etc. (εἰσί τινες οἵ.. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.24)}}; ἐστὶν ἃ χωρία, πολίσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.12, 65; ἐστὶν ἃ εἰπεῖν [prev. author] 2.67; ἦσαν οἵ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.2.14}}; the _singular_ Verb is used even with _masculine_ and _feminine_ _plural_, ἐστὶν οἵ, αἵ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 1, “VC” 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.16}}; more frequently in oblique cases, ποταμῶν ἐστὶ ὧν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.187; ἐστὶν ἀφ᾽ ὧν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.65}}; ἐστὶ παρ᾽ οἷς, ἐστὶν ἐν οἷς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.23, 5.25: in questions ὅστις is used, ἔστιν ἥντινα δόξαν.. ἀπεκρίνατ; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 85b: with _relative_ Particles, ἐστὶν ἔνθα}}, = Latin _est ubi_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅπῃ, ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου, **some**how, **some**where, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 331d, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, etc.}}; in questions expecting a _negative_ answer, ἐ. ὁπόθεν, ὅπω; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 35a, “R.” 493e, etc.; οὐ γάρ ἐσθ᾽ ὅπως 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 61, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.102, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 620}}; οὐκ ἔ. ὅπως οὐ **in any case, necessarily**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 188; οὐκ ἔ. ὡς 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 76e, etc.}}; ἐ. ὅτε, ἔσθ᾽ ὅτε, **some**times, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 180.2, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 56, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, etc.}}
**A.V** ἦν is sometimes used with _plural_ _masculine_ and _feminine_, usually at the beginning of a sentence, **there was**, τῆς δ᾽ ἦν τρεῖς κεφαλαί {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 321; (but in ἦν δ᾽ ἐρῳδιοί τε πολλοί 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 46, compare 59, +others, it may be taken as _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_)}}; ἦν δ᾽ ἀμφίπλεκτοι κλίμακες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 520 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἦν ἄρα κἀκεῖνοι ταλακάρδιοι epigram cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.184; less frequently ἔστι, ἔστι δὲ μεταξὺ.. ἑπτὰ στάδιοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.26, compare 7.34; ἔστι.. ἄρχοντές τε καὶ δῆμος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 463a}}; before dual Nouns, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 58, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 500d.}}
**A.VI** ἔστι _impersonal_, with _infinitive_, **it is possible**, ἔστι γὰρ ἀμφοτέροισιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.246; ἔστι μὲν εὕδειν, ἔστι δὲ τερπομένοισιν ἀκούειν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.392}}; εἴ τί πού ἐστι (i.e. πιθέσθαι){{Refs 5th c.BC+|4.193; τοιάδε.. ἐστὶν ἀκοῦσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 1055 (_anapaest meter_); ἔστι τεκμήρια ὁρᾶν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1163, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.105, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 53.6, etc.}}; so in _imperative_, _optative_, and _subjunctive_, ἔστω ἀποφέρεσθαι τῷ βουλομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.10.7; μυρία ἂν εἴη λέγειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 271e; ὅπως ἂν ᾖ δρᾶν “IG” 2.1054.91: more frequently in _negative_ clauses, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.267, etc.}}; followed by ὥστε with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 656}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, ἁδόντα δ᾽ εἴη με τοῖς ἀγαθοῖς ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.96; ἔστιν ἐκπεσεῖν ἀρχῆς Δία 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 757}}: sometimes not _impersonal_ in this sense, θάλασσα δ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ ἦν ἰδεῖν {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pers.” 419.}}
**A.VI.b** ἔστω in argument, **let it be granted**, ἔστω τοῦτο ἀληθὲς εἶναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “de Compositione Verborum” 25; ἔστω σοι τοῦθ᾽ οὕτως 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.987b; ἔστω εἶναί τινα τοιοῦτον 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator Chr.74.24.}}
**B** most frequently, **to be**, the Copula connecting the predicate with the Subject, both being in the same case: hence, **signify, import**, τὸ γὰρ εἴρειν λέγειν ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 398d}}; especially in the phrase τοῦτ᾽ ἔστι, **hoc est**; Σκαιόλαν, ὅπερ ἐστὶ Λαϊόν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Publicola” 17: with numerals, τὰ δὶς πέντε δέκα ἐστίν}} twice five **are** ten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.7}}; εἶναί τις or τι, **to be** somebody, something, **be** of some consequence, see at {τι; οὐδὲν εἶναι} {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 562d, etc.}}
**B.2** _periphrastic_ with the Participle to represent the finite Verb: with _perfect_ _participle_ once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, τετληότες εἰμέν, for τετλήκαμεν, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.873}}; so in Trag. and _Attic dialect_, ἦν τεθνηκώς, for ἐτεθνήκει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 869}}; ἔσται δεδορκώς{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1179; εἰμὶ γεγώς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1299; πεφυκός ἐστι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1473; δεδρακότες εἰσίν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68; κατακεκονότες ἔσεσθε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.36}}: with _aorist_ _participle_, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, βλήμενος ἦν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.211}}; so προδείσας εἰμί, οὐ σιωπήσας ἔσε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 90, 1146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 460}}: with _present_ _participle_, ἦν προκείμενον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 371; φεύγων Ὀρέστης ἐστίν [prev. author] “Ch.” 136; εἴην οὐκ ἂν εὖ φρονῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1330}}; τί δ᾽ ἐστί.. φέρο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 991, compare 274, 708; λέγων ἐστίν τις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1179; ἦν τίς σ᾽ ὑβρίζων [prev. author] “HF” 313; πόρρω ἤδη εἶ πορευόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 204b; βαδίζων εἰμί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 36; frequently in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, ἦσαν ἱέντες 1.57, +others; even εἰσὶ διάφοροι ἐόντες 3.49 (assuming variant):}} — if the _Article_ is joined with the Part., the noun is made emphatic, Κᾶρές εἰσι οἱ καταδέξαντες the persons who showed her **were** Carians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.171; αὐτὸς ἦν ὁ μαρτυρῶν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 798; δόλος ἦν ὁ φράσας 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 197 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**C** εἶναι is frequently modified in sense by the addition of _adverbs_, or the cases of Nouns without or with Preps.:
**C.I** εἶναι with _adverbs_, where the _adverb_ often merely represents a Noun and stands as the predicate, ἅλις δέ οἱ ἦσαν ἄρουραι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.122, etc.}}; ἀκέων, ἀκήν εἶναι, **to be** silent,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4.22, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.82; σῖγα πᾶς ἔστω λεώς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 532; διαγνῶναι χαλεπῶς ἦν ἄνδρα ἕκαστον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.424}}; ἀσφαλέως ἡ κομιδὴ ἔσται **will go on** safely, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.134}}; ἐγγύς, πόρρω εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 356e: frequently _impersonal_ with words implying good or ill fortune, Κουρήτεσσι κακῶς ἦν}} **it fared** ill with them, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.551; εὖ γὰρ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 89, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1188, etc.; ἡδέως ἂν αὐτοῖς εἴη 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 59.30.}}
**C.II** with _genitive_, to express descent or extraction, πατρὸς δ᾽ εἴμ᾽ ἀγαθοῖο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.109; αἵματός εἰς ἀγαθοῖο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.71, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.71, etc.; πόλεως μεγίστης εἶ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.3.19.}}
**C.II.b** to express the material of which a thing is made, ἡ κρηπίς ἐστι λίθων μεγάλων **consists of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.93; τῆς πόλιος ἐούσης δύο φαρσέων [prev. work] 186}}; τοιούτων ἔργων ἐστὶ ἡ τυραννίς **is made up of**.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92}}.ή, etc.
**C.II.c** to express the class to which a person or thing belongs, εἶ γὰρ τῶν φίλων **you are one of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 345; ἐτύγχανε βουλῆς ὤν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70; ὅσοι ἦσαν τῶν προτέρων στρατιωτῶν [prev. author] 7.44; Κριτίας τῶν τριάκοντα ὤν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.31}}; ἔστι τῶν αἰσχρῶν **it is in the class of** disgraceful things, i. e. **it is** disgraceful, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.2.}}
**C.II.d** to express that a thing belongs to another, Τροίαν Ἀχαιῶν οὖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 269; τὸ πεδίον ἦν μέν κοτε Χορασμίων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.117, etc.}}: hence, **to be of the party of**, ἦσαν.. τινὲς μὲν φιλίππου, τινὲς δὲ τοῦ βελτίστου {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.56, compare 37.53}}; **to be de pendent upon**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 737, etc.}}; **to be at the mercy of**, ἔστι τοῦ λέγοντος, ἢν φόβους λέγῃ {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 917.}}
**C.II.e** to express one's duty, business, custom, nature, and the like, οὔτοι γυναικός ἐστι **'tis** not a woman's **part**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 940; τὸ ἐπιτιμᾶν παντὸς εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.16}}; τὸ δὲ ναυτικὸν τέχνης ἐστίν **is matter of** art, **requires** art, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.142, compare 83.}}
**C.II.f** in LXX, **to be occupied about**, ἦσαν τοῦ θύειν {{LXX|LXX.2Ch.30.17}}; ἔσεσθαι, with _genitive_, **to be about to**, ἐσόμεθα τοῦ σῶσαί σε {{LXX|LXX.2Ki.10.11.}}
**C.III** with the _dative_, ἐστί μοι I **have**, frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}
**C.III.2** with two datives., σφίσι τε καὶ Ἀθηναίοισι εἶναι οὐδὲν πρῆγμα that they and the Athenians **have** nothing **to do** one with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.84; μηδὲν εἶναι σοὶ καὶ φιλίππῳ πρᾶγμα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.283}}; more shortly, σοί τε καὶ τούτοισι πρήγμασι τί ἐστ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33}}; τί τῷ νόμῳ καὶ τῇ βασάν; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 29.36}}; τί ἐμοὶ καὶ σο; Latin _quid tecum est mihi?_ {{NT|NT.Mark.5.7, etc.}}; also ἐμοὶ οὐδὲν πρὸς τοὺς τοιούτους (i.e. ἐστίν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.12; ἐν οἷς πρὸς τοὺς ἐναντίους ἐστὶ τῷ δήμῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.278}}; ἔσται αὐτῳ πρὸς τὸν θεόν, in tomb inscriptions, {{Refs|“JHS” 18.113, etc.}}
**C.III.3** with ἄσμενος, βουλόμενος, etc., added, ἐμοὶ δέ κεν ἀσμένῳ εἴη **'twould be** to my delight, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.108; οὐκ ἂν σφίσι βουλομένοις εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.35; προσδεχομένῳ [prev. author] 6.46; θέλοντι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1356 (Lyric poetry); ἡδομένοις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187c.}}
**C.IV** with Preps., εἶναι ἀπό τινος, ={εἶναί τινος}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|(above 11.a), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.6.9; εἰσὶν ἀπ᾽ ἐναντίων αὗται πραγμάτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 12d}}; but εἶναι ἀπ᾽ οἴκου **to be** away from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99.}}
**C.IV.2** εἶναι ἔκ τινος **to be sprung** from, εἴμ᾽ ἐκ Παιονίης, Μυρμιδόνων ἔξ εἰμι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.154, 24.397, etc.}}; ἔστιν ἐξ ἀνάγκης **it is** of necessity, i. e. necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 256d.}}
**C.IV.3** εἶναι ἐν.. **to be** in a certain state, ἐν εὐπαθείῃσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.22}}; ἐν ἀθυμία, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.46, etc.; ἐν ταραχαῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.218}}; εἶναι ἐν ἀξιώματι **to be** in esteem, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.130}}; οἱ ἐν τέλεϊ ἐόντες those in office, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.18, etc.}}; but εἶναι ἐν τέχνῃ, ἐν φιλοσοφία **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 562, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 59a.}}
**C.IV.3.b** ἐν σοί ἐστι **it depends** on thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 963; ἐν σοὶ γάρ ἐσμεν [prev. author] “OT” 314; so also ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.2, etc.}}
**C.IV.4** εἶναι διά.., much like{εἶναι ἐν}.., εἶναι διὰ φόβου, ={φοβεῖσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.34}}; εἶναι δι᾽ ὄχλου, ={ὀχληρὸν εἶναι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.73; εἶναι διὰ μόχθων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.25}}; εἶναι δι᾽ αἰτίας, ={αἰτιᾶσθαι}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.70}}; Geometry texts, **pass** through, διὰ τᾶς ἑτέρας διαμέτρου ἐόντος τοῦ ἐπιπέδου {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ κωνοειδέων καὶ σφαιροειδέων” 20.}}
**C.IV.5** εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑαυτῆς **to be** by oneself, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.23}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ ὀνόματος **to bear** a name, {{Refs|[prev. author] 39.21}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τοῖς πράγμασιν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.12}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινα **to be** against him, {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.33}}; εἶναι ἐφ᾽ ἑξήκοντα στάδια **to reach** sixty stadia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.11}}; εἶναι ἐπὶ τὰς ἁφάς **pass** through the points of contact, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollonius Biographus Perg. “Con.” 4.1}}; εἶναι ἐπί τινι, see above 3 b.
**C.IV.6** εἶναι πρός τινος **to be** in one's favour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10, 29, etc.}}; **to suit**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.11, etc.}}; εἶναι πρός τινι **engaged** in, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.31}}; πρὸς τοῖς ἰδίοις **mind** one's own affairs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309a6, “Ath.” 16.3; εἶναι πρὸς τὸ κωλύειν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.26.3}}; πρὸς τὸ πονεῖν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Teles p.46 H.; εἶναι περί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.7, etc.}}
**C.IV.7** εἶναι παρά τινι or τινα, = παρειναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 6.2.15, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.140.ά}} (assuming variant).
**C.IV.8** εἶναι ὑπό τινα or τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.17 (assuming variant), 6.2.4.}}
**C.IV.9** περὶ τούτων ἐστίν that **is** the question, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 30.}}
**C.IV.10** εἶναι ἀπό.., in Geometry texts, **to be constructed** upon, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Archimedes Geometra “περὶ σφαίρας καὶ κυλίνδρου” 2.9, “Con.Sph.” 7.}}
**D** ἐστί is very frequently omitted, mostly in the _present_ _indicative_ before certain predicates, as ἀνάγκη, ἄξιον, δυνατόν, εἰκός, ἕτοιμον, οἷόν τε, ῥᾴδιον, χρεών, etc., and after the _neuter_ of Verbals in -τέος, and such forms as θαυμαστὸν ὅσον: less frequently with other persons and moods, εἰμί omitted, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 92, “Aj.” 813}}; εἶ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.206}}; ἐσμέν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 634}}; ἐστέ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.463}}; εἰσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 499 (Lyric poetry), “IG” 2.778 B}}; _subjunctive_ ᾖ, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 659, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.32}}; _optative_ εἴη, {{Refs|“IG” 22.1183.12}}; _imperfect_ ἦν, {{Refs|[prev. work]2.778 B}}; _future_ ἔσονται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.394.}}
**E** the Inf. frequently seems redundant,
**E.1** in phrases implying power or will to do a thing, ἑκὼν εἶναι (see. ἑκών) κατὰ δύναμιν εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.32; εἰς δύναμιν εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 300c}}; τὸ ἐπ᾽ ἐκείνοις εἶναι, **quantum in illis esset**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.48, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.58; τὸ ἐπὶ σφᾶς εἶναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.28; τὸ κατὰ τοῦτον εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.9; κατὰ τοῦτο εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 317a}}; τὸ τήμερον, τὸ νῦν εἶναι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cra.” 396e, “La.” 201c, Theopomp. Comedy texts 98, Decrees cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2, etc.}}
**E.2** after Verbs of naming or choosing, σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσι τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e; σύμμαχόν μιν εἵλοντο εἶναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.134}}; of giving, δῶκε ξεινήϊον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.20.}}
**F** _imperfect_ ἦνissts. used where other languages take the _present_,
**F.1** after ἄρα, to express a fact which **is and has always been** the same, δέρμα δὲ ἀνθρώπου.. ἦν ἄρα σχεδὸν δερμάτων πάντων λαμπρότατον human skin then **it appears is**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.64; Κύπρις οὐκ ἄρ᾽ ἦν θεός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 359}}; ὡς ἄρ᾽ ἦσθ᾽ ἐμὸς πατὴρ ὀρθῶς{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1169; ἦ πολύμοχθον ἄρ᾽ ἦν γένος.. ἁμερίων [prev. author] “IA” 1330; ἦ στωμύλος ἦσθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.79}}; so also when there is reference to a past thought, τουτὶ τί ἦ; what **is** this? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 157, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 387c: so in the Aristotelian formula τὸ τί ἦν εἶναι (“APo.” 82b38, +others)}}, used to express **the essential nature** of a thing, where τί ἦν (for ἐστί) takes the place of the _dative_ in such phrases as τὸ ἀγαθῷ εἶναι, τὸ μεγέθει εἶναι, {{Refs|“APr.” 67b12, “de An.” 429b10.}}
**G** ἐγώ εἰμι, in {{LXX|LXX, pleonastic for ἐγώ, ἐγώ εἰμι οὐχ ἥμαρτον LXX.Judg.11.27, compare 6.18; also ἔσται πᾶς ἀποκτενεῖ με LXX.Gen.4.14.}}

3605:
** ὄζω**,
_Doric dialect_ **ὄσδω** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.149 (compare also III below)}}: _imperfect_ ὦζε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 2 (conjecture Pors. for ὦ Ζεῦ)}}: _future_ ὀζήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1059}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὀζέσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 25, “Geoponica” 12.29.5, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1523.39, “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.396}}: _aorist_ ὤζησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 635}}; _Ionic dialect_ ὤζεσα {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἐπικυήσιος” 25, LXX.Exo.8.14(10)}}: _perfect_ ὤζηκα {{Refs 9th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}}; but _perfect_ with _present_ sense ὄδωδα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 10 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.30 (Antip. Sid.), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.916d, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων ὀξέων παθῶν” 1.9}}: _pluperfect_ as _imperfect_ ὠδώδειν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 20}}; _Epic dialect_ ὀδώδειν (see. below):—**smell,** whether **smell sweet** or **stink,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _pluperfect_ with sense of _imperfect_, ὀδμὴ κέδρου.. ἀνὰ νῆσον ὀδώδει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.60; ὀδμὴ δ᾽ ἡδεῖα ἀπὸ κρητῆρος ὀδώδει, of wine, 9.210}}: later with _genitive_ _of things_, frequently with _neuter_ _adjective_ or _adverb_ added, **smell of** a thing, τόδ᾽ ὄζει θυμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1310; ὄζων τρυγός 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 50; βύρσης κάκιστον ὄζων [prev. author] “Eq.” 892, compare “V.” 38; also ὠδώδει ὑπὸ μύρων ὁ οἶκος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 20}}: _metaphorically_, **smell** or **savour** of a thing, Κρονίων ὄζων **smelling of** musty antiquity, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 398, compare 1007, “Ach.” 192, “Lys.” 616; καλοκἀγαθίας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.4}}; that from which the smell comes is also in _genitive_, ὄζων κακὸν τῶν μασχαλῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 852; τοῦ στόματος 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 67: so with double _genitive_, τῆς κεφαλῆς ὄζω μύρου 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 524; see below 11.}}
**II** frequently _impersonal_, ὄζει ἀπ᾽ αὐτῆς ὡσεὶ ἴων **there is a smell** from it as of violets, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.23}}; ὄζει ἡδὺ τῆς χρόας **there is a** sweet **smell** from the skin, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1020; τῆς γῆς ὡς γλυκὺ ὄζει 4th c.BC: Cratinus Junior Comicus 1}}; ὄζειν ἐδόκει τοῦ ἄρτου καὶ τῆς μάζης κάκιστον there seemed **to be** a most foul **smell** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.1}}; οὐκ ὄζει αὐτῶν (i.e. τῶν λαγῶν) **no scent** of the hares **remains,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.1, compare 7: with double _genitive_, ἱματίων ὀζήσει δεξιότητος}} **there will be an odour** of cleverness from your clothes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1059, compare “Pax” 529; ἀπὸ στόματος.. ὄζει ἴων, ὄζει δὲ ῥόδων, ὄζει δ᾽ ὑακίνθου 5th c.BC: Hermippus Comicus 82.8; ὄζει ἐκ τοῦ στόματος μελικήρας 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 25.}}
**III** _middle_, κακὸν ὀζόμενος, for ὄζων, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τόπων τῶν κατὰ ἄνθρωπον” 12; οἶνος.. ἄνθεος ὀσδόμενος 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.6; δριμὺ ὀσδομένου τοῦ σώματος “PSI” 4.297.3 (ca. 5th c.AD)}}. (Cf. Latin _odor,_ Lithuanian _uodžiu_ 'I smell'.)

3606:
** ὅθεν**,
_relative_ _adverb_ **whence,** ὑπὸ πλατανίστῳ, ὅ. ῥέεν ἀγλαὸν ὕδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.307}}; ἐξ Ἐνετῶν, ὅ. ἡμιόνων γένος{{Refs|[prev. work] 852; γένος δέ μοι ἔνθεν, ὅ. σοί 4.58, etc.; πόλεως ὅθεν εἶ “IG” 12(5).310.2 (from Paros)}}; also, **from whom** or **which,** ὅθεν περ Ὁμηρίδαι ἄρχονται, Διὸς ἐκ προοιμίου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 2.1; τὴν τεκοῦσαν.., ὅθεν περ αὐτὸς ἐσπάρη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1498; Φοῖνιξ, ὅθεν περ τοὔνομ᾽ ἡ χώρα φέρει 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 819.8: followed by Particles, ὅθεν περ (see. above)}}; ὅθεν δή {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 15 (_anapaest meter_); ὅθεν τε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.358.}}
**b** in _Attic dialect_ Prose, ὅθεν δή **from whatever source, in what manner soever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 267d; so ὁθενδήποτε Dosith. p.410 K.}}; also ἄλλοθεν ὁθενοῦν **from any** other **place whatsoever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 738c.}}
**2** for **where** or **whither,** by attraction, when the antecedent clause contains a notion of place whence, ἐκ δὲ γῆς, ὅ. προὔκειτ᾽ **from** the ground **where** it lay, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 701}}; ὅ... ἀπέλιπες, ἀποκρίνου answer [**from** the point] **where** you left off, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 497c; διεκομίζοντο.. ὅ. ὑπεξέθεντο παῖδας καὶ γυναῖκας 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.89}}; ὅθεν, ={ἐκεῖσε ὅθεν}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.17, 7.6.12, etc.}}; ὅθεν περ, ={ἐκεῖθεν ὅθεν περ}, {{Refs|“IG” 12.78.9,88.23.}}
**II** **whence, for which reason,** σφυρῶν.. κέντρα διαπείρας μέσον, ὅ. νιν Ἑλλὰς ὠνόμαζεν Οἰδίπουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 27, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 54, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 3.2, “IG” 22.1011.42, +others; ὅ... ἱδρύσαθ᾽ ἱερόν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 267.4}}; **for what reason,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 319b; ὅ. ἠνάγκασμαι κατηγορεῖν αὐτῶν, περὶ τούτων πρῶτον εἰπεῖν βούλομαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.1.}}

3607:
** ὀθόν-η**, ἡ,
**fine linen,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always _plural_, **fine linen cloths,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.107}}; of a woman's dress, ἀργεννῇσι καλυψαμένη ὀθόνῃσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.141, compare 18.595; ὀθόναις ἐσταλμένος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 3.2}}: _singular_, **a cloth,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Acts.10.11, 11.5, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.134, 6.795.}}
**2** later, **sails,** πνεύσεται εἰς ὀθόνας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.53.8 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), compare 10.5 (1st c.BC: Thyillus Epigrammaticus)}}: _singular_, **sail-cloth, sail,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “JTr.” 46, “VH” 2.37.}}
**3** in _plural_, of the **membranes** that enclose the pupil of the eye, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 84.8.}}

3608:
** ὀθόν-ιον**, τό,
_diminutive_ of ὀθόνη, **linen cloth,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 7, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 104, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 7.3.5, “PSI” 6.599 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.23.3, NT.John.19.40, etc.}}: _plural_, **linen cloths,** βύσσινα ὀ. {{LXX+2nd c.BC+|“OGI” 90.18 (Rosetta, 2nd c.BC),cf. LXX.Judg.14.13, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 34, etc.}}; **towels,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 6.203b}}; **linen bandages** or **lint,** for wounds, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 8, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1176.}}
**2** **sail-cloth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 47.20, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.89.2; so perhaps in “PPetr.” 1p.79 (3rd c.BC, _plural_).}}

3609:
** οἰκεῖος**, α, ον,
also ος, ον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 634}}; _Ionic dialect_ **οἰκήϊος**, η, ον: **in** or **of the house,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, δούρατ᾽ ἀμάξης οἰκήϊα θέσθαι “Op.” 457; λέβης 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 1; κῆρυξ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 757}}; **of** or **for household affairs, domestic** (for οἰκηΐη, see at {οἰκία} II), τὰ οἰ. **household affairs, property,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.37, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 661; τὰ οἰ. ἀγαθά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 9.18}}; τὰ οἰ. τὰ αὑτοῦ his **household goods,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.41}}; opposed to πολιτικά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40}}; opposed to τὰ τῆς πόλεως, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23b.}}
**2** Astrology texts, οἰ. ζῴδια **domiciliary** signs, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 37.21, +others}}
**II** of persons, **of the same household, family,** or **kin, related,** ὥς οἱ ἐόντες οἰκήϊοι as being **akin** to him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.65}}; οἰκεῖον οὕτως οὐδὲν.. ὡς ἀνήρ τε καὶ γυνή so **closely akin,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 647}}; ἀνὴρ οἰ. **kins**man, **relative, near friend,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108}}; οἱ οἰ. **kinsmen,** opposed to οἱ ἀλλότριοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.15, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.51}}; opposed to ὀθνεῖοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 316c}}; οἱ ἑωυτοῦ οἰκηϊότατοι his own **nearest kinsmen,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.65, compare 5.5, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.288}}; of the tie itself, κατὰ τὸ οἰ. Ἀτρεῖ because of his **relationship** to Atreus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.9.}}
**II.2** **friendly,** εἴχομέν ποτε.. τὸν τόπον τοῦτον οἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.4; οὓς ἂν ἡγήσαιτο -οτάτους τε καὶ ἑταιροτάτους 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 89e.}}
**III** of things. **belonging to one's house** or **family, one's own** (defined as ὅταν ἐφ᾽ αὑτῷ ᾖ ἀπαλλοτριῶσαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1361a21), οἰ. ἄρουραι 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.19; σταθμοῖς ἐν οἰκείοισι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 398}}; γῆ, χθών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 859, “Ant.” 1203}}; οἰκεῖον, ἢ 'ξ ἄλλου τινό; **born in the house,** or.. ? {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1162}}; αἱ οἰ. πόλεις **their own** cities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.5.2}}; ἡ οἰ. (i.e. γῆ), _Ionic dialect_ ἡ οἰκηΐη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.64}}; [ἀναθήματα] οἰκήϊα **his own property,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 92}}; πόλεμοι οἰ. wars **in one's own country,** of the Helot war in Laconia, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.118, compare 4.64; σῖτος οἰ. καὶ οὐκ ἐπακτός}}**homegrown,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.20.}}
**III.2** ={ἴδιος}, **one's own, personal, private,** οἰκείων κερδέων εἵνεκα {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 46; ἐὼν ἐν κακῷ οἰκηΐῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.45, compare 153, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.13; αἱ χεῖρες -ότεραι τοῦ σιδήρου [prev. author] 4.3.3}}; μηδὲν -οτέρᾳ τῇ ἀπολαύσει with enjoyment not **more our own,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.38, compare 7.70; ἀλλοτρίας γῆς πέρι οἰ. κίνδυνον ἔχειν [prev. author] 3.13}}; οἰ. ξύνεσις **mother** wit, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.138}}; πρὸς οἰκείας χερός by **his own** hand, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1176, etc.; for 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1220, see at {βορά}.}}]
**III.2.b** in Stoic Philos., **endeared by nature** to all animals, including man, τὸ πρῶτον οἰ. **what is earliest endeared,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 3.43, 4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor p.7A.}}
**IV** **proper to** a thing, **fitting, suitable,** οὔτε.. καλὸν οὐδὲν [οὐδ᾽] οἰκήϊον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.59.}}
**IV.2** with _dative_ _of things_, **belonging to, conformable to the nature of** a thing, προοίμιον οἰ. ἑκάστῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 772e, compare “R.” 468d, +others, and frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, as “EN” 1098a29: also with _genitive_, τὰ αὐτῶν οἰ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 96d; οἰ. τῆς διαλεκτικῆς 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 101b2, compare “EN” 1096b31, “Rh.” 1360a22; οἰ. πρός τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.105.1.}}
**IV.2.b** of persons, with _genitive_, **a student of..,** σοφίας {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.5}}; **addicted to,** καινοτομίας {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de vita Pythagorica” 30.176.}}
**IV.3** **proper, fit,** οἰ. κατάγελως **fit** subject for ridicule, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 160}}; οἰ. ὄνομα a word **in its proper, literal sense,** opposed to metaphor, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b35.}}
**B** _adverb_ οἰκείως has the same senses as the _adjective_, οἰ φέρε bear it **like your own affair,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 197}}; διαλέγεσθαι οἰ. τινί converse **familiarly** with him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.57; οἰ. χρῆσθαί τινι}}**to be on familiar terms,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.16; οἰ. διακεῖσθαί τινι [prev. author] “An.” 7.5.16; πρός τι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 13.1.2; οἰ. δέχεσθαί τινας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.215; οἰ. ἔχειν τινί [prev. author] 4.4, etc.}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 1.49}}; **-οτέρως** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 7a16}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.106.4.}}
**B.II** **properly, naturally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1118, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 2.17}}; opposed to ἀλλοτρίως, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.14U.}}
**B.II.2** **affectionately, dutifully,** ἔθαψε, περιέστειλεν οἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 325.12, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60.}}
**B.II.3** **literally, actually,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “φιλόσοφος ἱστορία - Doxographi Graeci” 39 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator}}
**B.II.4** Astrology texts, οἰ. σχηματίζεσθαι, of a planet, **to be in its domicile,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 58.27, +others}}

3610:
** οἰκέτ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
(οἶκος) **household slave,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 737, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.137, 7.170, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.30, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.4; δημόσιος οἰ. τῆς πόλεως 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.54.}}
**2** οἱ οἰκέται also, ={οἰκετεία}, **household,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 732 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.4, 106, 142, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 908, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.2.2: hence opposed to δοῦλοι, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 763a, 777a, 853e; διαφέρειν φησὶ Χρύσιππος δοῦλον οἰκέτου, διὰ τὸ τοὺς ἀπελευθέρους μὲν δούλους ἔτι εἶναι, οἰκέτας δὲ τοὺς μὴ τῆς κτήσεως ἀφειμένους “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.86: but frequently synonymous with δοῦλος, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1252b12, +others, “PLille” 29.2 (3rd c.BC), “IG” 5(1).1390.77 (Andania, 1st c.BC)}}; δοῦλος μεῖζον οἰκέτου φρονῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 796.}}
**II** as epithet of Apollo, ἱερέως.. Καρνείου Βοικέτα [Β = ϝ] {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).497, compare 589,608 (from Sparta). (Cf. οἰκότης.)}}

3611:
** οἰκέω**,
_Epic dialect_ **οἰκείω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 330}}, _Locrian dialect_ **ϝοικέω** {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).334.29}}; _Aeolic dialect_ _present_ _participle_ οἴκεις {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 69}}; argument _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _present_ _optative_ οἰκείη {{Refs|“BCH” 33.452}}: _imperfect_ ᾤκεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.218, +others, _Attic dialect_ ᾤκουν}} _Ionic dialect_ οἴκεον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.57}}: _future_ οἰκήσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1508 (Lyric poetry)}}: _aorist_ ᾤκησα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1}}: _perfect_ ᾤκηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1101:}} — _passive_ and _middle_, _future_ οἰκήσομαι in _middle_ sense,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|Men.Rh. p.363 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}; but in _passive_, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 11}}: _aorist_ ᾠκήθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.668, etc.; ᾠκησάμην 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 1.103 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus:}}_perfect_ ᾤκημαι as _middle_ and _passive_, _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ οἰκέαται, see {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2}}, 3: (οἶκος):
**A** _transitive_, **inhabit,** ὑπωρείας ᾤκεον πολυπίδακος Ἴδης {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.218 (elsewhere in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus always _intransitive_, see infr. B)}}; οἰ. τοῦτον τὸν χῶρον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.1, compare 175, etc.; οἰ. δόμους 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 961; ἄντρον [prev. author] “Eu.” 194: _metaphorically_, οἰ. αἰῶνα καὶ μοῖραν}} **have, enjoy,** E. [prev. cited]:—_passive_, **to be inhabited,** οἰκέοιτο πόλις Πριάμοιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.18; ἐς γῆν.. οἰκουμένην 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 221}}; διὰ τῆς οἰκεομένης through **the inhabited country,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.32; ὁδοιπόρεον ἐς τὴν οἰκεομένην [prev. author] 4.110}}; τὰς ἄλλας πόλιας οἰκεομένας μηδὲν ἧσσον although **inhabited** no less than before, {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.170: for ἡ οἰκουμένη, see entry.}}
**2** **colonize, settle in,** τὰς πλείστας τῶν νήσων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8, compare 2.27}}, etc.; πόλιν probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 360.11}}; νῆσον οἰκῆσαι, i.e. to be deported, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“POxy.” 1101.24 (4th c.AD)}}: in _perfect_ _passive_, **to be settled in, occupy,** τοῖσι τὰς νήσους οἰκημένοισι Ἴωσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27; αἱ δύο [πόλιες] νήσους οἰκέαται [prev. work] 142 (compare below 3).}}
**3** in _passive_, **to be settled,** of men or tribes to whom new abodes are assigned, τριχθὰ ᾤκηθεν καταφυλαδόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.668: _Ionic dialect_}}_perfect_ _passive_ οἴκημαι, as _present_, οἱ ἐν τῇ ἠπείρῳ οἰκημένοι Ἕλληνες **those who have been settled,** i.e. **those who dwell..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.27; οἱ ἐντὸς Ἅλυος ποταμοῦ οἰ. [prev. work] 28, compare 8.115}}; also of cities, **to be situated, lie,** παρ᾽ ὃν [ποταμὸν] Νίνος πόλις οἴκητο {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.193.}}
**II** **manage, direct** a household or a state, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1535 (uncertain meaning), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 386, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.37; οἴκει τὴν πόλιν ὁμοίως ὥσπερ τὸν πατρῷον οἶκον 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.19: _metaphorically_, μὴ τὸν ἐμὸν οἴκει νοῦν}} don't **manage..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 144:}} — _passive_, εὖ οἰκούμεναι πόλεις well **governed,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Hipp.” 486}}; μετρίως, ὀρθῶς, ἄριστα οἰ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 936b, “R.” 371c, 520d, etc.; πατρίδος τετυχηκὼς ἣ νόμοις.. μάλιστα οἰκεῖσθαι δοκεῖ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.150:}}_future_ οἰκήσεται in _passive_ sense, καθ᾽ ὅτι ἄριστα ἡ πόλις οἰκήσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.67, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 185a, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.22, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 58.62, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1284b38.}}
**B** _intransitive_, **dwell, live,** of persons, families, or tribes, **have their abodes, settlements,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus generally with ἐν.., ᾤκεον δ᾽ ἐν Πλευρῶνι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.116, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.200, 400; οἴκεε ἐν Πίνδῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1234, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 967 (Oracle texts), 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.78}}; especially of metics, ἐγ Κολλυτῷ οἰκοῦντι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 12.373.60, +others; κατὰ στέγας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 314; ὑπὸ χθονός [prev. author] “fragment” 450: after 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus with _dative_ (_locative_) alone, Σαλαμῖνι “IG” 12.1.2 (6th c.BC)}}; οὐρανῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.58; ναοῖσι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 314; ἐλθόνθ᾽ ἁγνὸν ἐς Θήβης πέδον οἰ. 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 10; παρὰ κρημνοῖσιν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.34; παρὰ ὄχθον 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1098 (Lyric poetry)}}; οἰ. μετά τινος, ={συνοικεῖν τινι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 414, 990}}; κέρδη μὲν οἰκήσαντα.. **having fixed my dwelling** [there] with gain to my hosts, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 92}}; τὸ τὴν φροντίδ᾽ ἔξω τῶν κακῶν οἰκεῖν γλυκύ sweet it is for the mind **to keep** free from cares, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 1390}}; ἐπὶ προστάτου οἰ. (see. προστάτης) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.9.}}
**B.II** of cities, **to be situated,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.166, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.3, 7.5 5}}; but τὴν πόλιν σποράδην καὶ κατὰ κώμας οἰκοῦσαν **formed** of detached villages, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.35, compare 4.39}} (so in _passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.1).}}
**B.II.2** **to be governed** or **administered,** τίς τῶν πόλεων διὰ σὲ βέλτιον ᾤκησε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 599d, compare 462d, 472e, 473a, 543a, +others}}: frequently hardly distinguishable from the _passive_ (above {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus II)}}; σωφρόνως γε οἰκοῦσα [πόλις] εὖ ἂν οἰκοῖτο a state **administered** by self-control **would be** well **governed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Chrm.” 162a; ἀρετῆς, ᾗ πόλεις τε καὶ οἶκοι εὖ οἰκοῦσι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.64}}; εἰς ὀλίγους, εἰς πλείους οἰκεῖν, **to be governed** in the interest of the few or the many, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37.}}

3612:
** οἴκ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**dwelling-place,** ἱερὸν ἔσχον οἴ. ποταμοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.9}}: in _plural_, **building, house,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17, 9.13, 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.5.2, etc.}}
**2** **room, chamber,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.164, 179, 2.121.ά, 148, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.134, 3.68, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 19.31, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 130d, “Prt.” 315d, etc.}}; especially **bed-chamber,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9, 10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217d}}; **dining-room,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus Cum. 2 (_plural_).}}
**II** special senses,
**II.1** **brothel,** τὴν θυγατέρα κατίσαι ἐπ᾽ οἰκήματος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121. έ, 126; στῆσαί τινα ἐπ᾽ οἰ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.23; ἐπ οἰ. καθῆσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 163b, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.94, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.19}}; compare τέγος.
**II.2** ={οἰκίσκος}, **cage** for animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.119.}}
**II.3** horse's **stable** or **stall,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Zenon Papyri du Caire 529.5 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II.4** **temple, shrine,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144.}}
**II.4.b** **room in a temple,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 12.4.2,14.}}
**II.5** **prison,** {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.47, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 75.4, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.29, NT.Act.12.7.}}
**II.6** **storeroom,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.6,19(_plural_).}}
**II.7** **workshop,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321d.}}
**II.8** **story, floor,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.52 (uncertain reading).}}
**II.9** _metaphorically_, αἰσθητικὸν οἴ., of man, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Secundus Gnomologus “Sententiae - FPG” 7.}}

3613:
** οἰκ-ητήριον**, τό,
**dwelling-place, habitation,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 171, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1114, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 393a4, compare “fragment” 482, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 12.5.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 28, “POxy.” 281.11 (_plural_, 1st c.AD), etc.}}
**2** Astrology texts, **house,** Thrasyll. in {{Refs|“Catalogus Codicum Astrologorum” 8(3).100.7.}}

3614:
** οἰκί-α**,
_Ionic dialect_ **οἰκίη**, Cretan dialect and _Locrian dialect_ **ϝοικία**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Leg.Gort.” 5.26, “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.8 (5th c.BC),compare “IG” 14.636 (from Petelia)}}:—ἡ, **building, house, dwelling,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.17, 114, etc.; ἡ οἰκία ἡ δημοσία “IG” 12.94.36; οἰκία ἱερά [prev. work]363.24; κατ᾽ οἰκίαν}} at **home,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 180d; ἰδίᾳ καὶ κατ᾽ οἰκίας [prev. author] “Lg.” 788a}}; ἔτυχεν ἐπὶ τῆς οἰ. was at **home,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Xenophon Ephesius Scriptor Eroticus 5.4: in Comedy texts and Attic Prose much more frequently than οἶκος: sometimes opposed to οἶκος}} as **house** to **set of apartments** or **room,** τᾶν οἰκιᾶν τιμὰν κομιζέσθω τῶ οἴκω ἑκάστω δύο μνᾶς {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 306.16 (Tegea, 4th c.BC),compare “PTeb.” 46.9 (compare 18) (2nd c.BC), 38.14, 15 (2nd c.BC), “PFay.” 31.11 (2nd c.AD).}}
**2** in _Attic dialect_ law, οἶκος was distinguished from οἰκία, the former being the **property** left at a person's death, his **estate,** the latter the **dwelling-house** only, as stated by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.5, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.224, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Gal.fragment” 12, etc.}}
**3** distinguished from συνοικία, as **one's own apartments** from **those let out to lodgers,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.124.}}
**II** **household, domestic establishment,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 520e}}; δὔ οἰκίας ᾤκει, i. e. he kept two **establishments,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.26, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265b26}}; more primitive than the πόλις,{{Refs|[prev. work] 1252b17, “EN” 1162a18, +others}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τῆς οἰκίας the **house**-steward, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 150.16 (3rd c.BC).}}
**III** **the household,** i. e. **inmates of the house,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 909b (_plural_).}}
**IV** **house** or **family from which one is descended,** οἰκίης ἀγαθῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.107}}; οἰκίης οὐ φλαυροτέρης{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 99; οἰκίης οὐκ ἐπιφανέος [prev. author] 2.172; τῇ Κύρου οἰκίῃ συγγενέες [prev. author] 3.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 472b; ἐκ τῶν μεγίστων οἰ. 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 117.5, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.126, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.6, etc.; περὶ ὀλίγας οἰ. αἱ.. τραγῳδίαι συντίθενται 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453a19; ἡ Μακεδόνων οἰ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.37.7; ἡ βασιλικὴ οἰ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.57.}}
**V** medical **school,** ἐξ οἰκίας Ἡροφίλου {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus “Praef.”, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(2).145.}}

3616:
** οἰκοδεσποτ-έω**,
**to be master of a house** or **head of a family,** {{NT|NT.1Tim.5.14.}}
**II** Astrology texts, **predominate,** [{{Refs 1st c.AD+|POxy. 235.16 (1st c.AD), “PLond.” 1.130.163 (1st-2nd c.AD), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.908c, 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 39, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Astr.” 20, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 64.8, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 9.5, etc.; compare {οἰκοδεσπότης} 11.}}]

3617:
** οἰκοδεσπότ-ης**, ου, ὁ,
**master** or **steward of a house,** {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 225, NT.Matt.10.25, “PMeyer” 24.2 (6th c.AD)}}: _metaphorically_, of God, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.22.4 (οἰκίας δ.}} was preferred by the Atticists, as in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 954b: so οἴκων δεσπόται 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.32, compare 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 348).}}
**2** **native ruler,** opposed to foreign emperor, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 2.11.}}
**II** Astrology texts, of a planet, **owner of a domicile** or otherwise predominant, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 97, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus cited in 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 9.5, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus Astr. 1.13, “PSI” 3.158.80 (3rd c.AD).}}

3618:
** οἰκοδομ-έω**,
_future_ -ήσω: _aorist_ ᾠκοδόμησα (not οἰκ- in _Attic dialect_): _perfect_ ᾠκοδόμηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 514b}}: but later _Attic dialect_ _perfect_ _passive_ οἰκοδομημένοι {{Refs|“IG” 22.1627.398}}:—**build a house**: generally, **build,** νεὼν καὶ βωμόν {{Refs|[prev. work]12.24.13}}; νηόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.21; οἰκίας [prev. work] 114}}; γέφυραν{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 186; πυραμίδας [prev. author] 2.101, compare 5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 42; [αἱ μέλιτται] οἰ. τὰ κηρία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 623b27: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 161e, 165d:}} —also in _middle_, οἰκοδομέεσθαι οἴκημα **build oneself** a house, **have** it **built,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.121.α᾽, compare 148; νεωσοίκους 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.7; τείχη 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.11; οἰκίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 372a, etc.}}: —_passive_, **to be built,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 127}}; **τὰ** **-ούμενα**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 730b8.}}
**b** generally, **fashion,** καταπέτασμα {{LXX|LXX.3Ki.6.36.}}
**2** _metaphorically_, **build** or **found upon,** ἔργα ἐπί τι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.15; οἰ. τέχνην ἔπεσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 749.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **build up, edify,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.1, 10.23, etc.}}; οἰ. εἰς τὸν ἕνα {{NT|NT.1Thes.5.11: but also in bad sense, οἰκοδομηθήσεται εἰς τὸ ἐσθίειν}} **will be emboldened,** {{NT|NT.1Cor.8.10}}; compare ἀνοικοδομέω.

3619:
** οἰκοδομ-ή**, ἡ,
={-δόμησις},{-δομία}, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 499.93 (3rd c.BC)}}, proverbial _Laconian dialect_ cited in [{{LXX+4th c.BC+|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see entry {ἵππους}, LXX.1Ch.26.27, “PGrenf.” 1.21.17 (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 655.2 (1st c.BC), 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.46, 3rd c.BC: Eratosthenes Epicus cited in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.1.15, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 5.2.5, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Camillus” 32: condemned by 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 394: earlier examples, as 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1137b30 (variant), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 3.8.5 codices, are uncertain}}]
**II** = {οἰκοδόμημα}, {{NT+1st c.AD+|NT.Matt.24.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Lucullus” 39, “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 4449 (from Beroea), +others: _metaphorically_, NT.1Cor.3.9.}}

3620:
** οἰκοδομ-ία**, ἡ,
={-δόμησις}{{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, “IG” 12.338.15 (probably), 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 154, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.93, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 804c, “PHal.” 1.181 (3rd c.BC).}}
**II** **building, edifice,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 758e, 759a, +others}}

3621:
** οἰκονομ-έω**,
**manage as a house-steward, order, regulate,** θαλάμους πατρός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 190 (Lyric poetry); τὴν οἰκίαν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 209d; τὰ ἴδια 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.4.12, etc.; τὸν ἴδιον βίον 3rd c.BC: Euphro Comicus 4}}; ταῦτα (i.e. meats) {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 110.20; ὄχλον “Comica Adespota” 119}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Oeconomica” 1343a23: with _dative_, ἄνθρωπος.. μεγίστοις -εῖται πράγμασιν 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 531.14.}}
**2** **dispense,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 256e}}; **disburse**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 667.20 (Athens, 2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **treat** a substance with another, πυρίτην ὀξάλμῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pseudo-Democritus Alchemista Alch. p.44 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}: _metaphorically_, of a poet, εἰ τὰ ἄλλα μὴ εὖ οἰ. **treat, handle,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1453a29}}; so (in _middle_) of an artist, οἰ. τὴν ὕλην {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 51}}:—_passive_, τὰ σκέμματα.. ᾠκονομήσθω {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “D.” 3.8.}}
**4** of public officers, **administer,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.26.6, 4.67.9}}:—_passive_, πολιτεία ἀρίστη ἡ ὑπὸ τῶν ἀρίστων -ουμένη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1288a34.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to be a house-steward,** {{NT|NT.Luke.16.2.}}

3622:
** οἰκονομ-ία**, ἡ,
**management of a household** or **family, husbandry, thrift,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36b, “R.” 498a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.1, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1141b32, “Pol.” 1253b2 ff.: _plural_, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 407b}}; **households,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 744b18.}}
**2** generally, **direction, regulation,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.29U.}}; especially of a State, **administration,** αἱ κατὰ τὴν πόλιν οἰ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.97}}; **principles of government,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.338; τῶν γεγονότων 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.4.3, +others; πολιτικὴ οἰ. 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.32 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; ἡ τῆς ἀρχῆς οἰ. 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 6.1.1}}; of a fund, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 577.9 (Milet., 3rd-2nd c.BC).}}
**3** **arrangement,** ἡ περὶ τὸν νοσέοντα οἰ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.2.24}}; ἡ περὶ τῶν ὠνίων οἰ. **market, fair**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“SIG” 695.35 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae.); οἰκονομίαι}}**proceedings,** {{Refs|Inscriptiones Graecae 9(1).226 (from Drymaea)}}; τίνα οἰκονομίαν προσαγήγοχας what **steps** you have taken, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 240.10 (3rd c.BC)}}; αὕτη φύσεως οἰ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.9.10}}; of a literary work, **arrangement,** ἡ κατὰ μέρος οἰ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.1, compare 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Epistula ad Pompeium” 4, “Comp.” 25, Scholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.328: _plural_, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.142a.}}
**4** in Egypt, **office of** οἰκονόμος, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 24.62 (2nd c.BC), +others}}
**5** **stewardship,** {{LXX+NT|LXX.Isa.22.19, NT.Luke.16.2.}}
**6** **plan, dispensation,** {{NT|NT.Eph.1.10, 3.2.}}
**7** in bad sense, **scheming,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 4.51.}}
**II** **public revenue** of a state, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“BMus.Inscription” 897.14, +others (Halic., 3rd c.BC).}}
**III** **transaction, contract,** or legal **instrument**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“CPR” 4.1 (1st c.AD), “BGU” 457.10 (2nd c.AD), etc.}}
**IV** magical **operation** or **process,** {{Refs|PMag.Par. 1.161,292,2009.}}

3623:
** οἰκόνομ-ος**, ὁ, ἡ,
**one who manages a household,** ={οἰκοδεσπότης}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 1.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 417a, etc.}}; **steward of an estate,** Ζήνωνι τῷ οἰ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 48.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; **house-steward,** being a slave, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(1).40, 1235, “IGRom.” 4.1699, “TAM” 2.518 (from Pinara), probably in “BCH” 52.413: _metaphorically_, οἰ. ἡδονῆς 4th c.BC: Alcidamas Rhetor cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1406a27.}}
**2** generally, **manager, administrator,** opposed to τύραννος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1314b7, etc.: hence}}
**2.b** title of a subordinate state official, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“IG” 5(2).389.15 (Lusi, 4th-3rd c.BC)}}; also of a high financial officer, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Inscription Prien.” 6.30 (4th c.BC), “BMus.Inscription” 448.7 (Ephesus, 4th c.BC)}}; under a monarch, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“OGI” 225.37 (3rd c.BC)}}; in Egypt, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“ORev.Laws” 3.3, 5.6, +others (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 39.11 (2nd c.BC), etc.}}; in charge of the Serapeum, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 56.7 (2nd c.BC, _plural_)}}; οἱ Καίσαρος οἰ., = Latin _procuratores Caesaris,_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 39}}; θεοῦ οἰ. **minister** of God, {{NT|NT.Tit.1.7, cf. NT.1Cor.4.1.}}
**II** as _feminine_, **housekeeper, housewife,** {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Phocylides Lyricus 3.7, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.7: Metaphorical, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 155.}}

3624:
** οἶκος**, ὁ,
**house,** not only of **built houses,** but of **any dwelling-place,** as that of Achilles at Troy (see. κλισία), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.471, 572, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 65}}; of the Cyclops' **cave,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.478}}; of a **tent,** {{LXX|LXX.Gen.31.33}}; οἶκον ἱκάνεται is coming **home,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.7}}; εἰς or ἐς οἶκον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 459, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 240; πρὸς οἶκον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 867, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1491, etc.; κατ᾽ οἶκον [prev. author] “El.” 929, etc.; κατ᾽ οἶκον ἐν δόμοις [prev. author] “Tr.” 689}}; οἱ κατ᾽ οἶκον{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 934; αἱ κατ᾽ οἶκον κακοπραγίαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.60; τἂν οἴκῳ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 579; ἐν οἴκῳ καθεύδειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.1.4,8; οἱ ἐν οἴκῳ “PCair.Zen.” 93.10 (3rd c.BC); ἐξ οἴκου ἀποδημεῖν [prev. work] 44.23 (3rd c.BC)}}; ἐπ᾽ οἴκου ἀποχωρῆσαι go **home** wards, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.87, compare 30, 108, 2.31, etc.}}; ἀπ᾽ οἴκου from **home,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 1.99, etc.}}; compare οἰκία.
**b** frequently omitted after εἰς or ἐν, see at {εἰς} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1.4c, ἐν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1.2.}}
**2** **room, chamber,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.356, 19.514, 598; οἶ. θερμός 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.164}}; **dining-hall,** ἑπτάκλινος οἶ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista Comedy texts 66, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.18}}; **room** in a temple, {{Refs|“IG” 42(1).110 A 27, +others(Epid., _plural_): _plural_ οἶκοι}} frequently, = **a single house,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.417, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 230, 524, etc.}}; κλαυθμῶν τῶν ἐξ οἴκων **domestic** griefs, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ag.” 1554 (_anapaest meter_); ἀπ᾽ οἴκων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 762}}; ἐς or πρὸς οἴκους, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 311, 383}}; κατ᾽ οἴκους at **home, within,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.79, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 65, 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 4.52.}}
**3** of public buildings, **meeting-house, hall,** οἶ. Κηρύκων {{Refs|“IG” 22.1672.24; Δεκελειῶν [prev. work] 1237.33}} of **treasuries** at Delos, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|“JHS” 25.310, +others, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see {θησαυρό; ἐγκριτήριοι οἶ}}}]., see at {ἐγκριτήριος}; **temple,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|Inscriptiones Graecae 4.1580 (from Aegina), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.143, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1373, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 600; οἶ. τεμένιος ἱερός “SIG” 987.3, compare 25 (Chios, 4th c.BC)}}; ἐν τῷ οἴ. τοῦ Ἄμμωνος {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 79.4 (2nd c.BC)}}; ὁ οἶ. [τοῦ θεοῦ] {{NT|NT.Matt.21.13, +others}}; of a funerary monument, {{Refs|“BCH” 2.610 (from Cibyra), 18.11 (from Magnesia)}}; ἀΐδιοι οἶ., i.e. tombs, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.51.}}
**4** **cage** for birds, {{Refs|[prev. author] 13.82}} (assuming variant, οἰκ[ίσκ]ῳ Valck.); **beehive,** {{Refs|Gp. 15.2.22.}}
**5** Astrology texts, **domicile** of a planet, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PLond.” 1.98r. 12, +others(1st-2nd c.AD), 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.7, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 7.25, 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.141, 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 162.2.}}
**II** **one's household goods, substance** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|compare (οἴκοθεν 2), οἶ. ἐμὸς διόλωλε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.64; ἐσθίεταί μοι οἶ. 4.318; καὶ οἶ. καὶ κλῆρος ἀκήρατος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.498; οἶκον δέ κ᾽ ἐγὼ καὶ κτήματα δοίην 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.314, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.53, 7.224, etc.}}: in _Attic dialect_ law, **estate, inheritance,** οἶκον κατασχεῖν τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.15, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.93, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.15, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.4, etc.; οἶ. πέντε ταλάντων 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 7.42}}; compare οἰκία.
**III** a reigning **house,** οἶ. ὁ βασιλέος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.31, compare 6.9, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.137, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.41; Ἀγαμεμνονίων οἴκων ὄλεθρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 862 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 594 (Lyric poetry)}}; also of any **family,** {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.4, LXX.Gen.7.1, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.85}}; οἶ. Σεβαστός, = **domus Augusta,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.520; οὐδενὸς οἴκου δεύτερον γενόμενον “IG” 42(1).84.32 (Epid., 1st c.AD); τοὺς πρώτους τᾶς πόλιος οἴκους [prev. work]86.15([prev. passage], 1st c.AD). (Orig. ϝοῖκος, compare ϝοίκω, οἰκία}}: cf. Sanskrit _veśás, viś-_ 'house', Latin _vicus, vicinus,_ etc.)

3625:
** οἰκουμέν-η**
(i.e. γῆ), ἡ, **inhabited region,** see at {οἰκέω} {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus 1}}; then **the Greek world,** opposed to barbarian lands, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.35; πᾶσα ἡ οἰ. [prev. author] 18.48; in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362b26, ἡ οἰ.}} = **the inhabited world** (including non-Greek lands, as Ethiopia, India, Scythia), as opposed to possibly uninhabited regions,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|compare 2nd c.AD: Cleomedes Astronomus 2.1; in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392b26, ἥδε ἡ οἰ.}} = our **world** (= Asia, Libya, Europe); οἰκουμέναι **worlds,**{{LXX+4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 31; ἡ φιλία περιχορεύει τὴν οἰ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Gnomologium Vaticanum - Kleine Schriften” 52; σοῦ (i.e. Ptolemy 11 or 111) τῆς οἰ. πάσης βασιλεύοντος “PSI” 5.541.7, cf. LXX.1Esd.2.3}}; loosely, **the whole world,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Euxenippo” 33 (probably), 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 179, “PMag.Lond.” 121.704, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Halc.” 3, 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.350a}}: so perhaps in some passages cited under {{Refs|11.}}
**II** **the Roman world,** ὁ ἀγαθὸς δαίμων (etc.) τῆς οἰ., i.e. the Emperor, {{NT+1st c.AD+|“OGI” 666.4,668.5, “POxy.” 1021.5 (1st c.AD), “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2581-2,4416, NT.Luke.2.1, NT.Act.17.6, 24.5, “Sammelb.” 176.2 (2nd c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 10.7, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 7.}}
**III** ἡ οἰ. ἡ μέλλουσα **the world** to come, i.e. the kingdom of Christ, {{NT|NT.Heb.2.5.}}

3626:
** οἰκουρός**, όν,
(οὖρος B) **watching** or **keeping the house,** of a watch-dog, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 970}}; of a cock, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.998b}}; οἰ. ὄφις, of the sacred serpent in the Acropolis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 759, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 72 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**II** **keeping at home**: as _substantive_, οἰκουρός, ἡ, **mistress of the house, housekeeper,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 487, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1277}}: as _adjective_, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. author] “HF” 45 (_masculine_); ἡ θεὸς ἡ καλουμένη οἰ. “PLond.” 1.125v. 11 (5th c.AD)}}; used in praise of a good wife, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.431, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 56.3.}}
**II.2** contemptuously of a man, **stay-at-home,** opposed to one who goes forth to war, λέοντ᾽ ἄναλκιν.. οἰκουρόν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1225, compare 1626, 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.82; τὸν ὑγρὸν τοῦτον καὶ οἰ. 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.751a; δίαιτα οἰ. καὶ ἀργή [prev. author] “Per.” 34.}}

3627:
Included with: ** οἰκτ-ίρω** [ῑ]
(in codices frequently -ειρ-, but -ῑρ- in early Inscrr., {{Refs|“IG” 12.971,976,982}}, see entry near the end; compare κατοικτίρω): _imperfect_ ᾤκτ (ε) ιρον {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Stesichorus Lyricus 18}}: _future_ οἰκτῐρῶ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 199.6 (-ερεῖ codices):}}_aorist_ ᾤκτ (ε) ιρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.814, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 354, +others}}; _Ionic dialect_ οἴκτ (ε) ιρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.52}}:—_passive_, only _present_ and _imperfect_ {{NT+8th c.BC+|[prev. passage], 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.40, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1412:—later forms (as if from οἰκτειρέω): _future_ οἰκτειρήσω Scholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.740, LXX.Exo.33.19, NT.Rom.9.15, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Descriptiones” 30.18: _aorist_ ᾠκτείρησα Scholia 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 353: _aorist_ _passive_ οἰκτειρηθῆναι [prev. work] 637.—Cf. οἰκτείρημα}}:—**pity, have pity upon,** with _accusative_ _person_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.814, 16.5, Hdt.[same place], 7.38; ἐλεῆσαι καὶ οἰ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 288d}}; οἰ. τινά τινος **pity** one **for** or **because of** a thing, οἰκτίρω σε θεσφάτου μόρου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1321, compare “Supp.” 209; also οἰ. τινά τινος ἕνεκα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 2.7; ἐπί τινι [prev. work] 4}}: with _accusative_ _of things_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1233, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 328, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 3.1.2.}}
**2** with _infinitive_, οἰ. νιν λιπεῖν I **am sorry** to leave her, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 652}}; οἰ. εἰ.. **to be sorry that** it should be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.7}}. [_Aeolic dialect_ οἰκτίρρω {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus Gr. 2.558: hence οἰκτῑρω is probably from οἰκτιρ-ψω, and οἰκτίρετε [ῐ] in “Anthologia Graeca” 7.267 (3rd c.BC: Posidippus Comicus) is an error: but the _Attic dialect_}}_future_ is οἰκτερῶ _accusative_ to {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.559.]}}

3631:
** οἶνος**, ὁ,
**wine,** μέλας οἶ. (compare οἶνοψ) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.265, 9.196; ἐρυθρός 5.165, 9.163; αἶθοψ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.462, 4.259; ἡδύς 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.349, 9.204; ἡδύποτος 15.507; μελιηδής 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.346, +others; μελίφρων 6.264; παλαιός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.340, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.48, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 75; οἴνους παλαιοὺς εὐώδεις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.4.9; ἐΰφρων 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.246; εὐήνωρ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.622; οἶνον ἔμισγον ἐνὶ κρητῆρσι καὶ ὕδωρ 1.110: with Preps., ἐν οἴνῳ}} over one's **cups,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1227, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 23.8; παρ᾽ οἴνῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 780; παρ᾽ οἶνον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.143d; μετὰ παιδιᾶς καὶ οἴ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.28}}: also in _plural_, ἡ ἐν τοῖς οἴ. διατριβή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 641c, 645c; ἐπ᾽ οἴνοις 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 153.9: _plural_ also, οἶνοι}} **wines,** X.[same place], {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 573a, +others}}; οἶνος δωδεκάδραχμος **wine** at {{Refs 4th c.BC+|12 drachmae the cask, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.20: proverbial, οἶ. τῷ φρονεῖν ἐπισκοτεῖ 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 135}}; οἶνος καὶ ἀλάθεα (see. ἀλήθεια), **in vino veritas,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 57, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.1; οἶνος.. ἀληθής 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217e}}; οἴνῳ τὸν οἶ. ἐξελαύνειν 'to take a hair of the dog that bit you', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 300.1: οἶνος is.frequently omitted, πίνεὶν πολύν (i.e. οἶνον) 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 569, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 18.11}}; especially with names of places, Θάσιος, Χῖος, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 124, 125, 126}}: resin was used as a preservative, πισσίτης οἶ. {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.676c.}}
**2** **fermented juice** of other kinds, οἶνος ἐκ κριθέων πεποιημένος barley **wine,** a kind of **beer,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.77}}; οἶ. φοινικήϊος palm**-wine,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 86, compare 1.193}}; **lotus-wine,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.177, etc.}}; from which drinks grape**-wine** (οἶ. ἀμπέλινος) is expressly distinguished, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.60.}}
**II** **the wine-market,** τρέχ᾽ ἐς τὸν οἶ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 299.}}
**III** name of Dionysus, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Fragmenta” 216. (ϝοῖνος “Leg.Gort.” 10.39), “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 148 H}}.; cf. Latin _vinum._??

3633:
** οἴομαι**,
in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always un _contraction_ **ὀΐομαι** (except οἴομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.193, οἴοιτο 17.580, 22.12)}}, see below:—the shortened form **οἶμαι** is the one chiefly used in Trag., οἴομαι only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 758, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 28}}; but οἴομαι is frequently in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus (“Eq.” 407, +others); 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus}} does not use either form; in _Attic dialect_ Prose codices vary, but οἶμαι prevails, and was exclusively used in parenthesis (see. infr. IV): _imperfect_ ᾠόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 270, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 791, etc.; also 1 _person_ ᾤμην [prev. author] “fragment” 636, etc.}}: _future_ οἰήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 397a, etc., later οἰηθήσομαι 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “περὶ τῆς ἀρίστης διδασκαλίας” 42}}:—_Epic dialect_ _aorist_ **ὠϊσάμην** (see. below): _aorist_ ὠΐσθην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.453, 16.475}}; _participle_ ὀϊσθείς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.453}}; _Attic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ _aorist_ ᾠήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 14, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.8, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.130, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 178c, etc.}}; but rare in Comedy texts and Trag., οἰηθῇς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 860, οἰηθείς, -εῖσα, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 194.2, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 986}}; also _aorist_ _infinitive_ οἰήσασθαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 896}}:—_active_, _Epic dialect_ _present_ **ὀΐω** and **οἴω**, but only in _1st pers._ _singular_ (see. below); _Laconian dialect_ οἰῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 81, 156, 998, 5th-6th c.BC(?): Epilycus Comicus 3}}. [In the un _contraction_ forms, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses ῑ in ὀΐομαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.644, ὀΐεαι 1.561, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.380, ὀΐεται 17.586, ὀϊόμεθ᾽ 21.322, 22.165, ὀϊόμενος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.728, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.351, +others (οἰόμενος 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 8.2), ὠΐετο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.248, ὀΐσατο 1.323, 9.213, 19.390, ὀϊσάμενος 15.443 (but the variant{ὀϊσσατο},{ὀϊσσάμενος} in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}] can be supported by ὀϊσσάμενος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.1135, compare “Epica Alexandrina Adespota” 2.41, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 1006, by ὑποίζεσθαι (: ὑπονοεῖν) 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, and by ὠῐσάμην 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.291, ὠΐσατο [ῐ] 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 2.8, etc.)}}; _active_ _present_ ὀΐω has ῑ when it stands at the end of a line, also in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.215 (in fourth foot), 18.259 (before caesura in third foot)}}; but ῐ in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.558, 13.153, 23.467, etc.; οἴω as _disyllable_ is always at the end, except in 15.298, 21.533, 23.310}}.]:—**forebode, presage,** with _accusative_, κῆρας ὀϊομένῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.283; γόον δ᾽ ὠΐετο θυμός 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.248}}; **expect,** ἐελδομένοισι μάλ᾽ ἡμῖν, οὐδ᾽ ἔτ᾽ ὀϊομένοισι {{Refs|24.401}}; **suspect,** ἤ τι ὀϊσάμενος, ἢ καὶ θεὸς ὣς ἐκέλευσεν {{Refs|9.339; ἦ τινά που δόλον ἄλλον ὀΐεαι 10.380}}; **fear,** κατὰ θυμὸν ὀΐσατο, μή ἑ λαβοῦσα οὐλὴν ἀμφράσσαιτο {{Refs|19.390; τῷ ἑπόμην.., ὀϊόμενός περ, ἀνάγκῃ 14.298: _absolutely_, αἰεὶ μὲν ὀΐεαι, οὐδέ σε λήθω}} **thou art** ever **suspecting,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.561; πατὴρ δ᾽ ἐμὸς αὐτίκ᾽ ὀϊσθεὶς πολλὰ κατηρᾶτο 9.453, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.443: followed by ὡς, καὐτὸς ὀΐεαι ὥς κεν ἐτύχθη}} you can **guess** how it would have happened, {{Refs|3.255, compare 17.586: with _accusative_ et}}_future_ _infinitive_, ὀΐομαι ἄνδρα χολωσέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.78; ἅ τιν᾽ οὐ πείσεσθαι ὀΐω [prev. work] 289, compare 5.252, 284, +others}}; τὸ καὶ τελέεσθαι ὀΐω {{Refs|1.204; ἀλλ᾽ οὐ νῦν ἐρύεσθαι ὀΐομαι 20.195}}: with _accusative_ et _present_ _infinitive_, referring to present time, οὐδέ τι θυμῷ ὠΐσθη δόλον εἶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.453, compare 10.232; ὀΐσατο γὰρ θεὸν εἶναι 1.323; τῶ δ᾽ ὀΐω κείνης τάδε πάσχειν ἐννεσίῃσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.894}}: with _accusative_ et _aorist_ _infinitive_, referring to past time, τῇ δ᾽ ὀΐω κατανεῦσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.558, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.27, +others: the}}_subjunctive_ of the _infinitive_ must frequently be supplied from the context, διωκέμεναι γὰρ ὀΐω I **fear** [they] are pursuing me, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.278, compare 1.201, 12.212, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.66, +others: with}}_infinitive_ alone, when both Verbs have the same subject, as κιχήσεσθαι δέ δ᾽ ὀΐω I **think** I shall catch you, {{Refs|6.341}}; **mean, intend,** with _future_ _infinitive_, οὐ γὰρ ἔγωγ᾽ ἔτι σοὶ πείσεσθαι ὀΐω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.296, compare 170, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.215}}: with _present_ _infinitive_, οὐ γὰρ ὀΐω ἀνδρῶν δυσμενέων ἑκὰς ἱστάμενος πολεμίζειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.262.}}
**II** impersonal, only {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.312, ἀλλά μοι ὧδ᾽ ἀνὰ θυμὸν ὀΐεται}} **there comes a boding** into my heart.
**III** **think, suppose, believe,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, as 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.59, 5.644, etc.; οἶμαι βοὴν ἄμικτον ἐν πόλει πρέπειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 321}}; οἶμαι γάρ νιν ἱκετεύσειν (ἱκετεῦσαι codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 462}}; κτήσεσθαι (-σασθαι codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.19}}; διαπράξεσθαι (-ξασθαι codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 13.53; ἤδη γὰρ αὐτοὺς οἴομαι δεδειπνάναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 464}}; opposed to εἰδέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 506c4; οἴονται, ἴσασι δ᾽ οὐδέν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1389b17, compare “APo.” 75a15: followed by ὅτι.., 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.90b}}:—_passive_, μάρτυρας δύο παρεχέτω ὀμνύντας οἰόμενον **that he is the putative father,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|Schwyzer 784a (Tenos, 4th c.BC).}}
**IV** parenthetically, mostly in first person, ἐν πρώτοισιν, ὀΐω, κείσεται among the first, **I ween,** will he be lying, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.536; ἔπειτά γ᾽, ὀΐω, γνώσεαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.309, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.153, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.255, etc.: in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in _active_ form ὀΐω, except ὀΐομαι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.140, and perhaps 14.363, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 758}}; in _Attic dialect_ this parenthetic use is probably confined to the shorter form οἶμαι, _imperfect_ ᾤμη; rarely in other persons than the first, as οὐκ οἴει ἀναγκασθήσετα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 486c, compare “Tht.” 147b}}; πόσης οἴεσθε γέμει σωφροσύνη; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Smp.” 216d.}}
**IV.2** expressive of modesty or courtesy, to avoid over-great bluntness of assertion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Grg.” 483c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.3.11, etc.}}: even between a _preposition_ and its case, ἐξ οἶμαι τῆς ἀκροτάτης ἐλευθερίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 564a; ἐν οἶμαι πολλοῖς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.3}}; or between _Article_ and _substantive_, οἱ γὰρ οἶμαι βέλτιστοι {{Refs|[prev. author] 54.38.}}
**V** answering a question, **I think so, I should think so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 919, etc.; νὴ τὸν Ἡρακλέα, οἶμαί γε [prev. author] “Th.” 27; οἶμαι ἔγωγε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 47d, etc.}}; οἴεσθαί γε χρή one must **think** so, it would seem so, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 325c, “Cri.” 53d, “Phd.” 68b, “Grg.” 522a.}}
**VI** _Attic dialect_ phrases:
**VI.1** πῶς οἴε; **you can't think** how, to add force, like{πῶς δοκεῖς}; πόθος τὴν καρδίαν ἐπάταξε πῶς οἴει σφόδρα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 54.}}
**VI.2** οἴομαι δεῖν I **think** it my duty, **think** fit, hence sometimes, **intend, purpose,** λέγειν οἴεται δεῖν ποιεῖν δεινούς **his object is** to train orators, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 95c, compare 86b, “Tht.” 207e; βδελυρὸς καὶ ὑβριστὴς ᾤετο δεῖν εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.143; τοὺς ἐχθροὺς ἀμυνόμενοι τεθνάναι δεῖν ᾤοντο [prev. author] 60.31, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 316c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.6.26, “Mem.” 4.6.3, 6}}; [ὁ ἀκόλαστος] οἰόμενος δεῖν [διώκει τὰ ἡδέα] **intentionally,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1152a6, compare 1136b8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 225b}}; but οἴομαι δὲ δεῖν οὐδέν **methinks** there is no need, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 28; and in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus2.144d ἆρ᾽ οὐκ ἀναγκαῖον.. οἰηθῆναι δεῖν.. ἡμᾶς εἰδέναι ἢ τῷ ὄντι εἰδέναι}}; must we not either **think** we know or really know? (δεῖν being superfluous).
**VI.3** οἴομαι without δεῖν I **mean to, intend,** οὐκ οἴει.. δοῦναι δίκη; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 12.26, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 62d, “Ly.” 200b, “Ep.” 324b}}; οὐ βαλεῖν (variant{βάλλειν}).. ᾠήθη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1135b14, compare 1172a7.}}

3634:
** οἷος**, οἵα,
_Ionic dialect_ οἵη, οἷον, (ὅς) **such as, of what sort,** _relative_ and indirect _interrogative_ _pronoun_, _correlative_ to direct _interrogative_ ποῖος, _indefinite_ ποιός, _demonstrative_ τοῖος: ὅσσος ἔην οἷός τε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.630; ὁσσάτιόν τε καὶ οἷον 5.758: with _accusative_, οἶδ᾽ ἀρετὴν οἷός ἐσσι}} **what a man** for valour, {{Refs|13.275: frequently to be rendered by an}} _adverb_, οἷος πόλεμόνδε μέτεισι as he rushes into war, {{Refs|[prev. work] 298, etc.:}} —Usage:
**I** οἷος in an independent sentence as an exclamation of astonishment, ὢ πόποι.., οἷον ἔειπες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.455}}; οἷ. τὸ πῦρ **what** a fire is this ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1256, compare “Pers.” 733, +others; οἷ. εἰργάσασθε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 450a; οἷ. ἄνδρα λέγεις ἐν κινδύνῳ εἶναι [prev. author] “Tht.” 142b; οἷα ποιεῖς [prev. author] “Euthphr.” 15e, etc.: so in _neuter_ as}} _adverb_, see below see {{Refs|1.}}
**I.2** in indirect sentences, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 624, 1402, 1488, etc.; ὁρᾶτε δὴ ἐν οἵῳ ἐστέ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.2.12; ὁρῶν ἐν οἵοις ἐσμέν [prev. author] “An.” 3.1.15.}}
**II** more frequently as _relative_ to a _demonstrative_ τοῖος, τοιόσδε, τοιοῦτος, or to ὁ, ὅδε, as τῷ ἴκελον, οἷόν ποτε Δαίδαλος ἤσκησεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.591; θέαμα τοιοῦτον οἷ. καὶ στυγοῦντ᾽ ἐποικτίσαι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1296}}: but the _demonstrative_ _pronoun_ is frequently omitted, οὐ γὰρ ἐμὴ ἲς ἔσθ᾽ οἵη πάρος ἔσκεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.669, etc.}}; οἷ. καὶ Πάρις.. ᾔσχυνε **like as** Paris also.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 399 (Lyric poetry), compare “Pers.” 21, +others: with δή added, τοιόνδ᾽, οἷ. δὴ σύ}} **such as** you **surely** are, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.376, see below see 2}}: with περ added, οἵη περ φύλλων γενεή, τοίη δὲ καὶ ἀνδρῶν **just such as..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|6.146, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 607, 1046, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.19}}: very frequently with τε added (this οἷός τε must be distinguished from οἷός τε with _infinitive_, see{{Refs 8th c.BC+|below 111.2), οἷός τε πελώριος ἔρχεται Ἄρης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.208, compare 17.157, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.106, etc.; οἷός που 20.35: οἷός τις, οἷόν τι}} generalizes a Comparison, **the sort of person who.., thing which..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.638, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.348}}; οἶσθα εἰς οἷόν τινα κίνδυνον ἔρχει..; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 313a}}; with attraction of οἷος to the case of its antecedent (which may follow instead of preceding), οἵοις περ σὺ χρώμενοι συμβούλοις {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.185}}; and with further attraction of the subject of the _relative_ clause, οὐ γάρ πω τοίους ἴδον ἀνέρας.., οἷον Πειρίθοον (for οἷος Πειρίθοος) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.263; πρὸς ἄνδρας τολμηρούς, οἵους καὶ Ἀθηναίους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.21, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.3; περὶ τοῦ τοιούτου.., οἵου τοῦ ἑνός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 161b.}}
**II.2** οἷος, οἵα, οἷον frequently introduce an 'indirect exclamation', giving the reason for what precedes, ἄνακτα χόλος λάβεν, οἷον ἄκουσε **because of what** he heard, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.166, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.479}}; ἐμακάριζον τὴν μητέρα οἵων τέκνων ἐκύρησε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.31; ἀγανάκτησιν ἔχει ὑφ᾽ οἵων κακοπαθεῖ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.41; τὴν ἐμαυτοῦ τύχην [ἀπέκλαον], οἵου ἀνδρὸς.. ἐστερημένος εἴην 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117c, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 701.}}
**II.3** sometimes in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus οἷος}} introduces the reason for saying what is said, ὠκύμορος.. ἔσσεαι, οἷ᾽ ἀγορεύεις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.95, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.611; αἲ γάρ με θυμὸς ἀνείη ὤμ᾽ ἀποταμνόμενον κρέα ἔδμεναι, οἷα ἔοργας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.347.}}
**II.4** οἷα δὴ εἴπας saying **so and so,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86 (compare ὅσος 111.2): later with other Particles, see infr. VI.}}
**II.5** οὐδὲν οἷον.. there's nothing **like..,** ἀλλ᾽ οὐδὲν οἷ. ἐστ᾽ ἀκοῦσαι τῶν ἐπῶν there's nothing **like** hearing the verses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 966, compare “Lys.” 135; οὐδὲν γὰρ οἷ. ἀκούειν αὐτοῦ τοῦ νόμου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.46, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447c, 481b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 3.14.}}
**II.6** never used like _adverb_ οἷον (infr. V. I) with an _adjective_ = **how,** for in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.653, οἶσθα.. οἷ. ἐκεῖνος δεινὸς ἀνήρ}} = **what manner of man,** namely dread, compare {{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.94, 21.108, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.493, etc.}}
**II.7** as _indefinite_, **a sort** or **kind of..,** ὁ δ᾽ ἕτερος οἷός ἐστιν οἰκουρὸς μόνον only **a kind of** watch-dog, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 970}}; so with _adjective_, ἀφόρητος οἷος.. κρυμός **of an** intolerable **kind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.28}}; θαυμαστὸς οἷος **of a** wonderful **nature,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Zeux.” 6}}; οἷος ἔμπειρος πολέμου **rather** skilled in war, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.18}}; οἷον τετανότριχα **rather** straight-haired, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 2b}}; frequently with _superlative_, χωρίον οἷον χαλεπώτατον country **of a** very difficult **kind,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.2, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 978, “Ach.” 384, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23a; ἀνὴρ οἷος κράτιστος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1165b27; κόλπος οἷος βαθύτατος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 14.26: in some of these passages with _superlative_ it is possible to take οἷος as _relative_, supplying δυνατόν,compare ξύμμαχος ἔσομαι οἷος ἂν δύνωμαι ἄριστος “IG” 12.39.28.}}
**III** οἷος with _infinitive_ implies fitness or ability for a thing, οὐ γὰρ ἦν ὥρα οἵα τὸ πεδίον ἄρδειν **fit** for watering, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.13, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 487d}}; οἷοι φιλεῖν, μισεῖν, etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.2, etc.: frequently with τοιοῦτος}} expressed, τοιοῦτός τις οἷ. διαπονεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 395a, compare “R.” 415e}}; τὸ πρᾶγμα μέγα.. καὶ μὴ οἷ. νεωτέρῳ βουλεύσασθαι not **proper** for a young man to advise upon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.12}}: without _infinitive_, λόγους οἵους εἰς τὰ δικαστήρια {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 272a.}}
**III.b** οἷός εἰμι I **intend,** ἐβιάζετο.. καὶ οἷος ἦν ἐξευρεῖν τὴν θύραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 159 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.9, +others, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.21}}; οἷος ἦν κατεσθίειν was **on the point of** eating, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus Car. cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 7.345d.}}
**III.2** more frequently οἷός τε with _infinitive_, **fit** or **able** to do, λιποίμην οἷός τ᾽.. ἀέθλια κάλ᾽ ἀνελέσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.117 (preceded by τοῖον [prev. work] 173), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.29, 67, 91; λέγειν οἷός τε κἀγώ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 343, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.16, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 8.69, etc.}}; **inclined** to.., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.90.5, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 4.6.3: most frequently in _neuter_ _sing._ and _plural_, οἷόν τε [ἐστί]}} it is **possible** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.80, etc.; οἷά τε [ἐστί] 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.194, etc.; a _dative_ is sometimes added, μὴ οἷόν τε εἶναι ἐμοὶ κωλῦσαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.14.}}
**III.3** without _infinitive_, πάνυ προθύμως ὡς οἷός τ᾽ ἦν ἐπήραξεν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 314d: but mostly in _neuter_, οἷόν τε [ἐστίν]}} it is **possible,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.9; οὐχ οἷόν τε ἐστίν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 198, etc.}}: with _superlative_, καλὸν ὡς οἷόν τε μάλιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 349e; ὡς οἷόν τε σμικρότατα [prev. author] “Prm.” 144b; ὡς οἷόν τε διὰ βραχυτάτων [prev. author] “Grg.” 449d}}: without _superlative_, φρίττειν.. ποιεῖ ὡς οἷόν τε πάντας {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 387c (uncertain reading)}}; ὡς οἷόν τε sometimes **so far as possible,** of what cannot be done completely, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 8.75, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1313a39, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 3.}}
**IV** sometimes twice in the same clause, οἷ᾽ ἔργα δράσας οἷα λαγχάνει κακά after **what** deeds **what** sufferings are his ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 751}}; ἱερῶν οἵαν οἵων χάριν ἠνύσω **what** thanks **for what** offerings ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 994, compare 1045, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 144: so in Prose, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.29, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 195a.}}
**V** as _adverb_ in _neuter_ _singular_ οἷον, in Poets and _Ionic dialect_ Prose also _plural_ οἷα, **how,** οἷον δή νυ θεοὺς βροτοὶ αἰτιόωνται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.32, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.601, 13.633, 15.287: also with _adjectives_, οἷον ἐερσήεις}} **how** fresh, {{Refs|24.419}}; οἷον δὴ Μενέλαον ὑπέτρεσας **seeing how** thou didst shrink from M., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.587, compare 21.57. (Regul. _adverb_ οἵως is rare, as in 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.7.2, _falsa lectio_ in 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1363}}; οἷος ὢν οἵ. ἔχεις **in what a state** art thou for such a man! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 923; οἵως τέ σφ᾽ ἐσάωσα 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 4.786.)}}
**V.2** **as, just as,** οἷον ὅτε.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.295: also _neuter_ _plural_ οἷα, οἷά τις.. ἀηδών 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1142 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 105 (Lyric poetry), etc.: so οἷά τε in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, οἷά τε ληϊστῆρες 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.73, compare 9.128, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.175: strengthened by Particles, οἷα δή 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.84, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219e, “R.” 467b; οἷα δή που 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 1.14}}: later a double form occurs, οἷον ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 94, Scholia 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus p.113 H.; οἷον ὥσπερ.. 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista “Praeparatio Sophistica” p.80 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus, uncertain in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.563f.}}
**V.2.b** **as for instance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 218e, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 333a11, etc.}}; οἷον τί λέγει; **as for example,** what do you mean ? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 207a, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.271, where οἷον is}} _adjective_
**V.2.c** in numerical estimates, **about,** οἷον δέκα σταδίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.90; μῆκος οἷον ἐπὶ δέκα δακτύλους “IG” 22.1457.17.}}
**V.2.d** οἷον parenthetically prefixed to any word or phrase, **as it were, so to speak,** οἷον υἱεῖς γνησίους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 278a, compare “Cra.” 396a, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 532a6, “GA” 753a33; τῶν οἷον προγυμναζομένων 2nd c.AD: Marcus Antoninus Imperator 6.20; τοῦ οἷον δόγματος 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 9.69}}; τὴν.. οἷον φάτταν the ring-dove **of my parable,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 199b.}}
**V.2.e** οἷον explanatory, **that is to say,** τὸ νῦν ῥηθησόμενον, οἷον.. what I am about to mention, **_i.e. _,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 639b6, compare “GC” 333a11, +others}}; κατὰ τὸ μέσον, οἷον κατὰ τὴν συμβολὴν τῶν ἐν αὐτοῖς διαγωνίων in the middle, **i. e.** at the intersection of their diagonals, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Hypotyposis astronomicarum positionum” 3.16, compare 22, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {οἰοφάγῳ}, +others}}]
**V.3** like{ὡς, ἅτε}, with a _participle_, οἷα ἀπροσδοκήτου κακοῦ γενομένου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.5, compare 8.95, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203b.}}
**V.4** οὐχ οἷον or μὴ οἷον, followed by ἀλλ᾽ οὐδέ or ἀλλὰ μηδέ, **not only not.., but not even..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.20.12, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.18, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.249 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, etc.}}
**VI** strengthened with various Particles, οἷος δή, see above 11.4: **οἱοσδήποτε**, οἱαδήποτε, οἱονδήποτε, **of such and such a kind,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1114b17}}; **οἱοσδηποτοῦν**, **of whatever kind,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC(?): Hero Mechanicus “*Geometry texts” 12.30, +others, “BGU” 895.28 (2nd c.AD), 7th c.AD: Paulus Aegineta Medicus 6.88}}; **οἱοσδήπως**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|“OGI” 521.4 (Abydos, 5th-6th c.AD)}}; **οἱοσδητισοῦν**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work]515.16 (Mylasa, 3rd c.AD)}}; **οἱοσοῦν**, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Justinianus Imperator “Novellae” 30.7.1}}; **οἱοσποτοῦν**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 146b26}}; **οἱοστισοῦν**, see reading in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.76.13. [οῐ is found in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.275, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.312; and sometimes in Trag., as 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1256}}, especially in the form οἷός τε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1415, “OC” 262, 803, +others]}}

3635:
** ὀκν-έω**,
_Epic dialect_ **ὀκνείω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.255}}: _imperfect_ ὤκνεον {{Refs|20.155}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.72}}: _aorist_ ὤκνησα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.103, etc.}}: (ὄκνος):—**shrink from** doing, **scruple, hesitate** to do a thing, with _infinitive_, ὀκνείω ἵππων ἐπιβαινέμεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.255; ἀρχέμεναι πολέμοιο ὤκνεον 20.155.}} —In _Attic dialect_ mostly with collateral sense of the feeling which causes the hesitation, and so,
**1** of shame or fear (in a moral sense), ὀκνῶ προδότης καλεῖσθαι I **shrink from** being called, **fear** to be called, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 93, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.61, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator “Fragmenta” 23}}; οἷα ἐγὼ ὀκνῶ πρὸς ὑμᾶς ὀνομάσαι **shrink from** naming, **hesitate** to name, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.19, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 462e; τοσαῦθ᾽ ὅσ᾽ ὀκνήσαιμ᾽ ἂν.. εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.103, compare 24.7, etc.}}
**2** of pity, σὰς ὀκνῶ θρᾶξαι φρένας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 628, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1271.}}
**3** most commonly of cowardice or indolence, μεμηνότ᾽ ἄνδρα.. ὀκνεῖς ἰδεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Aj.” 81, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, etc.}} —The Homeric _construction_ with _infinitive_ continued most common (see. above): rarely with _accusative_, πῶς τὸ μητρὸς λέκτρον οὐκ ὀκνεῖν με δε; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 976; ὃν μήτ᾽ ὀκνεῖτε [prev. author] “OC” 731, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.21; ἂν ὀκνῇς τὸ μανθάνειν 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.1; also ὀ. περί τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.5.20; ὀ. μή.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 257c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.9, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.18.}}
**II** frequently also _absolutely_, **shrink, hesitate, hang back,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.50, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 22, 320, 5th c.BC: Antipho Sophista 55, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 18, etc.}}; of soldiers, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1297b11.}}

3636:
** ὀκν-ηρός**, ά, όν,
**shrinking, timid,** ἐλπίδες -ότεραι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 11.22; ἀσθενέας καὶ ὀ. 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 28; -ότερος ἐς τὴν πρᾶξιν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.3.5; ἐς τὰ πολεμικὰ -ότεροι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, compare 1.142}}; especially from fear, opposed to τολμηρός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.24; τὸ θῆλυ -ότερον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 608b13}}. _adverb_ **-ρῶς** **reluctantly,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.7; ὀ. διακεῖσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.28}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.6.}}
**2** **idle, sluggish,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Hierocles Facetiarum Scriptor “Facet.” 211, +others}}
**II** of things, **causing fear, vexatious, troublesome,** ἡμῖν μὲν.. ταῦτ᾽ ὀκνηρά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 834.}}

3639:
** ὄλεθρ-ος**, ὁ,
**ruin, destruction, death,** αἰπὺς ὄ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.174, +others; λυγρὸς ὄ. 10.174, +others; ὄ. ἀδευκής 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.489; οἴκτιστος ὄ. 23.79; ἵνα ψυχῆς ὤκιστος ὄ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.325}}; ὀλέθρου πείρατα, like{τέλος θανάτοιο}, the consummation **of death,** {{Refs|6.143}}; ὄ., opposed to γένεσις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.21, 27}}; οὐκ εἰς ὄλεθρο; as an imprecation, **plague take thee!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 430}}; χρημάτων ὀλέθρῳ by **destruction** of property. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.27; εἶναι ἐν ὀλέθρῳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.29; ἐπ᾽ ὀλέθρῳ τῶν χρωμένων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 534; ἐκκλησιάζειν ἐπ᾽ ὀλέθρῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 84; οὐκ ἐπὶ δουλείᾳ κολάζοντες οὐδ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ὀλέθρῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 471a: _plural_, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.140S.}}
**II** **that which causes destruction, pest, plague,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 326}}; contemptuously of persons, γεγονὼς κακῶς καὶ ἐὼν ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142; ὑπὸ γερόντων ὀ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 325; ὄ. ἄνθρωπος 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 376, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 533.13}}; ὄ. Μακεδών, of Philip, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.31}}; ὄ. γραμματεύς a **pestilent** scribe, of Aeschines, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.127}}; τὸν βάσκανον, τὸν δ᾽ ὄ. the cheat, the **pest!** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 21.209; ἀνθρώπους οὐδ᾽ ἐλευθέρους ἀλλ᾽ ὀ. [prev. author] 23.202; πολλοὶ ὄ. καὶ μεγάλοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 491b.}}
**III** **seduction,** Ἑλένης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1382 (troch.).}}

3641:
** ὀλίγος** [ῐ], η, ον,
later **ὀλίος** (which see), of Size, **little, small,** frequently in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, rarer later, opposed to μέγας, σάκος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.376; κῦμα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.94, etc.}}; ὀλίγῃ ὀπί with **small, low** voice, {{Refs|14.492}}; of stature,{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|9.515; ὀ. κῶρος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.47; οὐκ ὀλίγης αἷμα βοὸς κέχυται Callias Epigrammaticus “Aet.Oxy.” 2080.85}}; of Space, ὀ. χῶρος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.161, etc.}}; of Time, χρόνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|19.157, 23.418, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.38, etc.; ἐν βραχεῖ κὠλίγῳ χρόνῳ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 646 (cf. IV. 3).}}
**2** sometimes in a sense between that of Size and Quantity, ὀ. δόσις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.208; οὖρα ὀλίγα}}**not copious,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.2}}; ὑποστάσιες ὀλίγαι **slight** sediments, {{Refs|[prev. work] 17}}; ὀ. καὶ οὐδέν **little** or nothing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 23a; οὐδὲν ἢ ὀ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 651b17.}}
**3** of Degree, ὀ. καὶ μέγας **of low** and high **degree,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Callinus Epicus 1.17.}}
**4** **weak,** ἀθυμοτέρη καὶ ὀλιγωτέρη φύσις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ παρθενίων” 1.}}
**II** of Number, **few,** or of Quantity, **little,** not in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, frequently in _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1417, “Eq.” 717, etc.}}, but rare in Trag., as ὀλίγα κακά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 330.—The governing body in Oligarchies and the oligarchical party in Democracies was called οἱ ὀ., 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.38, 8.9, etc.}}; ἡ ὑπὸ τῶν ὀ. δυναστεία, αἱ διὰ τῶν ὀ. δυναστεῖαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 291d, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 60.25; ἑνὸς καὶ πλήθους τὸ ὀ. μέσον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 303a.}}
**II.2** with _infinitive_, ὀλίγους.. στρατιῇ τῇ Μήδων συμβαλεῖν **too few** to engage.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.109, compare 7.207; μὴ.. αἱ σφέτεραι δέκα νῆες ὀλίγαι ἀμύνειν ὦσιν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.50.}}
**III** _neuter_ ὀλίγον as _adverb_, **a little, slightly, little,** with Verbs, ὀ. παρακλίνας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.424, compare 11.52, 12.452; φροντίσας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 163; ὀ. τοῦ ποιήματος προελθών 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 339d: also _neuter_ _plural_, ἠκροβολίσαντο ὀλίγα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.73.}}
**III.2** with _comparative_ _adjectives_, ὀλίγον προγενέστερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.789; ὀ. ἧσσον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.365; στιβαρώτερον οὐκ ὀ. περ 8.187; φέρτερος οὐκ ὀ. περ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.217}}; ὀ. τι πρότερον, variant for{ὀλίγῳ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79, 81, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 262b, etc.; ὀ. ὕστερον [prev. author] “Grg.” 454b, etc.}}; but ὀλίγῳ is more frequently in Prose, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus [same places] (with variant), 7.113, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 460c, “R.” 327c, etc.}}
**IV** special Phrases:
**IV.1** ὀλίγου δεῖν **almost** (see. δεῖ II); ὀλίγου ἐδέησε καταλαβεῖν wanted **but little** of overtaking, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.γ᾽: hence ὀλίγου}} alone, **within a little, allbut, almost,** ὀλίγου σεκύνες διεδηλήσαντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.37, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 348, 381, “Nu.” 722, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 14.17, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361b, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.334, etc.; ὀ. ἅπαντες 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b; ὀ. ἐς χιλίους}}**close upon** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1, 000, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.124}}; ὀ. ἦλθεν ἑλεῖν{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(see. below 9) 2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 1.13.6.}}
**IV.2** δι᾽ ὀλίγου **at a short distance,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1098, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89, 3.21, uncertain in 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 762 (Lyric poetry)}}; for (during) **a short time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77, 2.85, 3.43}}; within or after **a short time,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 6.11, 47, 7.39, etc.}}; but
**IV.2.b** δι᾽ ὀλίγων **in few words,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 31d, etc.; see infr. VI. 2.}}
**IV.3** ἐν ὀλίγῳ (χώρῳ is added in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.70)}} **in a small space, within small compass,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1126 (Lyric poetry); ἐν ὀ. στρατοπεδευομένοις 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26}}; κυκλωθέντων ἐν ὀ.{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 96; εἰς ταὐτὸ πάντα.. ἁθροίσαντ᾽ ἐν ὀ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.18}}; also ἐν ὀ. (i.e. χρόνῳ) **for a brief time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.92}}; but also, **in a short time, quickly,** ἔγνων καὶ περὶ τῶν ποιητῶν ἐν ὀ. τοῦτο {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 22b, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.55, NT.Act.26.28.}}
**IV.3.b** ἐν ὀλίγοις **one among few,** i.e. **exceedingly, remarkably,** ποταμὸς ἐν ὀ. μέγας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.52; ἐν ὀλίγοισι Περσέων.. ἀνὴρ δόκιμος [prev. author] 9.41}}: frequently in later writers, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pompeius” 10, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 3.1; so σὺν ὀλίγοις, see below 10.}}
**IV.4** ἐξ ὀλίγου **at short notice, suddenly,** ἐξ ὀ. καὶ δι᾽ ὀργῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, compare 61, 4.108, etc.}}
**IV.5** ἐς ὀλίγον, like{παρ᾽ ὀλίγον}, **within a little,** ἐς ὀ. ἀφίκετο τὸ στράτευμα νικηθῆναι {{Refs|[prev. work] 129.}}
**IV.6** ἐπ᾽ ὀλίγον **for a short time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προρρητικόν” 1.26, 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 4.4.29, “Geoponica” 7.12.22, 10.7.10, etc.}}; **a little at a time,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 18; εἴρηται ἐν τῷ [βιβλίῳ] ἐπ᾽ ὀλίγον}}**a little way on, near the beginning,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 15.428.}}
**IV.7** κατ᾽ ὀλίγον **by little and little,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 85d, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Nec.” 11, etc.; ἐκ τοῦ κατ᾽ ὀ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.34, 1st c.AD: Athenaeus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 1.2.6}}: but the _adjective_ frequently takes the gender and number of its _substantive_, κατ᾽ ὀλίγους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.93, 8.113}}; οὗτοι κατ᾽ ὀλίγους γινόμενοι ἐμάχοντο fought **few at a time, in small parties,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.102, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 197d}}; κατ᾽ ὀλίγον μαχεῖται (i.e. τὸ πλῆθος αὐτῶν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.10.}}
**IV.8** μετ᾽ ὀλίγον τούτων **shortly after..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.1.2.}}
**IV.9** παρ᾽ ὀλίγον **with little to spare, only just,** ἀπέφυγες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 870 (Lyric poetry)}}; **to within a short distance of,** παρ᾽ ὀ. ἦλθε.. ἐκπεσεῖν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.55.4, compare 18.46.12}}; but
**IV.9.b** παρ᾽ ὀ. ποιεῖσθαι hold of **small** account, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.11.}}
**IV.10** σὺν ὀλίγοις, ={ἐν ὀλίγοις, ἐπαινεθῆναι} {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 3; see supr. IV. 3b.}}
**V** regular _adverb_ ὀλίγως is rare, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.2Pet.2.18, 2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Isa.10.7; οὐκ ὀ. “Anthologia Graeca” 12.205 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus).}}
**VI** Comparison:
**VI.1** the _comparative_ is commonly supplied by μείων, ἥσσων, or ἐλάσσων (which see); the older form ὀλείζων (from *ὀλειγ-yων) is found in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, λαοὶ δ᾽ ὑπ᾽ ὀλείζονες ἦσαν (ὑπολίζονες codices) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.519}}; so in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.76.8 (written ὄλεζον [prev. work]63.17, +others); τοῖσι.. ὀλείζοσι μυστηρίοισιν [prev. work]6.76, compare 95; ὀλείζους is probably in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 2.1 (μείζους codices)}}; so in Alexandrian Poets, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 72 (ὀλίζοσι codices), “Anthologia Graeca” 9.521}}; ἐς ὀλίζονας ἀστέρας ἄρκτου poetical cited in {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|4th c.AD(?): Theodosius Alexandrinus Grammaticus in Hilgard “Exc.exHdn.” p.19; also ὀλίζωνες 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 123, ὀλίζωνα [prev. work] 372}}; ὀλιζότερος is found in {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. author] “Al.” 479, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 3.65, 394}}, compare ὀλιζό; ὀλιγώτερος is found in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Virg.” I}} (with the sense **weaker,** see{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|above 1.4), 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.70, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 42, “Mith.” 24, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 2.42, 6.51.}}
**VI.2** _superlative_ ὀλίγιστος, η, ον, always of Number or Quantity, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.223, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 723, “IG” 12.54.7, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 115, “Pl.” 628, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 473b, +others}}; ὀλιγίστου, _superlative_ of ὀλίγου (cf. IV. I), **very nearly,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc., 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (ubi ὀλιγωστοῦ)}}; ὀλίγιστον as _adverb_, **least, very little,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 587b; τὸ ὀ.}}**at least,** [{{Refs|[prev. author] “Prm.” 149a; ὡς ὀλίγιστα [prev. author] “Grg.” 510a, “Lg.” 953a (variant{ὀλιγοστά})}}]; ἐν ὀλιγίστῳ διασαφῶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1262.54; so δι᾽ ὀλιγίστων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Epistulae” 351d (interpolated). (Aspirated in papyri of ii-3rd c.AD, as μεθ᾽ ὁλίγον “BGU” 388.11, compare 146.10.)}}

3643:
** ὀλῐγωρ-έω**,
_aorist_ I written ὠλιώρησα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“IG” 12(8).53.5 (Imbros, 2nd c.BC)}}: —**esteem little** or **lightly, make small account of,** with _genitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.4.3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 28d, “Phd.” 68c, etc.}}
**2** _absolutely_, **take no heed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.9, 6.91, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.41, etc.; εἴς τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1379b28}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 180b; τοῖς οὕτως ὠλιγωρημένοις 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.21.}}
**3** later with _accusative_, **neglect,** τὴν ἄμπελον {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“PAvrom.” 1.25 (1st c.BC)}}: with _infinitive_, **intermit, neglect** to do something, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.243.}}

3650:
** ὅλος**, η, ον,
_Ionic dialect_ **οὖλος**, η, ον, as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (twice, see below), 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus (see. below), 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.4, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 14, “Carn.” 13, +others (but ὅλος in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.126, 4.64, 7.167, 8.113}} (compare ἡμι-ολίας {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5.88), 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 1.7, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.18, 5.12, 6.7 (but οὖλος 8.56)}}; ὅλως uncertain in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 73 codices)}}:—**whole, entire, complete in all its parts,** of persons and things, ἄρτος οὖλος a **whole** loaf, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.343}}; μηνὶ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ οὔλῳ in a **whole** month, {{Refs|24.118}}; οὖλος ὁρᾷ, οὖλος δὲ νοεῖ, οὖλος δέ τ᾽ ἀκούει (i.e. ὁ θεός) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 24}}; ὅλος ἑσπέρας ὀφθαλμός, i.e. the **full** moon, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.19}}; ὁ ὅ. χρόνος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.30; τρεῖς ὅλους.. ἑκμήνους χρόνους 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1136}}; ἐπ᾽ ὤμοις ὅλην πόλιν φέρων a **whole** city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1131; ἐκπιεῖν ὅλον πίθον [prev. author] “Cyc.” 217; ὅλους ἐκ κριβάνου βοῦς 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 85; λαβράκιον ὀπτᾶν ὅ. 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 222.3, etc.}}; πόλεις ὅλαι **whole, entire** cities, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512b}}; ὅλη ἡ πόλις, the city **as a whole,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 519e}}; ὅλους ποιητὰς ἐκμανθάνειν learn **whole** poets by heart, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 811a: it may either precede the _Article_ or follow the}} _substantive_, τῆς ἡμέρας ὅλης in the course of the **whole** day, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.11; δι᾽ ὅλης τῆς νυκτός [prev. work] 4.2.4}}; ὅλην τὴν νύκτα or τὴν νύκτα ὅλην, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 7.5.15, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 67.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 219c; ὅ. τὸ δέρμα 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 498; ἡ πόλις ὅ. [prev. author] 882, etc.}}: less frequently between _Article_ and _substantive_, τὸν ὅ. ἀμφὶ χρόνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 2.30; ἡ ὅ. ἀδικία 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 344c; τὸ ὅ. πρόσωπον [prev. author] “Prt.” 329e; τῇ ὅ. φάλαγγι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.8.11: joined with εἷς, ἡμέρας.. οὐχ ὅλης μιᾶς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 480; εἶδος ἓν ὅλον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 56e; with πᾶς, ὅλην καὶ πᾶσαν τὴν οἰκίαν [prev. author] “Lg.” 808a, compare “R.” 486a; πρὸς τῷ διακινδυνεύειν ὅ. καὶ πᾶς ἦν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.94.10 (so without πᾶς, οὕτως ἔκφρων ἦν καὶ ὅλος πρὸς τῷ λήμματι καὶ τῷ δωροδοκήματι, ὥστε.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.127); τὸ ὅ. αὐτοῖς ἦν καὶ τὸ πᾶν Ἀπελλῆς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 5.26.5.}}
**2** **whole,** i. e. **safe and sound,** ὑγιὴς καὶ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77a.}}
**3** **entire, utter,** ὅ. ἁμάρτημα an **utter** blunder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.7}}; πλάσμα ὅ. ἐστὶν ἡ διαθήκη **utter** fiction, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.29.}}
**4** _neuter_ as _adverb_, ὅλον or τὸ ὅ. **wholly, entirely,** διαφέρει ὅ. τε καὶ πᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.109b; διαφέρει ὅ. καὶ τὸ πᾶν [prev. author] “Lg.” 944c; ὅλῳ καὶ παντί [prev. author] “Phd.” 79e; τῷ ὅ. καὶ παντὶ διοίσει [prev. author] “R.” 527c; τῷ παντὶ καὶ ὅ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 734e; εἰς τὸ ὅ. [prev. author] “Plt.” 302b: with a _preposition_, κατὰ ὅλον}} **on the whole, generally,** opposed to ἀπολαβὼν μέρος τι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 392d; so κατὰ ὅλου [prev. author] “Men.” 77a}}; δι᾽ ὅλου, καθ᾽ ὅλου (see. διόλου, καθόλου); αἱ κράσεις δι᾽ ὅλων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1078c, compare 1078d: in this signification also without a _preposition_, τὸ μὲν ὅ.}}**generally speaking,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 261a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.11}}; τὸ δ᾽ ὅ. and **in general, in short,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 33.16 (2nd c.BC)}}; οὐδὲ Φιλόξενον ὅλ᾽ ἐξ ὅλων εὗρον I have **entirely failed** to find P., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 936.20 (3rd c.AD).}}
**5** ={πᾶς}, **all,** ὅλων στρατηγός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1105, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 225, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 47.482, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.216 (Paul. Sil.), 7.679 (from Sophronius)}}; ὅλη πόλις **every** city, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.14.23}}; πρὸ τῶν ὅ. τὸ προσκύνημά σου ποιῶ before **all things,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PTeb. 418.4 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἀσπάζομαι.. πάντας τοὺς ἐν τῇ οἰκίᾳ ὅ. κατ᾽ ὄνομα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.404.15 (4th c.AD),compare “PIand.” 13.20 (4th c.AD).}}
**II** as _substantive_, τὸ ὅ. **the universe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 508a, “Ly.” 214b, etc.}}; differing from τὸ πᾶν, as implying a definite order, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1024a3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 204a}} {ὁλοσηρικοπράτης} (but as not including void, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.1.7); also ἡ τῶν ὅ. τάξις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.22.}}
**II.2** τὰ ὅ. **one's all,** τὰ ὅ. πεπρακέναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28}}; τοῖς ὅ. ἡττᾶσθαι lose **one's all,** be **utterly** ruined, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.64; in full, τοῖς ὅ. πράγμασιν ἐσφαλμένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.33.1, etc.}}; τοῖς ὅ., ={ὅλως}, **altogether,** Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.39}}: with _negative_, not **at all,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.135S., 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.274,304 J}}.; τοῖς ὅ. ἠφάνισαν **utterly** destroyed it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 152.14 (1st c.AD),compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.262 J}}.; κινδυνεύει τῷ ὅ. ἐξαρθῆναι there is a risk of its being **entirely** carried away, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 133.19 (1st c.AD).}}
**III** _adverb_ ὅλως (_Doric dialect_ οὔλως {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Pempelus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.25.52)}} **wholly, altogether,** ὅ. σοφόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568a; ἀλγοῦνθ᾽ ὅ. [prev. author] “Phlb.” 36a}}; ὅ. ψεύδεται he speaks **utter** falsehood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.31, etc.}}
**III.2** **on the whole, speaking generally, in short,** ὅ. δ᾽ οὐδεὶς ἔστιν ὅντιν᾽ οὐ πεφενάκικ᾽ ἐκεῖνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.7, compare 14, +others; διψῆν καὶ πεινῆν καὶ ὅ. τὰς ἐπιθυμίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 437b, compare “Cra.” 406a}}; τί οὖν κωλύει πάντα ἀφῃρῆσθαι καὶ ὅ. τὴν πολιτεία; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.3; ὅ. εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 202b19, etc.}}
**III.3** frequently with a _negative_ (first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 73, assuming variant), οὐχ ὅ. or ὅ. οὐ}} not **at all,** ὅ. μὴ διαλέγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.35; ὅ. οὔτ᾽ ἀφελὼν οὔτε προσθείς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.35; οὔτ᾽ ἐλεῶν οὔθ᾽ ὅ. ἄνθρωπον ἡγούμενος [prev. author] 21.101, compare 46; οὐδὲ εἷς ὅ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 65.9; μὴ ὄντος ὅ. τοῦ Σωκράτους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 13b19; μηδὲ ὅ. εἶναι τοὺς θεούς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 10.}}
**III.4** **actually, really,** καλῶς ποιήσεις ἐλθοῦσα.. πρὸς ἡμᾶς ἵνα ὅ. ἴδωμέν σε {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1676.31 (3rd c.AD)}}; so perhaps in {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Cor.5.1. (ὅλ (ϝ) ος from I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} ***sólwos,** cf. Sanskrit _sárvas_ 'whole', and perhaps Latin _salus_, **salvus.**)

3653:
** ὄλονθος**, ὁ,
(ἡ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.113)}}, **edible fruit of the wild fig,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 160.1, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Causis Plantarum” 5.1.8, Anonymus}} (ὁ φιλόσοφος) cited in {{LXX+5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 3.77f, LXX.Cant.2.13; ὄ. χειμερινοί 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ γυναικείης φύσιος” 33, +others}}
**2** **sterile summer fruit of the cultivated fig,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.133.}}
**3** **sterile male inflorescence of the caprifig,** breeding ψῆνες, which fertilize σῦκα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.193.}}

