**2** **whole,** i. e. **safe and sound,** ὑγιὴς καὶ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 6.12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 77a.}}
**3** **entire, utter,** ὅ. ἁμάρτημα an **utter** blunder, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.7}}; πλάσμα ὅ. ἐστὶν ἡ διαθήκη **utter** fiction, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 45.29.}}
**4** _neuter_ as _adverb_, ὅλον or τὸ ὅ. **wholly, entirely,** διαφέρει ὅ. τε καὶ πᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.109b; διαφέρει ὅ. καὶ τὸ πᾶν [prev. author] “Lg.” 944c; ὅλῳ καὶ παντί [prev. author] “Phd.” 79e; τῷ ὅ. καὶ παντὶ διοίσει [prev. author] “R.” 527c; τῷ παντὶ καὶ ὅ. [prev. author] “Lg.” 734e; εἰς τὸ ὅ. [prev. author] “Plt.” 302b: with a _preposition_, κατὰ ὅλον}} **on the whole, generally,** opposed to ἀπολαβὼν μέρος τι, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 392d; so κατὰ ὅλου [prev. author] “Men.” 77a}}; δι᾽ ὅλου, καθ᾽ ὅλου (see. διόλου, καθόλου); αἱ κράσεις δι᾽ ὅλων {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.1078c, compare 1078d: in this signification also without a _preposition_, τὸ μὲν ὅ.}}**generally speaking,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 261a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.11}}; τὸ δ᾽ ὅ. and **in general, in short,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|PTeb. 33.16 (2nd c.BC)}}; οὐδὲ Φιλόξενον ὅλ᾽ ἐξ ὅλων εὗρον I have **entirely failed** to find P., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 936.20 (3rd c.AD).}}
**5** ={πᾶς}, **all,** ὅλων στρατηγός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1105, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 225, 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 47.482, “Anthologia Graeca” 5.216 (Paul. Sil.), 7.679 (from Sophronius)}}; ὅλη πόλις **every** city, {{LXX|LXX.1Ki.14.23}}; πρὸ τῶν ὅ. τὸ προσκύνημά σου ποιῶ before **all things,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|PTeb. 418.4 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἀσπάζομαι.. πάντας τοὺς ἐν τῇ οἰκίᾳ ὅ. κατ᾽ ὄνομα {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PLond.” 2.404.15 (4th c.AD),compare “PIand.” 13.20 (4th c.AD).}}
**II** as _substantive_, τὸ ὅ. **the universe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 508a, “Ly.” 214b, etc.}}; differing from τὸ πᾶν, as implying a definite order, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1024a3, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 204a}} {ὁλοσηρικοπράτης} (but as not including void, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.1.7); also ἡ τῶν ὅ. τάξις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.22.}}
**II.2** τὰ ὅ. **one's all,** τὰ ὅ. πεπρακέναι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.28}}; τοῖς ὅ. ἡττᾶσθαι lose **one's all,** be **utterly** ruined, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] 9.64; in full, τοῖς ὅ. πράγμασιν ἐσφαλμένος 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.33.1, etc.}}; τοῖς ὅ., ={ὅλως}, **altogether,** Philipp. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.39}}: with _negative_, not **at all,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.135S., 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.274,304 J}}.; τοῖς ὅ. ἠφάνισαν **utterly** destroyed it, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 152.14 (1st c.AD),compare 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor 2.262 J}}.; κινδυνεύει τῷ ὅ. ἐξαρθῆναι there is a risk of its being **entirely** carried away, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 133.19 (1st c.AD).}}
**III** _adverb_ ὅλως (_Doric dialect_ οὔλως {{Refs 5th c.AD+|Pempelus Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.25.52)}} **wholly, altogether,** ὅ. σοφόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 568a; ἀλγοῦνθ᾽ ὅ. [prev. author] “Phlb.” 36a}}; ὅ. ψεύδεται he speaks **utter** falsehood, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 15.31, etc.}}
**III.2** **on the whole, speaking generally, in short,** ὅ. δ᾽ οὐδεὶς ἔστιν ὅντιν᾽ οὐ πεφενάκικ᾽ ἐκεῖνος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.7, compare 14, +others; διψῆν καὶ πεινῆν καὶ ὅ. τὰς ἐπιθυμίας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 437b, compare “Cra.” 406a}}; τί οὖν κωλύει πάντα ἀφῃρῆσθαι καὶ ὅ. τὴν πολιτεία; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.3; ὅ. εἰπεῖν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 202b19, etc.}}
**III.3** frequently with a _negative_ (first in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 73, assuming variant), οὐχ ὅ. or ὅ. οὐ}} not **at all,** ὅ. μὴ διαλέγεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.35; ὅ. οὔτ᾽ ἀφελὼν οὔτε προσθείς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.35; οὔτ᾽ ἐλεῶν οὔθ᾽ ὅ. ἄνθρωπον ἡγούμενος [prev. author] 21.101, compare 46; οὐδὲ εἷς ὅ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 65.9; μὴ ὄντος ὅ. τοῦ Σωκράτους 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 13b19; μηδὲ ὅ. εἶναι τοὺς θεούς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tim.” 10.}}
**III.4** **actually, really,** καλῶς ποιήσεις ἐλθοῦσα.. πρὸς ἡμᾶς ἵνα ὅ. ἴδωμέν σε {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1676.31 (3rd c.AD)}}; so perhaps in {{NT+5th c.BC+|NT.1Cor.5.1. (ὅλ (ϝ) ος from I.- 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus}} ***sólwos,** cf. Sanskrit _sárvas_ 'whole', and perhaps Latin _salus_, **salvus.**)

3655:
** ὄμβρος**, ὁ,
**A** **storm of rain, thunder-storm,** sent by Zeus, ὅτ᾽ ἐπιβρίσῃ Διὸς ὄ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.91; χειμάρρους.. ὀπαζόμενος Διὸς ὄμβρῳ 11.493; ὡς δ᾽ ὅτ᾽ ἂν ἀστράπτῃ πόσις Ἥρης.., τεύχων ἢ πολὺν ὄ. κτλ. 10.6}}; of a storm at sea, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus “Supp.” 26.4; ὄ. λάβρος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.12}}; distinct from ὑετός or common rain, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 394a31}}; but sometimes, **heavy rain,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.98, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 146, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 78}}: in _plural_, **rains,** ὄ. πολλοὶ καὶ λάβροι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.50, compare 2.25, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.81, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 350.}}
**A.2** generally, **water,** as an element, μήτε γῆ, μήτ᾽ ὄ. ἱερός, μήτε φῶς [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1428, compare 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 98.2, 21.5: _falsa lectio_ for{ὄλβος} in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 953; ὄ. ἀναγκαῖοι}}]**urine,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.443.}}
**A.3** **inundation,** τῶν παρακειμένων ὑδάτων {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 61 (b). 133 (2nd c.BC), +others}}; ὀχετοὺς ἀγαγεῖν οἳ ἄξουσιν τὸν ὄ. εἰς τὰς ἐξαγωγούς {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PCair.Zen.” 383.13 (3rd c.BC).}}
**A.II** _metaphorically_, **storm, shower,** ἐν πολυφθόρῳ Διὸς ὄ., of a battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).49; δέδοικα δ᾽ ὄμβρου κτύπον.. τὸν αἱματηρόν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1533 (Lyric poetry)}}; μέλας ὄ. χάλαζά θ᾽ αἱματοῦσσ᾽ (χαλάζης αἵματος codices) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1279; ὄμβρῳ δακρυόεντι 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 16.345; πυρὸς ὄμβροι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 3.22; ἡδὺς ὄ. ἀοιδῆς “Anthologia Graeca” 9.364 (from Nestor).}}

3656:
** ὁμῑλ-έω**,
_Aeolic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _present_ **ὀμίλλει** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Alcaeus Lyricus 61.29}} Lobel, **to be in company with, consort with,** μνηστῆρσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.21, +others, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 500c, etc.: with Preps., ἠὲ μετὰ Τρώεσσιν ὁμιλέοι ἦ μετ᾽ Ἀχαιοῖς 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.86, compare 834; ἐνὶ πρώτοισιν ὁ. 18.194, compare 535}}; πὰρ παύροισι.. ὁμιλεῖς **consortest** with few, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.383.}}
**2** _absolutely_, μηδ᾽ ἄλλοθ᾽ ὁμιλήσαντες **joining in company,** {{Refs|4.684}}; περὶ νεκρὸν ὁ. **throng** about the corpse, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.641, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.19.}}
**II** in hostile sense, **join battle with,** ὁμιλέομεν Δαναοῖσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.523, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.265; μετὰ τοῖσιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.502; σὺν Λαπίθαισί σε Κενταύρων ὁμιλῆσαι δορί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 792 (Lyric poetry)}}: _absolutely_, **join battle,** εὖτ᾽ ἂν πρῶτον ὁμιλήσωσι φάλαγγες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.158.}}
**III** of social intercourse, **hold converse with, be acquainted with, associate with,** τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.130; κακοῖς ἀνδράσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 753 (troch.)}}; ἀλλήλοις, μετ᾽ ἀλλήλων, πρὸς ἀλλήλους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 188d, “Plt.” 272c, “Lg.” 886c; τούτῳ τῷ τρόπῳ πρὸς τοὺς ἐρωμένους ὁ. [prev. author] “Phdr.” 252d}}; so of political intercourse, εἰθισμένος πρὸς ἡμᾶς ἀπὸ τοῦ ἴσου ὁ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77; ἡμῖν ἀπὸ τοῦ ἴσου ὁ. [prev. author] 3.11}}; of scholars, ὁ. τινί **frequent** a teacher's lectures, **be his pupil,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.15, 39}}; ὁ. τῇ Ὁμήρου ποιήσει **to be familiar** with it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Pr.Im.” 26}}; compare ὁμιλητής.
**III.2** **to be friends,** οἱ μάλιστά τινι ὁμιλέοντες {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.99.}}
**III.3** **speak to, address, harangue,** with _dative_, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.4.7: _absolutely_, ὑπερηφάνως ὁ. [prev. author] 16.34.6; πρὸς ἵππον 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 15.2; πρὸς ἀλλήλους NT.Luke.24.14}}: generally, **speak, converse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.116 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus; κατά τινα διάλεκτον 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 9.179; Ἑβραϊστί 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 11.5.6}}; ὁ. τινὶ περί τινος **talk** to.., {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 928.5 (2nd c.AD)}}:—_passive_, _perfect_ _participle_ ὡμιλημένος **used in conversation,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 2.27S.}}
**IV** of marriage or sexual intercourse, γυναιξὶ καὶ παρθένοις ὁ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.25; παιδικοῖς [prev. author] “Mem.” 2.1.24, etc.; σὺν τοῖς φιλτάτοις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 367, compare 1185; cf. Moer. p.276 P.}}
**V** of things or business which one has to do with, **attend to, busy oneself with,** ὁμιλεῖν ἀρχῇ, πολέμῳ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.55, 70; καινοῖς πράγμασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1399, compare ὁμιλία 1.4}}; φιλοσοφίᾳ, γυμναστικῇ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 496b, 410c; παιδείᾳ “OGI” 505.7 (from Aezani)}}; ἐμ Μούσαις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work]282.16 (5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus Mae., 3rd c.BC)}}; πονηροτάτοις σώμασιν ὁ., of a physician, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 408d}}; also like{χρῆσθαι}, **meet with, enjoy,** ὁ. τύχαις **to be in** good fortune, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 1.61; εὐτυχίᾳ ὁ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 354 (Lyric poetry)}}; but also,
**V.2** of the things themselves, πλαγίαις φρένεσσιν ὄλβος οὐ πάντα χρόνον ὁ. does not **consort with** a crooked mind, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 3.6, compare “P.” 7.6}}; κυλίκων νεῖμεν ἐμοὶ τέρψιν ὁμιλεῖν gave me their delight **to keep me company,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1201 (Lyric poetry); πλοῦτος καὶ δειλοῖσιν ἀνθρώπων ὁμιλεῖ 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 1.51, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 940: in physical sense, ὁ ὁ βραχίων τῷ κοίλῳ τῆς ὠμοπλάτης πλάγιος}} **fils** obliquely **into..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 1}}; of a plaster, **to be in contact,** ὁ. τῷ νοσέοντι μέρει {{Refs|[prev. author] “περὶ ἰητροῦ” 3.}}
**VI** **deal with** a man, **bear oneself towards** him, καλῶς ὁ. τινί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator “Epistulae” 4.9; πρός τινα [prev. author] 2.24; τῷ δήμῳ πρὸς χάριν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 35.3}} (so in _passive_, συνειθισμένοι ὑπὸ πάντων πρὸς χάριν ὁμιλεῖσθαι {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ παρρησίας” p.62 O.)}}; ταῦτα ἡ ἐμὴ νεότης.. ἐς τὴν Πελοποννησίων δύναμιν.. ὡμίλησε these were the achievements of my youth **in intercourse** with their power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.17.}}
**VII** of place, **come into, enter, visit,** with _dative_, διαβάντες τὸν Ἅλυν.. ὡμίλησαν τῇ Φρυγίῃ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.26, compare 214, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 7.8}}; βαρεῖα χώρᾳ τῇδ᾽ ὁ. heavily **will I visit** this land, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 720; ὁ. παρ᾽ οἰκείαις ἀρούραις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 12.19; ὁ. τοιᾷδε πόλει 5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 292; poetry also ὁ. ἄνθεσιν 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 47}}:—_passive_, τὰ ὁμιλούμενα τῶν χωρίων **most frequented,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 1.16.}}
**VIII** ἐκτὸς ὁμιλεῖ (i.e. τῶν ξυντρόφων ὀργῶν) he **wanders** from his senses, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 640 (Lyric poetry).}}

3657:
** ὁμῑλ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **intercourse, company,** ἔσθ᾽ ὁμιλίας κακῆς κάκιον οὐδέν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 599; τὸ ξυγγενές τοι δεινὸν ἡ θ᾽ ὁ. [prev. author] “Pr.” 39, etc.}}; ὁ. τινός **communion** or **intercourse with** one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.174; πρός τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 70, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 203a, +others}}; τοὺς ἀξίους δὲ τῆς ἐμῆς ὁ. of my **society,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 776; ἡ σὴ ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 283d}}; ὁ. χθονός **intercourse with** a country, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1408}}; ἔχειν ἐν θεοῖς ὁ. live among them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IA” [1622]; ἥκειν εἰς ὁ. τινί 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1489}}; ἡ καθ᾽ ὑμᾶς αὐτοὺς πολιτεία καὶ ὁ. public and **private life,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.68}}; ἐξ ὁμιλίας by **persuasion,** opposed to βίᾳ, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator “Epistulae” 1.12}}: also in _plural_, ἀνθρώπων κακῶν-ίαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.α᾽,compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 33.14, etc.; φθείρουσιν ἤθη χρήσθ᾽ ὁ. κακαί 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1024 (= 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 218)}}; Ἑλληνικαὶ ὁ. **association** with Greeks, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.77; ἐνδίκοις ὁ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 966 (Lyric poetry)}}; αἱ.. συγγενεῖς ὁ. **intercourse** with kinsfolk, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 51; ὁ. κακαῖς χρῆσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 550b; αἱ τῶν ἀνθρώπων ὁ. καὶ αἱ τῶν πραγμάτων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1336b32, etc.}}
**2** **sexual intercourse,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.182, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 8.22, “Mem.” 3.11.14, etc.; νυμφικαὶ ὁ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1400; ὁ. τῶν ἀφροδισίων 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 582a26}}; ἡ πρὸς τοὺς ἄρρενας or τῶν ἀρρένων ὁ., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1272a24, 1269b29.}}
**3** **instruction,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.6 and 15}}; **lecture,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 3.19}}: in _plural_, title of work by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).656.}}
**4** ὁμιλέειν ὁμιλίῃ to be versed in it by **practice,** opposed to λόγῳ εἰδέναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 10.}}
**5** ἡ πλείστη ὁ. τοῦ ὀνόματος its commonest **usage,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Epicurus Philosophus “Epistulae” 1p.22U.}}; so ὁμιλίαι φωνῆς, αἱ τῶν λέξεων ὁ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.288 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Oec.” p.59 J}}.; αἱ κοιναὶ ὁ. **common** **usage,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.1}}; τῶν ἰδιωτῶν -ίαι{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|[prev. work] 64; ἡ ἀνὰ χεῖρα -ία 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 37.2; ἡ κοινὴ καλουμένη καὶ ἀνὰ χεῖρα -ία 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 2.7.}}
**II** **association, company,** ἀνδρῶν τῶν ἀρίστων ἐπιλέξαντες ὁμιλίην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.81, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 57.}}
**II.2** in _collective_ sense, τήνδ᾽ ὁμιλίαν χθονός these **fellow-sojourners** in the land, {{Refs|[prev. work] 406}}; ναὸς κοινόπλους ὁ. ship-**mates,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 872; ἀδελφῶν ἡ παροῦσ᾽ ὁ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 581, compare “Hipp.” 19 (uncertain reading).}}

3658:
** ὅμῑλ-ος**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **ὄμιλλος** {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 658.55}}: ὁ: (ὁμός, ἴλη) **any assembled crowd, throng of people,** for a feast, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.225}}; for a spectacle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.603, 23.651, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 9.123, +others, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 123, +others, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 100, +others, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}: rare in _Attic dialect_ Comedy texts and Prose, as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 323, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65, 4.112}}; especially **the mass of the people, the crowd,** opposed to the chiefs, προπάροιθεν ὁμίλου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.22; ὅ. Δαναῶν, Τρώων, etc., 19.402, 4.86, +others}}; ἵππων καὶ ἀνδρῶν ὅ. {{Refs|10.338}}; τὸν ψιλὸν ὅ. the **crowd** of irregulars, opposed to ὁπλῖται, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.125}}; **mob,** διδασκάλῳ χρείωνται ὁμίλῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 104; ὁ πολλὸς ὅ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.88, compare 3.81; τυφλὸν δ᾽ ἔχει ἦτορ ὅ. ἀνδρῶν ὁ πλεῖστος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.24}}; but also ὅ. πολλὸς μὲν Ἕλλην περιοικέει a large Hellenic **population,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.23.}}
**b** of inanimate objects, [σῆμα] οὔ τι μεμιγμένον ἐστὶν ὁμίλῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.196.}}
**2** **throng** of battle, τὴν ἔξαγ᾽ ὁμίλου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.353, compare 4.516}}, etc.; πρώτῳ ἐν ὁ. in the forefront of **battle,** {{Refs|17.471: generally}}**tumult, confusion,** βοῇ καὶ ὁμίλῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.59; σοφίῃ καὶ μὴ βίῃ καὶ ὁ. [prev. author] 3.127.}} —The word seems not to be used in _plural_

3659:
** ὄμμα**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **ὄππα** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.11}}: τό:— **eye,** _poetry_ word, rare in Prose {{Refs 8th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 45c, +others, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.26, “Mem.” 1.4.6, +others, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Sensu” 50, +others, 3rd c.BC: Polystratus Epicureus “Herc.” 346p.81V., “BGU” 713.9 (1st c.AD), “IG” 42(1).121.121 (Epid.)): 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus}} only use _plural_, κατὰ χθονὸς ὄμματα πήξας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.217; ὕπνον ἐπ᾽ ὄμμασι χεῦε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.492, etc.: _sing._ in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.63 and Trag. (see. below):}} —Phrases: ὀρθοῖς ὄμμασιν ὁρᾶν τινα look **straight** at, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1385; ἀναβλέψαι ὀρθ. ὄμμ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.30; ἐξ ὀμμάτων ὀρθῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 528}}; also οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὄμμασιν ποίοις βλέπων πατέρα ποτ᾽ ἂν προσεῖδον how I could have looked him in the face,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1371, compare 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.121; ὁρᾶν τινα ἐν ὄμμασι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 241}}; ποῖον ὄ. πατρὶ δηλώσ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 462}}; τέοισί με χρὴ ὄμμασι.. φαίνεσθα; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.37}}; λαμπρὸς ὄμματι radiant in **look** or **expression,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 81; ἄλλοσ᾽ ὄ. θἀτέρᾳ δὲ νοῦν ἔχειν [prev. author] “Tr.” 272}}; προσέσχον ὄ. turned their **eyes** on him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 931}}; ἐς σὸν ἐλθεῖν ὄ. come within **sight** of thee, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 887}}; κατ᾽ ὄμματα before **one's eyes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 760}}; κατ᾽ ὄμμα ἐλθεῖν face to face, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1064}}; κατ᾽ ὄμμα στῆναι **in full sight, openly,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 1117}}; opposed to νύκτωρ, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ba.” 469}}; κρατιστεύων κατ᾽ ὄμμα in **eye-sight,** of the Sun, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 102 (Lyric poetry) (but λαμπρὰ καὶ κατ᾽ ὄμμα καὶ φύσιν is uncertain in 379)}}; πρευμενοῦς ἀπ᾽ ὄμματος ἰδέσθαι look kindly on, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 210; πεύθομαι δ᾽ ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων νόστον [prev. author] “Ag.” 988 (Lyric poetry)}}; ὡς ἀπ᾽ ὀμμάτων to judge by **the eye,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 15, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 216}}; ἐν ὄμμασι before one's **eyes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 604; ἐν τοῖς ὄ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.11; ἐπ᾽ ὀμμάτων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1153 (Lyric poetry); so παρ᾽ ὄμμα, εἰ δ᾽ ἦν παρ᾽ ὄμμα θάνατος [prev. work] 484}}; ἐξ ὀμμάτων out of **sight,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “IA” 743; ἄπειμ᾽ ἐξ ὀ. 4th-5th c.BC: Phrynichus Tragicus 21}}; πρὸ ὀμμάτων τίθεσθαι, ποιεῖν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1455a23, “Rh.” 1386a34}}; πρὸ ὀ. θέσις Polystr. [prev. cited]
**2** _metaphorically_, τὸ τῆς ψυχῆς ὄ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 533d, 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “Protrepticus” 21.κδ᾽.}}
**II** **the eye of heaven,** i.e. **the sun,** ὄ. αἰθέρος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 285, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 194 (_anapaest meter_)}}; but ὄ. νυκτός is a periphrasis for **night** (see. below see), ἕως.. νυκτὸς ὄμμ᾽ ἀφείλετο (i.e. τὴν μάχην) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 428}}; ὅταν δὲ νυκτὸς ὄ. λυγαίας μόλῃ the dark night, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 110; νυκτὸς ὄ. τῆς μελαμπέπλου 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 89; compare ὀφθαλμός 111, βλέφαρον 11.}}
**III** generally, **light:** hence, _metaphorically_, **that which brings light,** ὄμμα ξείνοισι **a light** to strangers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.56; ὄ. δόμων νομίζω δεσπότου παρουσίαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 169; ἄελπτον ὄμμ᾽ ἐμοὶ φήμης ἀνασχὸν τῆσδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 203.}}
**III.2** _metaphorically_, **anything dear** or **precious, as the apple of an eye,** ὄ. γὰρ πάσης χθονὸς.. ἐξίκοιτ᾽ ἄν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 1025.}}
**IV** **face** or human **form,** ὦ δυσθέατον ὄ. [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1004; ἐμπαίει τί μοι ψυχῇ ξύνηθες ὄ. [prev. author] “El.” 903; τὸ ἐρωτικὸν ὄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 253e: as periphrastic of the person, ὄ. πελείας, ={πελεία}, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 140 (_anapaest meter_)}}]; ὄ. νύμφας, ={νύμφα}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 527 (Lyric poetry)}}; ξύναιμον ὄ., ={ξυναίμων}, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Aj.” 977}}; ὦ ταυρόμορφον ὄ. Κηφισοῦ, ={ὦ ταυρόμορφε Κηφισέ}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1261; see above 11 and compare ὄνομα IV.}}
**V** ὄ. τυκτόν **eye-hole** in a helmet, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 22.62.}}

3660:
** ὄμνῡμι**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.166, etc.}}; _imperative_ ὄμνῠθι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.585, ὄμνῡ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1185, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 746, cf. Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86. γ}}; _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὀμνύτω {{Refs|“IG” 12.134.5}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὀμνύντων. Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47, “IG” 12.87.24}}: _imperfect_ ὤμνυν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 520, “Ec.” 823, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.10, etc.}}: also (from _present_ ὀμνύω) _3rd.pers._ _singular_ _imperative_ ὀμνῠέτω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.175}}; _participle_ ὀμνύουσα {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 2}}: _imperfect_ ὤμνῠον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.278, Foed. cited in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.19, 24, “IG” 22.236.14, etc.}} (for _present_ _indicative_ the Trag. and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus use only ὄμνυμι, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and _Attic dialect_ Prose writers also ὀμνύω}} which also occurs in Comedy texts, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.9, 4th c.BC: Amphis Comicus 42, 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 101, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 241.1, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 160; in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.153 ὀμνύντες is restored for the uncertain form ὀμοῦντες)}}: _future_ ὀμοῦμαι, εῖ, εῖται, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.233, 9.274, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 194, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 246, “Lys.” 193, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.3.11, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _1st pers._ _plural_ ὀμιώμεθα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 183}}; later _future_ ὀμόσω {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.201 (2nd c.AD: Strato Epigrammaticus), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 23, etc.}}: _aorist_ ὤμοσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.253, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ὤμοσσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.313; _Epic dialect_ also without augment ὄμοσα, -οσσα, 19.113, 10.328}}: _perfect_ ὀμώμοκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 612, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1471, etc.}}: _pluperfect_ ὠμωμόκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.35, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.16, 19.318}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 10.26.3}}, _aorist_ _participle_ ὀμοσάμενος {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 531.28 (Dyme, 3rd c.BC), “IG” 5(2).357.11 (from Stymphalus), also in compounds ἀντ-, ἀπ-, δι-, ὑπ-}}:—_passive_, _future_ ὀμοσθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.34}}: _aorist_ ὠμόσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.10, (ὑπ-) 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “Fragmenta” 202; but ὠμόθην 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 2.40, (ὑπ-) 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 48.25}}: _perfect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὀμώμοται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1284, ὀμώμοσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 816, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1377a11}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὀμώμονται Legal cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.98}}; _participle_ ὀμωμοσμένος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.10, 22.4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1377b7 (ὠμοσμένος see reading in 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Αιβυκή” 83, etc.)}}:—**swear,** with _accusative_ _cognate_, ὀμνυέτω δέ τοι ὅρκον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.175, +others; ὅτις κ᾽ ἐπίορκον ὀμόσσῃ 3.279; ἑκὼν ἐπίορκον ὀμόσσας 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 282: with _dative_ _person_, νῦν μοι ὄμοσσον.. ὅρκον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.108, +others; πρός τινα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.331, 19.288}}:—_passive_, ὀμώμοται γὰρ ὅοκος ἐκ θεῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1284; ὅρκων ὀμωμος μένων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 7.10; εἰ ὀμώμοσται οὗτος [ὁ ὅρκος] 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1377a11, compare b7.}}
**II** **swear to** a thing, **affirm** or **confirm by oath,**
**II.1** followed by _accusative_, ταῦτα δ᾽ ἐγὼν ἐθέλω ὀμόσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.187, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1145, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Agesilaus” 1.11}}; ὄ. τὰς σπονδάς Foed. cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47; τὴν εἰρήνην 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.32, compare 9.16; θεῶν πίστεις τινί 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.30, etc.}}
**II.2** followed by _future_ _infinitive_, **swear that one will..,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.373, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 623, 941}} (_present_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.188 codices): frequently with ἦ μέν, _Attic dialect_ ἦ μήν}} preceding the _infinitive_, καί μοι ὄμοσσον ἦ μέν μοι.. ἀρήξειν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.76, compare 10.321, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.26, etc.: also by}}_aorist_ _infinitive_ and ἄν, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 7.7.40}}: by _present_ _infinitive_, **swear that one does..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 357}}: by _perfect_ _infinitive_, **swear that one has..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.119; ὤμνυς μὴ γεγονέναι 5th c.BC: Magnes Comicus 6}}: by _aorist_ _infinitive_, **swear that one did..,** ὀμνύουσι μὴ 'κπιεῖν ἀλλ᾽ ἢ μίαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 143.9, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.179}}(_aorist_ _infinitive_ is perhaps used, without ἄν, in _future_ sense, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.170 (assuming variant))}}: sometimes a clause follows in the _indicative_, ὀμνύω.., ἦ μὴν ἐγὼ ἐθυόμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.31; ὄμνυμί σοι.., οὐκ ἤθελον.. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Adon.” 22.}}
**II.3** _absolutely_, εἶπον ὀμόσας ἄν I would have **given my word of honour,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 215d.}}
**III** with _accusative_ of the person or thing sworn by, **swear by,** νῦν μοι ὄμοσσον ἀάατον Στυγὸς ὕδωρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.271; γαιήοχον ἐννοσίγαιον ὄμνυθι 23.585, compare 15.40, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.7, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 529, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1185, etc.; ὀμωμοκὼς τοὺς θεούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.217}}; ὄμνυμι θεοὺς καὶ θεάς followed by _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.17, compare 6.1.31: rarely with _dative_, τῷ γὰρ ὄμνυτ᾽}}; ἢ σιδαρέοισ; {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 248: in Prose also with Preps., ὀ. καθ᾽ ἱερῶν τελείων Legal cited in 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.97, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.47; κατ᾽ ἐξωλείας 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.119; κατὰ τῆς Πολιάδος 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 32; εἰς τὸν Οὐιτέλλιον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Otho” 18; ἐπὶ τῶν ἱερῶν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 38.20.5; ἐν Κυρίῳ LXX.Judg.21.7; ἐν τῷ ναῷ NT.Matt.23.16:—}}_passive_, ὀμώμοσται Ζεύς Zeus **has been sworn by, adjured,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 816, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1241.}}

3662:
** ὁμοι-άζω**,
(ὅμοιος) **to be like,** interpolated in {{NT|NT.Mark.14.70, variant in NT.Matt.23.27.}}
**II** _transitive_, **compare, liken,** {{Refs|Diom. p.365K.}}

3664:
** ὅμοιος**
or (as in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, _Ionic dialect_, and old _Attic dialect_)}} **ὁμοῖος**, α, ον (compare ἐρῆμος, ἑτοῖμος): later _Epic dialect_ also **ὁμοίιος** (B, which see); _Aeolic dialect_ **ὔμοιος** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.20}} (_adverb_ **-ως**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|“IG” 12(2).69a6); 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus}} **ὑμοῖος** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 665 A 15 (Orchom., 4th c.BC)}}: (ὁμός):—**like, resembling,** ὡς αἰεὶ τὸν ὁ. ἄγει θεὸς ὡς τὸν ὁ. 'birds of a feather flock together', {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.218; ὁ ὅ. τῷ ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 510b; ὁ ὅ. ὡς τὸν ὅ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1155a34; τὸ ὅ. τῷ ὁ. φίλον [prev. work] 1165b17, see below 6}}; ὡς ἐπὶ τῶν ὁ. as in **similar** cases (of persons), {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 79.18 (2nd c.AD)}}, etc.: _comparative_ **-ότερος** **more like,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 79b}}: _superlative_ **-ότατος** **most like,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.92, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 833 (Lyric poetry), etc.}}
**2** **the same,** ἄμφω γὰρ πέπρωται ὁμοίην γαῖαν ἐρεῦσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.329; χρὼς οὐκέθ᾽ ὁ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.182}}; hence (i.e. ἑαυτῷ), **always the same, unchanging,** αἰεὶ πόδας καὶ χεῖρας ὁμοῖοι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 114; ὅ. τὴν γνώμην}}**the same as ever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.76; γνῶμαι πρὸς τοὺς αὐτοὺς κινδύνους ὁμοῖαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.89; ἀεὶ ὅ. εἶ, ὦ Ἀπολλόδωρε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 173d}}; ἓν καὶ ὅ. one and **the same,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 271a.}}
**3** **equal in force, a match for one,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.632, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.96.}}
**4** of things, **suiting, according with,** πολλά τε καὶ ὅ. ἑαυταῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “R.” 549e; ὅ. τῇ φύσει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 167.}}
**5** ὁμοῖον ἡμῖν ἔσται it will be **all the same, all one** to us, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.80; σὺ δ᾽ αἰνεῖν εἴτε με ψέγειν θέλεις, ὁμοῖον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1404, compare 1239, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 1069; ἐν τῷ ὁ. καθειστήκει 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.49.}}
**6** τὸ ὅ. ἀνταποδιδόναι give **'tit for tat',** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.18}}; so τὴν ὁμοίην (i.e. δίκην, χάριν) ἀποδιδόναι τινί {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.119, 6.21, 62}}; τὴν ὁ. φέρεσθαι παρά τινος to have **a like return** made one, [{{Refs|[prev. passage]; ἐπ᾽ ἴσῃ καὶ ὁμοίᾳ, see at {ἴσος} II.2.}}]
**7** ἐν ὁμοίῳ ποιεῖσθαί τι hold a thing **in like esteem,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 7.138, 8.109.}}
**8**to be uniform, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 261b25: proverbial, οὐδ᾽ ὅκου χώρης οἱ μῦς ὁ. τὸν σίδηρον τρώγουσιν}}**like any other food,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 3.76}} (but perhaps **all alike,**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1296 codex R, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.15 (assuming variant), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.763c)}}: _comparative_ **-ότερον**{{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 11.233 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus)}}: _superlative_ **-ότατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 281.}}

3666:
** ὁμοι-όω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 393c}}: _aorist_ ὡμοίωσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 33, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.8}}:— _middle_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (see. below)}}:—mostly in _passive_, _future_ ὁμοιωθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 964d}}, or in _middle_ form ὁμοιώσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158}}: _aorist_ ὡμοιώθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.103, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 510a, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.114, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ _infinitive_ ὁμοιωθήμεναι (see. below): _perfect_ ὡμοίωμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 431e}}:—**make like,** ὁμοιώσασ᾽ ἐμοὶ εἴδωλον ἔμπνουν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 33; πᾶν παντὶ ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 261e; ἑαυτὸν ἄλλῳ [prev. author] “R.” 393c; χοῦν.. -ώσαντες τῷ ἄλλῳ χώρῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.28; τοῖς πεπλασμένοις καὶ τοῖς γεγραμμένοις τὴν τοῦ σώματος φύσιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.75; ἑαυτῷ τι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 324a10}}; πρὸς τὰ παρόντα τὰς ὀργὰς τῶν πολλῶν ὁ. **makes** them **correspond** to prevailing conditions, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.82}}:—_passive_, **to be made like, become like,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} only in _aorist_ _infinitive_ _passive_, ὁμοιωθήμεναι ἄντην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.187, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.120; ὁμοιωθέντ᾽ Ἀφροδίτῃ 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 22.5; ὀργὰς πρέπει θεοὺς οὐχ ὁμοιοῦσθαι βροτοῖς 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 1348, compare “Med.” 890; ἐς τὴν εὐβουλίαν.. ἄλλοις ὁ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.97, compare 5.103, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.158; κατὰ τὸ ἦθος ὁ. τοῖς ἐκείνου βουλήμασιν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.114.}}
**2** **liken, compare,** in _middle_, τὰς πάθας τὰς Κύρου τῇσι ἑωυτοῦ -ούμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.123:}} —later in _active_, τίνι ὁμοιώσω τὴν γενεὰν ταύτη; {{NT+4th c.AD+|NT.Matt.11.16; ἡ πόλις τινὰ Λυκούργῳ κατὰ τὸ ἦθος καὶ τὴν πρᾶξιν ὁμοιοῦσα “BSA” 29.35 (Sparta, 4th c.AD).}}
**II** _intransitive_, **to be like,** interpolated in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.45.}}

