3669:
** ὁμοί-ωσις**, εως, ἡ,
**a being made like,** θεῷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 176b.}}
**2** **likeness, resemblance,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Lapidibus” 41}}; opposed to ἀλλοίωσις, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 454c; καθ᾽ ὁμοίωσιν θεοῦ NT.Jam.3.9, cf. LXX.Gen.1.26.}}
**3** **establishment of a resemblance,** τῆς ὁ. ἕνεκεν αὐτῆς λαμβάνεσθαι τὰς μεταφοράς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Rh.” 1.177 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus}}
**3.b** Grammars, **comparison,** -ώσεως ἐπιρρήματα {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 642.6.}}
**4** **simile,** Ps.-{{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Vitae Homeri” 84, +others}}
**5** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **nine,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 57.}}

3670:
** ὁμολογ-έω**,
{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 980 (but very rare in Poets), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus and _Attic dialect_ (see. below)}}: _future_
**A** **-ήσω**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.144, etc.}}: _aorist_ ὡμολόγησα {{Refs|[prev. author] 9.88, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὡμολόγηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.29, etc.}}:—_middle_, _present_ and _aorist_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (see. below)}}:— _passive_, _future_ **-ήσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τέχνης” 4 (variant), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 171b}}: _aorist_ ὡμολογήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.29, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὡμολόγημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus (see. below)}}, etc.:—**to be** ὁμόλογος: hence,
**A.I** **agree with, say the same thing as,** with _dative_, λέγουσι Κορίνθιοι, ὁμολογέουσι δέ σφι Λέσβιοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.23, compare 171, 2.4}}; Κυρηναῖοι τὰ περὶ B άττον οὐδαμῶς -έουσι Θηραίοισι {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.154.}}
**A.II** **correspond, agree with,** whether of persons or things, with _dative_, [τὸ ἓν] ἑωυτῷ ὁ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 51; ὁμολογέουσι ταῦτα τοῖσι Ὀρφικοῖσι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.81; αὗται αἱ πόλιες τῇσι πρότερον λεχθείσῃσι ὁμολογέουσι κατὰ γλῶσσαν οὐδέν [prev. author] 1.142, compare 2.18}}; **have to do with,** ὁμολογέοντας κατ᾽ οἰκηϊότητα Περσίϊ οὐδέν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.54; τοῖς λόγοις τοὺς μάρτυρας -οῦντας 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.31; οὐκ ἔφη τοὺς λόγους τοῖς ἔργοις ὁμολογεῖν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.55}}; ὥστε μηδὲν ὁμολογεῖν τὼ τρόπω τὼ ἀλλήλων are utterly unlike, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 20.12.}}
**A.II.b** **to be coordinated,** πρὸς ἓν ἔργον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 1.8}}: _metaphorically_, of a vowel, **agree,** i. e. form a diphthong, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.737f.}}
**A.II.c** **to be suitable for,** with _genitive_, ὁμολογεῖ ὤμου ἡ περὶ τὴν ἑτέρην μασχάλην περιβολή {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “κατ᾽ ἰητρεῖον” 9.}}
**A.II.2** **agree to** a thing, **grant, concede,** ὁμολογῶ τάδε S. [prev. cited]: _absolutely_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.94; τινί τι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 195b; αὐτοῖς ὡμολογηκὼς ταύτην τὴν ὁμολογίαν [prev. author] “Cri.” 52a}}; ὁμολογοῖεν ἂν ἡμῖν οἱ ἄνθρωποι, ἢ ο; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 357a}}; ὅπως.. τῇ τύχῃ σου χάριτας ὁμολογεῖν δυνηθῶ that I may **avow** my gratitude.., {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PRyl.” 114.32 (3rd c.AD)}}; ὁ. χάριν θεοῖς **acknowledge** gratitude, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Laps.” 15 (ὁ. ἔν τινι NT.Matt.10.32}} appears to be an Aramaism; ὁ. ἐφ᾽ ἁμαρτίαις {{LXX|LXX.Sir.4.26):}} without _accusative_ _of things_, ὁμολογῶ σοι I **grant** you, i.e. {{Refs|1}}**admit** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 94}}; parenthetically, ἀφειλόμην, ὁμολογῶ I **allow** it, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.6.17: with}}_infinitive_, ὁ. Νικίαν ἑορακέναι **allows, confesses that** he has seen.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 181.3}}; ὁ. σε ἀδικεῖν I **confess** that I am wronging thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 265; ὁ. κλέπτειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 296, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 2.4.8; ὁ. καπηλεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.1}}; ὁ. οὐκ εἰδέναι **confess** ignorance, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Sophistici Elenchi” 183b8; ὁ. πατάξαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1422; ὁμολογοῦσι νοσεῖν μᾶλλον ἢ σωφρονεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 231d; ὁ. ἓν πάντα εἶναι 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 50; ὁ. Μειδίαν ἁπάντων λαμπρότατον γεγενῆσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.153}}, compare 197; especially in receipts, ὁ. ἀπέχειν, ἐσχηκέναι, etc., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.97.5 (3rd c.BC), “CPR” 229.3 (3rd c.AD)}}, etc.; in contracts, ὁ. διαλελύσθαι πρὸς ἀλλήλους {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PHib.” 1.96.5 (3rd c.BC),compare “BGU” 1160.3 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.; τοῦθ᾽ ὁ. ὡς.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 163a, compare “Lg.” 896c: also with Partic., ὁ. τινὰ δίκαια ὄντα [prev. author] “Cri.” 49e, 50a}}; see below with
**A.II.3** **agree** or **promise to** do, with _future_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.23, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.62, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174a, “Phdr.” 254b, etc.}}: with _aorist_ _infinitive_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 42.12}}: with _present_ _infinitive_, ὡμολόγησαν ἑκατὸν τάλαντα ἐκτείσαντες ἀζήμιοι εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.92}}: also frequently _absolutely_, **promise,** μισθῷ ὁμολογήσαντες (i.e. ἀπαλλάξεσθαι) {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.86}}; ὁ ὁμολογῶν the person **who gives an undertaking**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“BGU” 297.22 (1st c.AD)}}, etc.; **make an agreement, come to terms,** τινι with another, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.33, 7.172, +others}}; ἐπὶ τούτοισι on these terms, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.60, compare 8.140.β᾽, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.69.}}
**A.II.3.b** with _accusative_, **promise,** τῆς ἐπαγγελίας ἧς (for ἣν) ὡμολόγησεν ὁ θεὸς τῷ Ἀβραάμ {{NT|NT.Act.7.17; θεῷ ὑψίστῳ εὐχὴν Αὐρήλιος Ἀσκλάπων, ἣν ὡμολόγησεν ἐν Ῥώμῃ “IGRom.” 4.542 (from Phrygia).}}
**B** _middle_, in sense of _active_, ὑπεναντίος ὁ τρόπος.. ὁμολογεόμενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 1.11; αὐτοὶ ἑαυτοῖς ὁμολογούμενοι λόγοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 29c; νόμοι σφίσιν αὐτοῖς ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 2.17, compare 6.14; τὸ ταὐτὸν καὶ ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 741a; ὡμολογεῖτ᾽ ἂν ἡ κατηγορία τοῖς ἔργοις αὐτοῦ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.14; -ούμενος καὶ σύμφωνος κατὰ τὸν βίον 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 31.25.8; τοῦτο -ήσασθαι ὅτι.. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 439b, etc.}}
**C** _passive_, **to be agreed upon, allowed** or **granted by common consent,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.9, etc.; πλέον ἀνδρὶ ἑκάστῳ ἢ τρεῖς ὀβολοὶ ὡμολογήθησαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.29}}: with _infinitive_, with predicate added, **to be allowed** or **confessed to be** so and so, ἡ ὑπὸ πάντων ὁμολογουμένη ἄριστον εἶναι εἰρήνη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.62; ὁμολογεῖταί γε παρὰ πάντων μέγας θεὸς εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 202b, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.20, etc.}}
**C.2** without _infinitive_, ἡ τοῦ οἰκείου.. ἕξις.. δικαιοσύνη ἂν ὁμολογοῖτο **should be allowed** [to be] justice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 434a; -ούμενοι δοῦλοι 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.17}}; τοὺς -ουμένους θεούς those who are **admitted** [to be] gods, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Timocles Comicus 1.2, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.89.}}
**C.3** _absolutely_, ὁμολογεῖται **it is granted, agreed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 72a, +others}}; τὰ ὡμολογημένα **the things granted,** {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; ἐξ ὁμολογουμένου, ={ὁμολογουμένως}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.111.7.}}

3671:
** ὁμολογ-ία**,
_Ionic dialect_ -ιη, ἡ, **agreement,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 187b, +others}}; αἱ τῶν ὀνομάτων ὁ. verbal **consistency,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 164c.}}
**2** **assent, admission, concession,** τῶν ἐπικαλουμένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 11.44, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 461b, +others}}; κατὰ τὴν ἐμὴν ὁ. by my **admission,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 350e; ἡ ὑπέρ τινος ὁ. [prev. author] “Tht.” 169e}}; ἐξ ὁ. διαλέγεσθαι argue from **premises agreed upon** or **granted,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 110a33.}}
**3** **agreement, compact,** συνθήκη καὶ ὁ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 384d; ἐμμένειν τῇ ὁ. [prev. author] “Tht.” 145c, “Lg.” 840e: _plural_, ὁμολογίας παραβαίνειν, διαλύειν, [prev. author] “Tht.” 183d, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 4.175; τὰς ὁ. διαφυλάττειν [prev. author] 9.44; κατὰ τὰς ὁ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 443a; παρὰ τὰς ὁ. [prev. author] “Cri.” 52d.}}
**3.b** especially in war, **terms of peace, truce,** or **surrender,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.52}}; ὁμολογίῃ ἐχρήσαντο, of the conquered, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.150, compare 4.201; ἐς ὁμολογίην προσεχώρησαν [prev. author] 7.156; τὴν ὁ. δέξασθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.10}}; ἐς ὁμολογίην προκαλέεσθαι, of the conquerors, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.13; ἡ ὁ. ἡ πρός τινα γενομένη [prev. author] 1.61, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.120; ὁμολογίᾳ τὴν ἀκρόπολιν παραδοῦναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.90, compare 1.107.}}
**3.c** in Law, **contract, agreement,** συγγραφὴ καὶ ὁ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PEleph.” 2.2 (3rd c.BC)}}; ὁ. τινὸς πρός τινα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFay.” 91.1 (1st c.AD), etc.}}
**4** **vow,** {{LXX|LXX.Jer.51(44).25 (_plural_).}}
**5** in Stoic Philos., **conformity with nature, summum.. bonum, cum positum sit in eo quod** ὁμολογίαν **Stoici, nos appellemus convenientiam,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “de Finibus” 3.6.21; ψυχὴ πεποιημένη πρὸς ὁμολογίαν παντὸς τοῦ βίου “Stoicorum Veterum Fragmenta” 3.11.}}

3672:
** ὁμολογ-ουμένως**,
_adverb_, with _dative_, **conformably with,** τοῖς εἰρημένοις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 27: _absolutely_, [prev. author] “Oec.” 1.11.}}
**b** in Stoic Philos., ὁ. ζῆν, with or without τῇ φύσει, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Zeno Citieus Stoicus 1.45, Cleanth.[prev. work]125, Chrysipp. [prev. work]3.4.}}
**2** **by common consent, confessedly, admittedly,** ὁ. μαχιμώτατοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.90, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.140, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 186b, 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 6, etc.}}; ὁ. ἀγαθοί, ὁ. ἄριστοι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 186b, “Mx.” 243c; ἡ ὁ. ἰητρική 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 5.}}

3673:
** ὁμό-τεχνος**, ον,
**practising the same art,** τινι with one, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 187a.}}
**II** as _substantive_, **fellow-workman,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.89, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 328a, 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 7.15; ὁ ὁ. τινός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 171c, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.58, 5th c.AD: Aristaenetus Rhetor 1.19; οὐδεὶς τῶν ὁ. μου 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 173.7}}: as title applied to the good physician, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 7.}}
**II.2** _neuter_ ὁμότεχνον, τό, **guild,** τῶν λαναρίων{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Keil-Premerstein “Zweiter Bericht” No. 217 (2nd c.AD).}}

3674:
** ὁμοῦ**
(_Aeolic dialect_ ὔμοι {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 23.13)}}, _adverb_,
**I** properly of Place, **at the same place, together,** μὴ ἐμὰ σῶν ἀπάνευθε τιθήμεναι ὀστέ᾽, Ἀχιλλεῦ, ἀλλ᾽ ὁ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.84}}; ὁ. εἶναι, opposed to χωρὶς εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.1.7, etc.; ὁ. πάντες ἀναμεμιγμένοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 715, etc.}}
**I.2** generally, **together, at once,** ὁ. δ᾽ ἔχον ὠκέας ἵππους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.127; ἄμφω ὁ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.424; παρῆν ὁ. κλύειν πολλὴν βοήν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 401; χρόνος καθαιρεῖ πάντα.. ὁ. [prev. author] “Eu.” 286; δυοῖν ὁ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 329; εἰ γὰρ Αἰγίσθῳ θ᾽ ὁ.}}**likewise,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “El.” 1416: frequently accompanying two Substantives already connected by καί}} or τε, like Engl. **both,** εἰ δὴ ὁ. πόλεμός τε δαμᾷ καὶ λοιμὸς Ἀχαιούς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.61; ὁ. γλυφίδας τε λαβὼν καὶ νεῦρα 4.122}}; χίλι᾽ ὑπέστη, αἶγας ὁ. καὶ ὄϊς a thousand smaller cattle, **both** goats and sheep,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|11.245; θυμὸς τείρεθ᾽ ὁ. καμάτῳ τε καὶ ἱδρῷ 17.745, etc.; ἐπήγαγε λιμὸν ὁ. καὶ λοιμόν 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 243; ἐφόνευον ἄνδρας ὁ. καὶ ἵππους 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.64; οἴκτειρε θῆλυν ἄρσενός θ᾽ ὁ. γόον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 502; πόλιν τε κἀμὲ καί σ᾽ ὁ. στένει 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 64, compare “El.” 667, “Aj.” 1079; ὁ. ταράττων τήν τε γῆν καὶ τὴν θάλατταν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 431; ἱππέας τε καὶ ὁπλίτας ὁ. 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.30, etc.: repeated, ὁ. μὲν.., ὁ. δέ.. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 4}}; compare ὁμῶς.
**I.3** with _dative_, **together with, along with,** ὁ. νεφέεσσιν ἰών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.867; κεῖσθαι ὁ. νεκύεσσι 15.118; ὁ. τῇ λίμνῃ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.101; οἰμωγὴ.. ὁ. κωκύμασιν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 426: also with ὁμοῦ}} following the _dative_, ὅσσαι μοι ὁ. τράφεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.723}}; θεοῖς ὁ., ={ξὺν θεοῖς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 767}}; οὔ ποτ᾽ εἶμι τοῖς φυτεύσασίν γ᾽ ὁ. will never **meet** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OT” 1007; τοῖσιν ἐχθίστοισι συνναίειν ὁ. [prev. author] “Tr.” 1237, compare “OT” 337, “OC” 949, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 104.}}
**II** **close at hand,** ὁρῶ τάλαιναν Εὐρυδίκην ὁ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1180, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 245, “Pax” 513, “Th.” 572, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.2}}: also with _dative_, **close to,** τοῖς ἐμοῖς πλευροῖς ὁ. κλιθεῖσαν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1225, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.2.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theages” 129d}}; ὁ. τι τῷ (variant{τοῦ}) τίκτειν παρεγένεθ᾽ ἡ κόρη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 851, compare ὁ. τι τῷ τίκτειν 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.78}}; ὁ. τῷ θανάτῳ ὄντας **at** death's **door,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 4.36}}; ὁ. ἦν καὶ ἔχειν τὴν πόλιν καὶ τὸ γένος ὅλον μετὰ τῆς πόλεως it was **much the same thing,** {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 2.16.}}
**II.2** rarely with _genitive_, νεὼς ὁ. στείχειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1218}}; εἶναι ὁ. ἀλλήλων (variant{-οις}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.24; τόπου “CPR” 4.34 (1st c.AD)}}; κυμάτω [ν ὁ] μοῦ uncertain in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus “Supp.” 2.11.}}
**II.3** of amount, **in all, in round numbers,** ἀπὸ Σόλωνος ὁ. διακόσι᾽ ἐστὶν ἔτη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.251; εἰσὶν ὁ. δισμύριοι [prev. author] 25.51, compare 36.36, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 140; γίνονται ὁ πυροῦ ἀρτάβαι λ᾽ “CPR” 35.12 (3rd c.AD).}}

3676:
** ὅμως**,
_conjunction_ (from ὁμῶς, with changed accent), **all the same, nevertheless,** used to limit whole clauses, once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} (unless it is read in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.565), Σαρπήδοντι δ᾽ ἄχος γένετο.., ὅ. δ᾽ οὐ λήθετο χάρμης 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.393; ὅ. πιθοῦ μοι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1064, compare “Ant.” 519}}; κοὐκ ἐπίδηλος ὅ. and **yet** not so as to be observed, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 442; νῦν δὲ ὅ. θαρρῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193e, etc.: frequently strengthened by other words, ἀλλ᾽ ὅ.}} but **still,** but **for all that,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1085, etc.}}; ὅ. μήν (_Doric dialect_ μάν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 297d; ὅ. μέντοι [prev. author] “Cri.” 54d; ὅ. γε μήν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 631, 822; ὅ. γε μέντοι [prev. author] “V.” 1344, “Ra.” 61: used elliptically, πάντως μὲν οἴσεις οὐδὲν ὑγιές, ἀλλ᾽ ὅ. (i.e. οἰστέον) [prev. author] “Ach.” 956, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 843, “Ba.” 1027 (probably).}}
**II** frequently in apodosi after καὶ εἰ (κεἰ) or καὶ ἐάν (κἄν), κεἰ τὸ μηδὲν ἐξερῶ, φράσω δ᾽ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 234, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 933; but ὅ., though it belongs in sense to the apodosis, is frequently closely attached to the protasis, μέμνησ᾽ Ὀρέστου, κεἰ θυραῖός ἐσθ᾽ ὅ., i.e. κεἰ θ. ἐστι, ὅμως μέμνησο, [prev. work] 115}}; λέξον.., κεἰ στένεις ὅ., i.e. κεἰ στένεις, ὅ. λέξον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pers.” 295; κἂν ἄποπτος ᾖς ὅ., φώνημ᾽ ἀκούω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 15}}: sometimes it even stands in the protasis, ἐρημία με, κεἰ δίκαι᾽ ὅ. λέγω, σμικρὸν τίθησι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “OC” 957; ἐγὼ μὲν εἴην, κεἰ πέφυχ᾽ ὅ. λάτρις, ἐν τοῖσι γενναίοισιν ἠριθμημένος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 728.}}
**II.2** the protasis is frequently replaced by a _participle_, ὕστεροι ἀπικόμενοι ἱμείροντο ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.120}}; κλῦθί μου νοσῶν ὅ. (i. e. εἰ νοσεῖς ὅ. κλῦθι) {{Refs 6th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1115: strengthened, πιθοῦ, καίπερ οὐ στέργων ὅ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 712; ἱκνοῦμαι, καὶ γυνή περ οὖσ᾽ ὅ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 680; τάδ᾽ ἔρδω, καὶ τύραννος ὢν ὅ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 851; ἐρήσομαι δέ, καὶ κακῶς πάσχουσ᾽ ὅ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 280: sometimes it precedes, τόλμα.., ὅ. ἄτλητα πεπονθώς, for καίπερ πεπονθώς, ὅ. τόλμα, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1029: in Prose, οἱ δὲ.. ὅ. ταῦτα πυνθανόμενοι ἀρρώδεον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.74; οἱ τετρακόσιοι.. ὅ. καὶ τεθορυβημένοι ξυνελέγοντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.93, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.63, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.2.21: exceptionally, ἡ ἰσομοιρία τῶν κακῶν, ἔχουσά τινα ὅμως.. κούφισιν, οὐδ᾽ ὧς ῥᾳδία ἐδοξάζετο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75.}}
**II.3** where the protasis does not contain a verb, ἀπάλαμόν περ ὅ. (variant{ὁμῶς}) ἐπὶ ἔργον ἐγείρει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 20; βαρέα δ᾽ οὖν ὅ. φράσον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 810; κόλακι, δεινῷ θηρίῳ, ὅ. ἐπέμειξεν ἡ φύσις ἡδονήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 240b.}}
**III** used to break off a speech, **however..,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 74}}; to refer to something previously said or to the general situation, **after all, in spite of all,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.105, 3.28, 80, 7.1.}}

3677:
** ὄνᾰρ**, τό,
used only in _nominative_ and _accusative_ _singular_, the other cases being supplied by ὄνειρος (which see):— **dream, vision in sleep,** opposed to a waking vision, οὐκ ὄ., ἀλλ᾽ ὕπαρ ἐσθλόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.547, compare 20.90; ἡλίῳ δείκνυσι τοὔναρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 425; εἶδον ὄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1090}}; ἄκουε δὴ ὄ. ἀντὶ ὀνείρατος **dream** for dream, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 201d}}; ὥστε μηδὲ ὄ. ἰδεῖν, of profound sleep, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ap.” 40d: proverbial, τὸ ἐμόν γ᾽ ἐμοὶ λέγεις ὄ.}} 'you are telling me what I know already', [{{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 563d, compare 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus see {ταὐτὸν πέπονθα} compare (ὄνειρος 1, ὄνειαρ II).}}]
**2** proverbial of anything fleeting or unreal, ὀλιγοχρόνιον.. ὥσπερ ὄ. {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1020; σε παρέρχεται ὡς ὄ. ἥβη 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.8; πόθος δέ μοι ὡς ὄ. ἔπτα 2nd c.BC: Bion Bucolicus 1.58: in Prose, ἡ ἐμὴ [σοφία].., ὥσπερ ὄ. οὖσα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175e, compare “Men.” 85c; ὡς ὄ. ἐλευθερίας ὁρῶντας 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Theseus” 32; and without ὡς, σκιᾶς ὄ. ἄνθρωπος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 8.95}}; ὄ. ἡμερόφαντον ἀλαίνει, of an old man, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 82 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**II** in Trag. and _Attic dialect_ frequently as _adverb_, **in a dream, in sleep,** ὄ. γὰρ ὑμᾶς νῦν Κλυταιμνήστρα καλῶ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Eu.” 116}}; ὄ. διώκεις θῆρα{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 131; ὄ. πνεύσαντα νυκτός 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 65: frequently in 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus, ὄ. ἐπλουτήσαμεν “Tht.” 208b; ὄ. ὀνείρατα διηγεῖσθαι [prev. work] 158c}}, etc.; also οὐδ᾽ ὄ. not even **in a dream,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 107, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 1.11; πολιτικὸς ἀνὴρ οὐδ᾽ ὄ. 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 1.18.6}}; μηδ᾽ ἰδὼν ὄ. not even **in my dreams,** [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 518, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 173d, 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 4.18; ἃ μηδ᾽ ὄ. ἤλπισαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.275: hence frequently opposed to ὕπαρ, see.{ὕπαρ}}}] 11; κατ᾽ ὄναρ **in a dream,** condemned by {{NT+2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 395, but quoted by him from Polemo, is also found in NT.Matt.1.20, 2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 47(23).21}}: with sense, **in consequence of a dream,** in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“SIG” 1147 (Crete, 2nd-3rd c.AD), “Supp.epigram” 2.405 (from Macedonia).}}

3679:
** ὀνειδ-ίζω**,
_future_ **-ιῶ**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1423, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 430, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30a:}}_aorist_ ὠνείδισα (_Epic dialect_ ὀν-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.34, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὠνείδικα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.15}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 936, etc.:}}_future_ _middle_ ὀνειδιεῖσθε (in _passive_ sense) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1500}}: _aorist_ ὠνειδίσθην {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.4.10:}}
**I** **cast in** one's **teeth, make a reproach** to one, usually with _accusative_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, ἀλκὴν μέν μοι πρῶτον ὀνείδισας {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.34, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.380, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 718, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.90, 8.106; αἰσχύνομαί σοι τοῦτ᾽ ὀνειδίσαι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 917; ἃ δ᾽ εἰς γάμους μοι βασιλικοὺς ὠνείδισας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 547; ὀ. φόνον [τινί] 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.120; ὄνειδος ὀ. εἴς τινα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 754, “Ph.” 523}}: with a _relative_ clause instead of the _accusative_, Ἀγαμέμνονι.. ὀνειδίζων, ὅτι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.255, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30a, +others; ὀ. τινί, ὡς.. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.9.8, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.211 (without _dative_); ὀ. εἰς ἀχαριστίαν τινί, διότι.. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 28.4.11}}: with _infinitive_, εἴ τίς τῳ ὀ. φιλοκερδεῖ εἶναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hipparchus” 232c; ὀ. τινὶ τετρῆσθαι τὰ ὦτα 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.50: without _dative_ _person_, ὀνειδιῶν τι τῶν πάρος κακῶν}}**make a reproach,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1423, compare 441, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.143}}:—_passive_, **to be made a reproach,** καὶ σχεδὸν δὴ πάντα.. οὐκ ὀρθῶς ὀνειδίζεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 86d, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.77.}}
**II** without _accusative_ _of things_, **reproach, upbraid,**
**II.1** with _dative_ _person_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 27.16, etc.; τισὶ περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.79; [τινὶ] ἔς τι [prev. author] 8.92; without _dative_ _person_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.95.}}
**II.2** with _accusative_ _person_, **chide, reproach,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30e}}; τοιαῦτ᾽ ὀνειδίζεις με thus **dost thou reproach** me, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1002}}; also ἐπειδὴ.. τυφλόν μ᾽ ὠνείδισας (i.e. ὄντα) **didstreproach** me **with** being blind, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OT” 412}}:—_passive_, **to be reproached,** ἔκ τινων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 936; εἰς δειλίαν 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.62; τὴν μικροπολιτείαν}}**with..,** {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.39.29.}}

3681:
** ὄνειδ-ος**, εος, τό,
**reproach, rebuke, censure, blame,** especially by word, προθέουσιν ὀνείδεα μυθήσασθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.291; λέγ᾽ ὀ. 2.222; ὀ. βάζεις 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.461}}; εἶχε ὄ. καὶ ἀτιμίην was **in disgrace,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.71; ὄ. ὀνειδίζειν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 523}}; ὄ. φέρει it brings **reproach,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 590c; ὄ. τινὶ περιθεῖναι 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.18; περιάψειν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 21.24}}; ὡς ἐν ὀνείδει by way of **reproach,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 512c, compare “R.” 431b (without ὡς “Smp.” 189e)}}; ὀνείδει ἐνέχεσθαι, συνέχεσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” 808e, 944e: _plural_, ὀνείδη κλύειν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 757; κολάζειν ὀνείδεσι}}**with censures,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 847a; ὀνείδη ἔχει τὰ μέγιστα [prev. author] “R.” 344b; ὀ. ἐπιφέρειν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1123a32.}}
**2** **matter of reproach, disgrace,** σοὶ γὰρ ἐγὼ.. κατηφείη καὶ ὄ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.498; σοὶ μὲν δὴ.. κατηφείη καὶ ὄ., εἰ.. 17.556, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.36; ἐμῇ κεφαλῇ κατ᾽ ὀνείδεα χεῦαν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.463; τέκνοις ὄ. λιπεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 301; ὀνειδῶν καὶ κακῶν μέστους 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 22.31}}; ὄνειδός [ἐστι] with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 410: with _genitive_, τὸ.. πόλεως ὄ.}} the **disgrace** of the city, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 539; αὑτῆς ὄ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 984; ὄ. Ἑλλάνων [prev. author] “Aj.” 1191 (Lyric poetry); τὸ Λυσίου ὄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 277a}}; Oedipus calls his daughters τοιαῦτ᾽ ὀνείδη, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1494, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 855, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.132.}}
**3** the statement of {{Refs|12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 88.15, 647.36 that ὄ.}} meant originally **any report** of one, **reputation, character,** is not borne out by the passages he cites— ὄ. οὐ καλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 477; Θήβαις κάλλιστον ὄ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 821 (Lyric poetry); καλὸν ὄ. [prev. author] “Med.” 514, “IA” 305}}, which are plainly ironical. (Cf. Sanskrit _nindati, nid-_ 'insult', Gothic _ga-naitjan_ 'slander', Lett. **naids** 'hatred'.)

3685:
** ὀνίνημι** [ῐ], ὀνίνης
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 301c, ὀνίνησι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.45, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, etc.}}; _infinitive_ ὀνινάναι uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 600d}}; _participle_ ὀνινάς, ᾶσα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 58c (_imperfect_ supplied by ὠφέλουν)}}: _future_ ὀνήσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.36, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.141, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1004, etc.}}; _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὀνασεῖ {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.36}}: _aorist_ ὤνησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.509, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.76, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 933, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27c}}; _Epic dialect_ ὄνησα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.503}}:—_middle_, ὀνίνᾰμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 525c}}: _imperfect_ ὠνινάμην {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 380b}}: _future_ ὀνήσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.173, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 570, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 935, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30c:}}_aorist_ I ὠνησάμην only in {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 2.381 (unless in “Anthologia Graeca” 7.484 (3rd c.BC: Dioscorides Epigrammaticus) we accept ὠνάσατο [with ᾰ] for the meaningless ὠνόσατο); ὀνήσω (2 _person_ _sing._) in 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “ad Marcellam - Porphyrii Opuscula” 10 is _falsa lectio_ either for ὠνήσω or for ὤνησο}}: _aorist 2_ ὠνήμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1380, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 335, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 84c; _imperative_ ὄνησο 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.68}}; _participle_ ὀνήμενος {{Refs|2.33}} (compare ἀπ-); also ὠνάμην, ὤνασθε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1368, ὤναο 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.6, and frequently later, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “D Mort.” 12.2, etc.; ὤνατο “IG” 14.1389 ii 37, ὤναντο 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 1.23}}; _infinitive_ ὄνασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 517, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528a; _optative_ ὀναίμην, which is frequently (see. below 11.2)}}, may belong to either form: in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus ὠνάμην is the}}_aorist_ I of ὄνομαι:—_passive_ **ὀνέομαι** occurs twice, ὀνεῖται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.22.62, ὀνούμενοι Ps.- 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philopatr.” 26}}: _aorist_ _infinitive_ ὀνηθῆναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.5.2}}; _Doric dialect_ ὠνάθην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.55:}}
**I** _active_, **profit, benefit, help,** and sometimes **gratify, delight,** _absolutely_, βουλὴν.. ὑποθησόμεθ᾽ ἥτις ὀνήσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.36, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 429, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 533, etc.}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_ or _adverb_, ὀ. παῦρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 577; σμικρὰ ὀνήσει πόλιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 705 (_anapaest meter_), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 58c; μᾶλλον 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 55, 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “ὀξέων νούσων θεραπευτικόν” 1.4: c._accusative_ _person_, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.205, 7.172, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.141, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 314, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 1033, etc.}}: with _neuter_ _adjective_, ἄνδρας μέγα σίνεται ἠδ᾽ ὀνίνησι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.45, compare 9.509, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.38, etc.; πολλὰ ὀ. τινά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.67; τοσόνδε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 933; εἴ ποτε δή τι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.395: with _dative_ modi, εἴ ποτε δή σε ὄνησα ἢ ἔπει ἢ ἔργῳ [prev. work] 503: with}}_participle_, Ξενοφῶντα ὠνήσατε οὐχ ἑλόμενοι **by** not electing him, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.32, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 193d, “Hp.Ma.” 301c; ὡς ὤνησας ὅτι ἀπεκρίνω [prev. author] “Ap.” 27c: with double _accusative_, σὲ δὲ τοῦτό γε γῆρας ὀνήσει}} this **benefit** at least **will** thine old age **bestow on** thee, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.24}}; also οὐδεμίαν ὤνησε κάλλος εἰς πόσιν ξυνάορον **helped** her in her relations with.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 909.1.}}
**II** _middle_, **have profit** or **advantage, enjoy help** or **support, have enjoyment** or **delight,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.260, 7.173, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.415, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hippolytus” 517, etc.}}: with _participle_, **have benefit from** being or doing so and so, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1380, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 30c, “R.” 380b, “Men.” 84c, etc.}}: but most frequently with _genitive_, **have advantage from.., have delight** or **enjoyment of..,** δαιτὸς ὄνησο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.68; λέκτρων -ήσομαι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1348}}; πρὶν σφῷν ὄνασθαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 1025, cf. Alcaeus335: frequently with _neuter_}} _adjective_ added, τί σευ ἄλλος ὀνήσετα; what **good will** others **have** of thee, i. e. what good will you have done them? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 16.31; τοσόνδ᾽ ὀνήσῃ τῶν ἐμῶν.. πορθμῶν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 570, etc.; so ὄνασθαί τι ἀπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528a; also ὀ. τοῦτο ὅτι.. 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 12.2: also with an ironical sense, ὄναιο μέντἄν, εἴ τις ἐκπλύνειέ σε}} **you'd be the better of it,** if one were to wash you clean, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1062}}; ἁλσὶν διασμηχθεὶς ὄναιτ᾽ ἂν οὑτοσί **he'd be very nice** if he were rubbed down with salt, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Nu.” 1237}}; so ὠνάθην μεγάλως ὅτι.. **lucky for me** that.., {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.55; ὤνησο, διότι μὴ ὁ Ζεὺς ἐπήκουσέ σου 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Prom.” 20.}}
**II.2** _aorist_ _optative_ ὀναίμην, αιο, αιτο, in protestations, wishes, etc., ὄναιο **mayst thou have profit,** i. e. **bless thee..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1677, etc.: and with _genitive_, ὄναιο τῶν φρενῶν}} **bless thee** for.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “IA” 1359; ὄναισθε μύθων [prev. author] “IT” 1078, compare “Hel.” 1418}}; οὕτως ὀναίμην τῶν τέκνων so **may I have profit** of them, in a parenthesis, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 469; οὕτως ὄναισθε τούτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 28.20; ὄναιντο βίου 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 128}}; μή νυν ὀναίμην, ἀλλ᾽.. ὀλοίμην **may I** not **see good,** but die, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 644}}; ὄναιο τοῦ γενναίου χάριν **bless thee** for thy noble spirit, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 1042.}}
**II.3** _aorist_ _participle_ ὀνήμενος, of those to whom (or of whom) one says ὄναιο (ὄναιτο), **blessed,** ἐσθλός μοι δοκεῖ εἶναι, ὀνήμενος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.33: for this sense of a}}_participle_ compare ἐπίτριπτος, οὐλόμενος.

3686:
** ὄνομα**,
_Aeolic dialect_ and _Doric dialect_ **ὄνῠμα** {{Refs|“IG” 12(2).68.8 (Lesb.), “GDI” 4992a iii 7 (from Crete), “SIG” 1122.8 (from Selinus), “Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}; _Laconian dialect_ ***ἔνυμα** probably in _proper nouns_ Ἐνυμακρατίδας {{Refs|“IG” 5(1).213.45, Ἐνυμαντιάδας [prev. work] 97.20, 280.2}}; _poetry_ also (for the sake of meter) **οὔνομα** (see.below), which appears regularly in codices of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus (along with ὀνομάζω, as 2.50, 4.35, +others)}}, and sometimes in other _Ionic dialect_ prose authors (variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 25, etc.)}}, but is probably not Ionic; _Ionic dialect_ Inscrr. have only ὄνομα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.235.39 (from Oropus), etc.: 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus has οὔνομα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.194, 9.355, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.235, οὐνόματ᾽ (α) 17.260, ὄνομα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.16, 364, 366, 19.183, ὄνομ᾽ (α) 4.710}} et saep.:—**name** of a person or thing, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} always of a person, except ἐρέω δέ τοι οὔνομα λαῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.194 and in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.248 (see. infr. II); Οὖτις ἐμοί γ᾽ ὄ. 9.366, compare 18.5, 19.183, 247}}; Ἀρήτη δ᾽ ὄνομ᾽ ἐστὶν ἐπώνυμον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|7.54, compare 19.409, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 144: in Prose ὄνομα}} is used _absolutely_, **by name,** πόλις ὄ. Καιναί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.28, etc.: also _dative_, πόλις Θάψακος ὀνόματι [prev. work] 1.4.11 (variant)}}; ὀνόματι λέγειν **by name,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 21c; ἐπ᾽ ὀνόματος δηλοῦσθαι 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 18.45.4, etc.}}; κατ᾽ ὄνομα by **name,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Strato Comicus 1.14, “epigram Gr.” 983.4 (from Philae)}}; ἀσπάζου τοὺς φίλους κατ᾽ ὄ. each **by** his **name,** {{NT|NT.3John.14.}}
**2** ὄ. τίθεσθαι or θέσθαι τινί give one **a name,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.403, 406, 8.554, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 6, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 810:}} —_passive_, ὄ. κεῖταί τινι{{Refs|[prev. work] 1291}}; ὄ. ἐστι or κεῖται ἐπί τινι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.14.2, “Cyr.” 2.2.12}}; so ὄ. φέρειν or ἐπιφέρειν ἐπί τι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1119a33, “HA” 572a11.}}
**3** ὄνομα καλεῖν τινα call one **by name,** εἴπ᾽ ὄνομ᾽, ὅττι σε κεῖθι κάλεον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.550; καλοῦσί με τοῦτο τὸ ὄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 7.3, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 259, 800, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 393e, etc.}}:—so in _passive_, ὄ. δ᾽ ὠνομάζετο Ἕλενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 605, compare “El.” 694; ὄ. δημοκρατία κέκληται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.37; τὸ ἐναντίον ὄ. ἀφροσύνη μετωνόμασται [prev. author] 1.122; ὄ. ἓν κεκλημένους Σικελιώτας [prev. author] 4.64; λεγόμενοι τοὔνομα γεωργικοί 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 842e; but also ὀνόματί τινα προσαγορεύειν 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.40}}; reversely, ὄνομα καλεῖν τινι give **a name** to, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 279e, “Cra.” 385d; ὄ. καλεῖν ἐπί τινι [prev. author] “Prm.” 147d; τύμβῳ δ᾽ ὄ. σῷ κεκλήσεται.. Κυνὸς σῆμα 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 1271; τοὔνομα προσηγορεύθη 4th c.BC: Anaxilas Comicus 21.3.}}
**II** **name, fame,** Ἰθάκης γε καὶ ἐς Τροίην ὄνομ᾽ ἵκει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.248; οὐδὲ θανὼν ὄνομ᾽ ὤλεσας 24.93}}; ὄ. ἔχειν or σχεῖν ἀπό τινος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.71, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 282a; τὸ μεγα ὄ. τῶν Ἀθηνῶν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.64; τῷ μέλλοντι χρόνῳ καταλιπεῖν ὄ. ὡς.. [prev. author] 5.16; τοὔνομά τινος μεῖζον ἀφικνεῖται εἰς τὴν πόλιν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.1.20; ὧν ὀνόματα μεγάλα λέγεται ἐπὶ σοφίᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 281c; ὄ. μέγιστον ἔχειν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.64}}; ἐν ὀνόματι εἶναι to have **a name,** to be **notable,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 9.1.23; οἱ ἐν πράγμασιν ἐπ᾽ ὀνόματος γεγονότες 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 15.35.1; παράσιτοι δ᾽ ἐπ᾽ ὀνόματος ἐγένοντο}}**notably,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 6.240c; τῶν δι᾽ ὀνόματος παρασίτων [prev. work] 241a.}}
**III** **a name and nothing else,** opposed to the real person or thing, ἵνα μηδ᾽ ὄνομ᾽ αὐτοῦ ἐν ἀνθρώποισι λίπηται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.710; βοᾶς δ᾽ ἔτι μηδ᾽ ὄνομ᾽ εἴη 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 16.97}}; opposed to ἔργον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 454, “Hipp.” 502; περὶ ὄ. μάχεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 33.3; ἐκ τῶν ὀ. μᾶλλον ἢ τῶν πραγμάτων σκέψασθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.15}}; ὀνόματι διαφέρεσθαι dispute about a **word,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 285a, “Lg.” 644a.}}
**III.2** **false name, pretence, pretext,** ὀνόματι ἐννόμῳ ξυμμαχίας under the **pretence..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.60; μετ᾽ ὀνομάτων καλῶν [prev. author] 5.89; χώρα καλῶν ὀ. καὶ προσχημάτων μεστή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 495c, compare 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.5.4.}}
**IV** in _periphrastic_ phrases, ὄ. τῆς σωτηρίας, ={σωτηρία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 905, compare ὄνομ᾽ ὁμιλίας ἐμῆς (variant for ὄμμ᾽) [prev. author] “Or.” 1082}}: with the names of persons, _periphrastic_ for the person, ὦ φίλτατον ὄ. Πολυνείκους {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1702.}}
**IV.2** of persons, ὄχλος ὀνομάτων {{NT|NT.Act.1.15}}; ἕτερα ὀ. ἀντ᾽ αὐτοῦ.. πέμψαι Wilcken {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Chr.” 28.19 (2nd c.AD)}}; in Accountancy, both of persons and things (cf. Latin _nomen_), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “adv. Athenogenem” 6, 10 (both _plural_), “Jahresh.” 26 “Beibl.” 13 (Ephes., 2nd c.AD, _plural_)}}; βαρέσαι τὸ ἐμὸν ὄ. charge my **account,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 126.8 (6th c.AD); τὸν τόκον τὸν ὀνόματί μου παραγραφέντα [prev. work]513.22 (2nd c.AD)}}; in registers of titledeeds, etc., οἰκίας οὐ κειμένης ἐν ὀνόματι τῆς ἀποδομένης not booked under the **name** of the seller, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PLips.” 3 ii 25 (3rd c.AD)}}; ὀνόματι ἰδιωτικῆς under the **head** of private land, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PCair.Preis.” 47.10 (4th c.AD)}}; δικαιώματα.. ἑκάστῳ ὀνόματι παράκειται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 113.11 (2nd c.AD)}}; in tax-receipts, ἔσχον ὀνόματος Σομτοῦς on **account** of {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, “Ostr.Bodl.” ii 39 (2nd c.AD),compare “PFay.” 85.7 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}
**V** **phrase, expression,** especially of technical terms, ὀ. τὰ ἐν τῇ ναυτικῇ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 1.19: generally, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.187.}}
**VI** Grammars, **word,** opposed to ῥῆμα (expression), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 399b, compare “Ap.” 17c, “Smp.” 198b, 199b, 221e, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 9.9, 11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1404b5, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.72, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 12.25, +others, 4th c.BC: Demetrius Phalereus Rhetor “Demetrius on Style” 23, +others}}; τὸ ἰλλαίνειν ὄ. the **word** ἰλλαίνειν, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).679.}}
**VI.2** **noun,** opposed to ῥῆμα (verb, predicate), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 168b, “Sph.” 262a, 262b, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Poetica” 1457a10, “Int.” 16a19, +others}}; as one of five parts of speech, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.45}}; ὄ. κύριον a proper **name,** opposed to προσηγορικόν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 636.16, 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Pronominibus;” 26.12, +others (so ὄ. alone, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 681 ff., 2nd c.BC: Diogenes Babylonius Stoicus 3.213)}}; also of adjectives, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.222}}. (Cf. Gothic _namo,_ _genitive_ **namins,** Latin _nōmen,_ Sanskrit _nāma._)

3687:
** ὀνομάζω**,
_imperfect_ ὠνόμαζον {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 682 (Lyric poetry)}}, etc.; _Epic dialect_ ὀν- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.361, +others}}: _future_ ὀνομάσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 423d}}: _aorist_ ὠνόμασα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.339, etc.}}: _perfect_ ὠνόμακα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 219b}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ασθήσομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus “de Usu Partium” 6.16, +others:}}_aorist_ ὠνομάσθην and _perfect_ ὠνόμασμαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.96, 6.96, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ὀνόμασται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 9.1, etc.}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὠνομάδαται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 37.16}}:—_middle_, _imperfect_ ὠνομάζετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1021.}} —_Aeolic dialect_ or _Doric dialect_ _future_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ ὀνυμάξει (or -εῖ) {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.167 (from Cyrene)}}: _aorist_ ὀνύμαξε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.44}}; _middle_ _future_ ὀνυμάξομαι{{Refs|[prev. work] 7.5}}: _present_ ὀνυμάζεται{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Metop. cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 3.1.116}}: (ὄνομα):—**speak of by name, call** or **address by name,** of persons, πατρόθεν ἐκ γενεῆς ὀνομάζων ἄνδρα ἕκαστον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.68, compare 22.415 and ὀνομακλήδην}}; Πυθοδώρου.., ὃν Ἀθηναῖοι οὐκ ὀνομάζουσιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.1 (interpolated)}}; τοῖς προγόνοις -αζομένοις ἀπομνημονεύεται ὁπόστος ἀφ᾽ Ἡρακλέους ἐγένετο his descent.. is traced by **naming** his ancestors, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ages.” 1.2.}}
**2** of things, **name, specify,** περικλυτὰ δῶρ᾽ ὀνόμαζον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.449}}; but also, **name** or **promise,** opposed to giving, εἰ μὲν.. μὴ δῶρα φέροι, τὰ δ᾽ ὄπισθ᾽ ὀνομάζοι {{Refs|9.515}}; εἶναί τι ὀνομάζειν **use the term** 'being', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 160b, compare 166c, 201d}}; **dedicate,** τράπεζαν τῷ δαίμονι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 121}}:—_passive_, λόγοισι.. ὠνόμασται βραχέσι **have been expressed,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 294.}}
**II** ὀ. τινά τι **call** one something, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 2.44, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 681 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.6, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.3, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 1193}}; ὄνομα τί σε.. ὠνόμαζεν λεώ; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 87 (Lyric poetry):}} —rarely in _middle_, παῖδά μ᾽ ὠνομάζετο **called** me **his** son, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1021}}:—_passive_, ὄνομα δ᾽ ὠνομάζετο Ἕλενος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 605; τὴν αὑτῆς ἐπωνυμίαν ὀνομαζόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 238a; ἀντὶ γὰρ φίλων καὶ ξένων, ἃ τότ᾽ ὠνομάζοντο 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.46.}}
**II.b** **nominate,** ὀνομασθεὶς εἰς δεκαπρωτείαν {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1257.1, compare 1204.4 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II.2** εἶναι is frequently added _pleonastic_, τὰς ὀνομάζουσι εἶναι Ὑπερόχην καὶ.. whose **names they say are** Hyperoche and.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.33; σοφιστὴν ὀνομάζουσιν τὸν ἄνδρα εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311e, compare “R.” 428e (_passive_), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 13, etc.; compare καλέω 11.3 b.}}
**III** **name** or **call** with reference to, in accordance with, or after.., τινὰ or τι ἐπί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 493c}}:—_passive_, ἐπί τινος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.183; ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1036, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.12; ὁ τῆς ἀρίστης μητρὸς ὠνομασμένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1105.}}
**IV** **utter names** or **words,** ἐς τρὶς ὀνομάσαι Σόλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.86; μάλα σεμνῶς ὀνομάζων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.35, compare 122, 21.158:}} —_passive_, φύσις ἐπὶ τοῖς ὀνομάζεται ἀνθρώποισι **the name** φύσις **is given** by men to those things, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 8.4, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 9.1}}; παρανομίαν ἐπὶ τοῖς μὴ ἀνάγκῃ κακοῖς ὀνομασθῆναι **the name** of transgression **is applied..,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.98}}; ἀπὸ τούτου τοῦτο ὀνομάζεται (i.e. οὐ φροντὶς Ἱπποκλείδῃ) hence this **saying is used,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.130.}}
**V** **make famous,** in _passive_, οἱ ὠνομασμένοι persons **of renown,** variant for{διωνομασμένοι} in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 20.19.}} —Cf. ὀνομαίνω.

3688:
** ὄνος**, ὁ
and ἡ, **ass,** once in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.558; then in “IG” 12.40.12, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.135, etc., compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 580b3}}; ὄνοι οἱ τὰ κέρεα ἔχοντες, together with a number of fabulous animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.191, 192; ὄ. μονοκέρατος 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 499b19, “PA” 663a23, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.41:}} —frequently in proverbs:
**1** ὄ. λύρας (i.e. ἀκούων), of one who can make nothing of music, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 527, [prev. author] “Mis.” 18, cf. Varroap. 2nd c.AD: Gellius, Aulus Grammaticus Latinus 3.16.13, 4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 7.33; explained in 15th c.AD: Apostolius Paroemiographus 12.91a, ὄ. λύρας ἤκουε καὶ σάλπιγγος ὗς}}; ὄ. κάθηται, of one who sits down when caught in the game of ὀστρακίνδα, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 9.106, 112}}; the two proverbs combined by {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 229 ὄνοι δ᾽ ἀπωτέρω κάθηνται τῆς λύρας, compare κιθαρίζω.}}
**2** περὶ ὄνου σκιᾶς for an **ass's** shadow, i.e. for a trifle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 191 (see. Scholia), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 260c; ταῦτα πάντ᾽ ὄνου σκιά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 331.}}
**3** ὄνου πόκαι or πόκες, see at {πόκος} {{Refs|11}}; ὄνον κείρεις, of those who attempt the impossible, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 5.38.}}
**4** ἀπ᾽ ὄνου καταπεσεῖν, of one who gets into a scrape by his own clumsiness. with a pun on ἀπὸ νοῦ πεσεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1273, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 701d.}}
**5** ὄνος ὕεται **an ass** in the rain, of being unmoved by what is said or done, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 52, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Cephisodorus Comicus 1; ὄνῳ τις ἔλεγε μῦθον, ὁ δὲ τὰ ὦτα ἐκίνει 4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 7.30; ὄ. εἰς Ἀθήνας 15th c.AD: Macarius Paroemiographus “Prov.” 6.31.}}
**6** ὄ. ἄγω μυστήρια, i.e my part is to carry burdens, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 159.}}
**7** ὄνων ὑβριστότερος, of wanton behaviour, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.8.3; κριθώσης ὄνου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 876.}}
**8** ὦτ᾽ ὄνου λαβεῖν, like Midas, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 287.}}
**9** ὄ. εἰς ἄχυρα, of one who gets what he wants, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 6.91; ὄνου γνάθος, of a glutton, [prev. work] 100.}}
**10** ὄ. ἐν μελίσσαις, of one who has got into a scrape, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 36}}; but ὄ. ἐν πιθήκοις, of extreme ugliness, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 402.8}}; ὄ. ἐν μύρῳ 'a clown at a feast', {{Refs|10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}}
**11** εἰς ὄνους ἀφ᾽ ἵππων, of one who has come down in the world, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 34.2, compare 2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 2.33, etc.}}
**II** a fish of the cod family, especially the **hake, Merluccius vulgaris,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 67, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 599b33, “fragment” 326, 4th c.AD: Heniochus Comicus 3.3, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 1.151, etc.}}
**III** **wood-louse,** κυλισθεὶς ὥς τις ὄ. ἰσόσπριος [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 363, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 557a23 (variant{ὀνίοις}), 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.3.6}}], [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {σηνίκη};compare ὀνίσκος 11, ἴουλος IV.}}]
**IV** **wingless locust,** ={τρωξαλλίς}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.52.}}
**V** ὄνων φάτνη a nebulous appearance between the ὄνοι (two stars in the breast of the Crab), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 22.21, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 898, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 23 (ἡ τοῦ ὄνου φάτνη [prev. work] 43, 51), 2nd c.AD: Ptolemaeus Mathematicus “Tetrabiblos” 23.}}
**VI** ὄνου πετάλειον, ={φύλλον ὀνίτιδος}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 628.}}
**VII** from **the ass as a beast of burden** the name passed to:
**VII.1** **windlass,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.36, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Mechanica” 853b12.}}
**VII.2** **the upper millstone** which turned round, ὄ. ἀλέτης {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.5; also ὄ. ἀλετών 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 13, 204, compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}see entry {μύλη}; perhaps simply, **millstone,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.83: 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc.}} says that Aristotle also calls the **fixed nether millstone** ὄνος (but {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 964b38 says, ὄνου λίθον ἀλοῦντος}} when **the millstone** is grinding stone, as it does when no grist is in the mill).
**VII.3** **beaker, winecup,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 616, 2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 2 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus}}
**VII.4** **spindle** or **distaff,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.32, 10.125, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**VII.5** perhaps **coping** of a wall, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“Inscription Délos” 372 A 158 (3rd-2nd c.BC).}}
**VIII** **ass's load,** as a measure, πυροῦ ὄ. τρεῖς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFay.” 67.2 (1st c.AD).}}

3690:
** ὄξος**, εος, τό,
(ὀξύς) **poor wine, 'vin ordinaire',** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 35; κοτύλας τέτταρας ὄξους Δεκελικοῦ 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 285, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.14, “POxy.” 1275.20 (3rd c.AD);compare ὀξίνης 1.}}
**2** **vinegar** made therefrom, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 12.334.4, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 322, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 61, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.413, etc.; ὑπώμνυτο ὁ μὲν οἶνος ὄ. αὑτὸν εἶναι γνήσιον, τὸ δ᾽ ὄ. οἶνον αὑτό 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 65; σφόδρ᾽ ἐστὶν.. ὁ βίος οἴνῳ προσφερής· ὅταν ᾖ τὸ λοιπὸν μικρόν, ὄ. γίγνεται 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 240}}; ἐς τὰς ῥῖνας ὄ. ἐγχέων, as a mode of torture, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 620.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, of a **sour fellow,** χὡνὴρ ὄ. ἅπαν {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.148.}}

3691:
** ὀξύς**, ύδος, ἡ,
**wood sorrel, Oxalis Acetosella,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 27.112.}}
**2** ={ὀξύσχοινος}, **great sea-rush, Juncus acutus,** {{Refs|[prev. work] 21.113.}}
**3** ={ὀξαλίς}, **sorrel, Rumex acetosa,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 11.667.}}

3692:
** ὀπή**, ἡ,
**opening, hole,** ὀπὰς γὰρ εἶχεν οὐκ ὀλίγας [τὸ τριβώνιον] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 715}}; a mouse's **hole,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sannyrio Comicus 8}}; of **holes in the earth,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 559a4, +others}}; in a door, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 52}}; in a rock, {{LXX|LXX.Exo.33.22}}; of an orifice in the body, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 5.73; ἡ ὀπὴ τῶν ὤτων [prev. author] 2.84 (codex B).}}
**2** **hole in the roof,** serving as a chimney, ={κάπνη},{καπνοδόκη}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 317 (Lyric poetry), 350; καὶ δι᾽ ὀπῆς κἀπὶ τέγους [prev. author] “fragment” 11, compare 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 4.11.}}
**II** _plural_, in Architecture texts, **holes** in the frieze left to receive the beam-ends, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Vitruvius Scriptor de Architectura Latinus 4.2.4}}; later, of **windows, lights in doors,** αἱ ὀ. τῶν τοίχων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.BC: Ptolemaeus Epigrammaticus cited in 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis de Caelo commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 710.21; σιδήρια ἐξ ὀπῆς “IG” 4.39 (Aegina, 5th c.BC).}}
**III** (ὄψομαι, ὄπωπα) **sight,** τοὶ μήτ᾽ ἀκουὰν μήτ᾽ ὀπὰν πεπαμένοι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Cercidas Iambographus 4.23.}}

3693:
** ὄπισθεν**,
in _Ionic dialect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72, +others, “SIG” 46.65 (Halic., 5th c.BC), etc.)}} and late _Attic dialect_ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 16, etc.)}} **ὄπισθε** before a _consonant_, as also in Poets, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 545, “IT” 1333}}: _poetry_ also **ὄπῐθεν** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.181, +others, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).35, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 1001 (Lyric poetry)}}:— _adverb_:
**I** of Place, **behind, at the back,** opposed to πρόσθε, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.595; πρόσθε λέων ὄπιθεν δὲ δράκων μέσση δὲ χίμαιρα 6.181; ὄπισθεν καταλιπεῖν 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.209; μένειν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.332, etc.; πέμψει οὖρον ὄ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.34}}; ὄπιθεν κομόωντες with long **back**-hair, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.542; ὄπιθεν κομόωσαι ἔθειραι “IG” 12(9).1179.9 (from Euboea)}}; ὄ. ἕπεσθαι, ἀκολουθεῖν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus}} [same place], etc.; οἱ ὄπιθεν **those who are left behind,** e.g. at home, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.66}}; but also, **those who are in the rear,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.8}}; εἰ τοὺς ὄ. ἐς τὸ πρόσθεν ἄξομεν shall bring the **rear ranks** to the front (_metaphorically_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1249}}; αἱ ὄ. ἁψῖδες the **hinder** fellies, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.72}}; τὰ ὄ. the **hinder parts, rear, back,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.613; οἱ ὄ. ἁρμοί “IG” 12.372.117; εἰς τοὔπισθεν}}**back, backwards,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1410, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 261b, etc.}}; εἰς τ. τοξεύειν, i. e. **'versis sagittis',** like the Parthians, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.10: opposed to ἐκ τοὔπισθεν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 482, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.79, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.1.6; ἐν τῷ ὄ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 9.8, etc.}}; ὄ. ποιήσασθαι τὸν ποταμόν place the river **in his rear,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 1.10.9.}}
**I.2** _preposition_ with _genitive_, **behind,** στῆ δ᾽ ὄπιθεν δίφροιο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.468; ὄπισθε μάχης 13.536; ὄπισθε τῆς θύρης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.9; ἔμπροσθέ τε Θερμοπυλέων καὶ ὄ. [prev. author] 7.176; ὄ. ἐμοῦ.. εἰσῄει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 175a, etc.}}: sometimes after its case, δίφρου ὄπισθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.15}}; ἴμεν φάμας ὄπισθεν **follow** the voice, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.63; γνώμης πατρῴας πάντ᾽ ὄ. ἑστάναι 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 640; also τούτοισι δ᾽ ὄ. ἴτω 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 30; πνοιαῖς ὄπιθεν Βορέα 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 3.31 (assuming variant).}}
**II** of Time, **after, in future, hereafter,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.362, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.270, 18.168, etc.}}; either of a thing absolutely future, or of one which follows something else, opposed to αὐτίκα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.519; ὄπιθεν οὐ πολλόν 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 10(11).35; πολλοῖς μησὶν ὄ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus “Epigrammata” 22.8; compare ὀπίσω 11.1.}}
**II.2** ἐν τοῖσι ὄ. λόγοισι in the books **yet to come,** in the **following** books, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.22, 7.213; compare ὀπίσω 11.2}}: but, in Grammars, of what has gone before, [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.366, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see {Ἴωνες}, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1488}}]; ὁ ὄπιθεν χρόνος the **earlier** time, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“PMasp.” 158.22 (6th c.AD):}} —for _comparative_ ὀπίστερος, _superlative_ ὀπίστατος, (see entry). (Prob. from *ὄπις 'back', contained in ἀνόπιν, κατόπιν, μετόπιν, ὀπίσω.)

3694:
** ὀπίσω** [ῐ],
_Epic dialect_ **ὀπίσσω**, the former rare in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus and only in signification 1}}; _Aeolic dialect_ **ὐπίσσω** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 8.9}}:— _adverb_:
**I** of Place, **backwards,** opposed to to πρόσω, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.272}}; to προπρηνές,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|3.218; ἀνεχάζετο τυτθὸν ὀπίσσω 5.443; ἀ. πολλὸν ὀ. 16.710; πάλιν εἶσιν ὀπίσσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.149: in Prose also, τὸ ὀπίσω}} _contraction_ τοὐπίσω, τὸ ὀ. φεύγειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.207, compare 8.108; ἄναγε εἰς τοὐπίσω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528a, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.4, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 106, etc.; [γνάμ]φθη δ᾽ ὀπίσσω [φάσγα]νον 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.53}}; τὰ ὀ. the **hinder parts,** {{LXX+1st c.BC+|LXX.Joel.2.20; τοῦ προπύλου τὸ ὀ. μέρος “SIG” 756.17 (Athens, 1st c.BC); τὴν ὀ. τοῦ προπύλου στέγην [prev. work]12; ἐκ τοῦ ὀ.}} on the **back** (of the papyrus), {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“BGU” 1002.16 (1st c.BC)}}; τἀπίλοιπα ὀ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 58.37 (2nd c.BC).}}
**I.2** **back, back again,** of movement, ἀπήνυσαν οἴκαδ᾽ ὀπίσσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.326; ὀπίσω πάλιν οἴκαδε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 3.62; ἂψ ὀ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.74; ἀπίκοντο ὀ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.62; ὀ. πορευόμενοι [prev. work] 75; ὀ. ἀναπλῶσαι [prev. work] 78}}; ἐν τῇ ὀ. κομιδῇ on his way **back,** {{Refs|[prev. author] 8.120, compare 1.111, etc.}}
**I.3** **back again, again,** of action that reverses an action or occurrence, ἀνακτᾶσθαι ὀ. τὴν τυραννίδα {{Refs|[prev. work] 61, compare 68, 2.14; ἀποδόντες ὀ. [prev. author] 5.92.γ᾽}}; σφραγίζεις λύεις τ᾽ ὀπίσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 38 (_anapaest meter_).}}
**I.4** with _genitive_, δεῦτε ὀ. μου come **after** me, follow me, {{NT|NT.Matt.4.19.}}
**II** of Time, **hereafter,** since the future is unseen and was therefore regarded as **behind** us, whereas the past is known and therefore before our eyes, ἡμῖν τεκέεσσί τ᾽ ὀπίσσω πῆμα λίποιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.160}}; Τρῳαὶ δέ μ᾽ ὀπίσσω πᾶσαι μωμήσονται{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 411, compare 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 741, “Th.” 488; ἔς περ ὀπίσσω 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.199 (εἰσοπίσω 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1104 (Lyric poetry)); opposed to to νῦν, τούτῳ δ᾽ οὔτ᾽ ἂρ νῦν φρένες ἔμπεδοι [εἰσίν], οὔτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ὀπίσσω ἔσσονται 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.352; to προπάροιθε, σεῖο δ᾽, Ἀχιλλεῦ, οὔ τις ἀνὴρ προπάροιθε [ἦν] μακάρτατος, οὔτ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ὀπίσσω [ἔσσεται] 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.483}}; where ὀπίσσω and πρόσσω are opposed, πρόσσω must be the past and ὀπίσσω the **future,** οἶδε νοῆσαι ἅμα πρόσσω καὶ ὀπίσσω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.343; ἅμα πρόσσω καὶ ὀ. λεύσσει 3.109; ὅρα πρόσσω καὶ ὀ. 18.250, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.452; so ὅσα τ᾽ ἦν ὅσα τ᾽ ἔσθ᾽ ὅσα τ᾽ ἔσται ὀπίσσω 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 21.9 (cited in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1000a30)}}; οὔτ᾽ ἐνθάδ᾽ ὁρῶν οὔτ᾽ ὀπίσω neither present nor **future,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 488 (Lyric poetry); τοὐπίσω σκοπεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 49; θνητὸς πεφυκὼς τοὐπίσω πειρῶ βλέπειν Isidorus Tragicus 2; compare ὄπισθεν 11.1.}}
**II.2** ἐν τοῖσι ὀ. λόγοις in the books **yet to come,** in the **following** books, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.75; compare ὄπισθεν 11.2.}}

3695:
** ὁπλ-ίζω**,
_aorist_ ὥπλισα, _Epic dialect_ ὥπλισσα (see. below): _perfect_ ὥπλικα (παρ-) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.10}}: _pluperfect_ ὡπλίκει {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 78.6:}} —_middle_, _future_ -ίσομαι (ἐφ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.39 (Musicius Epigrammaticus), -ιοῦμαι Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.20:}}_aorist_ ὡπλισάμην, _Epic dialect_ ὁπλίσσατο (variant{ὡπλ-}) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.20}}:—_passive_, _aorist_ ὡπλίσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ὅπλισθεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.143}}: _perfect_ ὥπλισμαι [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 733, etc.— 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus usually uses the augment, but codices have ὁπλισάμεσθα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.429, ὅπλισθεν 23.143 (variant{ὥ-}): (ὅπλον, compare ὁπλέω, ὅπλομαι)}}]:—**make** or **get ready,** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} of meats and drink, ἐπεί ῥ᾽ ὥπλισσε κυκειῶ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.641; ὅπλισσόν τ᾽ ἤϊα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.289; δαῖθ᾽ ὁ. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 852:}} —Med, δόρπον or δεῖπνον ὁπλίζεσθαι **make oneself** a meal **ready,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.20, 16.453, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.86; ὡπλίσσατο λύχνον 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 84.1}}; ὁ. θυσίαν θεοῖς **cause** it **to be prepared,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1124.}}
**2** of chariothorses, **get ready, harness, equip,** αὐτὰρ ὅ γ᾽ υἷας ἄμαξαν.. ὁπλίσαι ἠνώγει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.190}} (so in _middle_, **prepare** or **get ready for oneself,** ἐΰτριχας ὡπλίσαθ᾽ ἵππους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|23.301); ὥπλιζον ἵππους προμετωτιδίοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.4.1}}:— _passive_, of ships, νῆες.. ὁπλίζονται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.288}}; of any implements, λαμπὰς διὰ χερῶν ὡπλισμένη **ready for use,**{{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 433}}; θώρακα.. περιβόλοις ὡπλισμένον **furnished with,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 993.}}
**3** of persons, especially of soldiers, **equip, arm,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.127, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 980, etc.}}; also, **train, exercise** soldiers, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.12}}: in _Attic dialect_ Prose, **arm** or **equip as** ὁπλῖται, ὁπλίζειν τὸν δῆμον πρότερον ψιλὸν ὄντα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.27, compare 6.100 (_passive_), 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.15, etc.}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, **make oneself ready, prepare** or **equip oneself, get ready,** ἀλλ᾽ ὅ γ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἔξω ἰὼν ὡπλίζετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.526}}; ὅπλισθεν (for ὡπλίσθησαν) δὲ γυναῖκες the women **got ready** [for dancing],{{Refs|23.143; Τρῶες.. ἀνὰ πτόλιν ὡπλίζοντο}}**were arming,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.55; ἀλλ᾽ ὁπλιζώμεθα θᾶσσον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.495; χαλκῷ ὁπλισθέντας 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.152; κατάπερ Κόλχοι ὡπλισμένοι [prev. author] 7.79; χρωμένους τῷ πλήθει ὡπλισμένῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 551e; ὁπλίζου, καρδία 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1242}}: with _infinitive_, τοὶ δ᾽ ὡπλίζοντο.. νέκυάς τ᾽ ἀγέμεν, ἕτεροι δὲ μεθ᾽ ὕλην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.417; βουσφαγεῖν ὡπλίζετο 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 627:}} —in _middle_, also with _accusative_, ὁπλίζεσθαι χέρα **arm one's** hand, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Or.” 926}} (in _active_, {{Refs|[prev. author] Alcaeus35 (_anapaest meter_))}}; ὁπλίζεσθαι θράσος **arm oneself with** boldness, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 996, compare “Anthologia Graeca” 5.92, NT.1Pet.4.1: frequently with _dative_ instrum., ὁπλιζώμεσθα φασγάνῳ χέρας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1223, compare “Ph.” 267; θύρσοις διὰ χερῶν ὡπλισμέναι [prev. author] “Ba.” 733.}}

3696:
** ὅπλον**, τό,
**tool, implement,** mostly in _plural_, like{ἔντεα},{τεύχεα}: (probably from ἕπω A):
**I** a ship's **tackle, tackling,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”), 2.390, +others, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 627}}; especially **ropes, halyards,** etc., δησάμενοι δ᾽ ἄρα ὅπλα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.430, etc.}}; in which sense {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} twice uses the _singular_, **rope,** {{Refs|14.346, 21.390: generally}}**any ropes,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.25, 9.115, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἄρθρων ἐμβολῆς” 78.}}
**II** **tools,** strictly so called, in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} especially of **smiths' tools,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.409, 412; in full, ὅπλα χαλκήϊα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.433: in _sing._, ὅπλον ἀρούρης sickle, “Anthologia Graeca” 6.95 (from Antiphil)}}; ὅπλον γεροντικόν staff, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 1.7}}; δείπνων ὅπλον ἑτοιμότατον, of the wine-flask, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.248 (Marc. argument).}}
**III** in _plural_, also, **implements of war, arms** and **armour,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (only in 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad”), αὐτὰρ ἐπεὶ πάνθ᾽ ὅπλα κάμε}} of the **arms** of Achilles, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|18.614, compare 19.21; ὅπλοισιν ἔνι δεινοῖσιν ἐδύτην 10.254, 272; so in 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 8.27, “IG” 12.1.9, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 14, etc.}}: rarely in _singular_, **weapon,** οὐδέ τι ἀρήϊον ὅπλον ἐκτέαται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.23, compare 174, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 161, 570, 942, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 474a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.4.15}}; ποτὶ πονηρὸν οὐκ ἄχρηστον ὅπλον ἁ πονηρία [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus] 275}}; **piece of armour,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 3.49.}}
**III.2** **the large shield,** from which the men-at-arms took their name of ὁπλῖται (εἰκόνα γραπτὴν ἐν ὅπλῳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 22.1012.18 (2nd c.BC),compare “IGRom.” 4.1302.35 (Cyme, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD), 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.75, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 15.44, 17.18); ὅπλον στύππινον “IG” 11(2).203 B 99 (Delos, 3rd c.BC)}}: _metaphorically_, τῆς πενίας ὅπλον ἡ παρρησία {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nicostratus Comicus 29; ὅ. μέγιστον.. ἁρετὴ βροτοῖς 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 433, compare 619.}}
**III.3** in _plural_, also, **heavy arms,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.53}}; ὅπλων ἐπιστάτης, ={ὁπλίτης}, opposed to κώπης ἄναξ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 379; ὁ πόλεμος οὐχ ὅπλων τὸ πλέον ἀλλὰ δαπάνης 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.83; ὅπλα παραδοῦναι [prev. author] 4.69; ὅπλα ἀποβάλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 27, etc.}}
**III.4** ὅπλα, ={ὁπλῖται}, **men-at-arms,** πολλῶν μεθ᾽ ὅπλων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 115 (Lyric poetry): and frequently in Prose, ἐξέτασιν ὅπλων ποιεῖσθαι}} to have a muster **of the men-at-arms,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.74, etc.}}; ὁ ἐπὶ τῶν ὅπλων στρατηγός, opposed to ὁ ἐπὶ τῆς διοικήσεως, Decrees cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.38, Decrees [prev. work] 115; χειροτονηθεὶς ἐπὶ τὰ ὅ. πρῶτος.. στρατηγός “IG” 22.682.44 (3rd c.BC)}}; στρατηγεῖν ἐπὶ τὰ ὅ. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 697 E (Delph., 2nd c.BC), etc.}}
**III.5** τὰ ὅ. **the place of arms, camp,** ἦλθεν εἰς τὰ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.12, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.5, etc.; ἐκ τῶν ὅ. προϊέναι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.111, compare 3.1.}}
**III.6** Phrases: ἐνέδυνον (variant{ἐνέδυντο}) τὰ ὅ. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.218, etc.}}; ἐν ὅπλοισι εἶναι or γενέσθαι to be **in arms, under arms,** {{Refs 7th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.13, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 303, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.56; ἐν ὄπλοισι [ἰππομ]άχεντας 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.19; ἐν ὅπλοις μάχεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 456d; ἡ ἐν τοῖς ὅπλοις μάχη [prev. author] “Lg.” 833e}}; ποιῆσαι ἐξέτασιν ἐν ὅπλοις Decrees cited in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 31.2; εἰς τὰ ὅ. παραγγέλλειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.13}}; ἐφ᾽ ὅπλοις or παρ᾽ ὅπλοις ἧσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 674, 357; μένειν ἐπὶ τοῖς ὅπλοις 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.8}}; for ὅπλα ῥίπτειν, ἀφιέναι, κατατίθεσθαι, (see entry).; for ὅπλα τίθεσθαι, see at {τίθημι}.
**IV** of **the arms** possessed by animals for self-defence, [τὸν ἄνθρωπον] οὐκ ἔχοντα ὅπλον πρὸς τὴν ἀλκήν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Partibus Animalium” 687a25, compare b4, +others}}
**V** **membrum virile,** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 74.30, “APl.” 4.242 (1st c.BC: Erycius Epigrammaticus), 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**VI** **a gymnastic exercise,** the last which came on in the games, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.63.}}

3697:
** ὁποῖος**, α, ον,
_Epic dialect_ **ὁπποῖος**, η, ον, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, but twice ὁποῖος, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.421, 19.77}}; _Ionic dialect_ **ὁκοῖος**, η, ον, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 70, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.82, +others, “GDI” iv p.883 (Erythrae, 4th c.BC)}}; Cretan dialect **ὀτεῖος** (which see): correlated to ποῖος: replaced by οἷος in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. after {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC:}}
**1** as Relat., **of what sort** or **quality,** ὁπποῖόν κ᾽ εἴπῃσθα ἔπος, τοῖόν κ᾽ ἐπακούσαις **as** [is] the word thou hast spoken, such shalt thou hear again, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.250}}; τοίῳ ὁποῖος ἔοι such **as** he might be, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.421; οὔθ᾽ οἷ᾽ ἔπασχεν οὔθ᾽ ὁποῖ᾽ ἔδρα κακά 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1272.}}
**2** in indirect questions, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.171, etc.; εὑρεῖν ὁποίοις φαρμάκοις ἰάσιμος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 475}}: in direct questions only as {{Refs 5th c.BC+|_falsa lectio_ in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 878, “Ba.” 663: sometimes followed by ποῖος}} in the same clause, οὐ γὰρ αἰσθάνομαί σου ὁποῖον νόμιμον ἢ ποῖον δίκαιον λέγεις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.4.13; οὐκ οἶδα ὁποίᾳ τόλμῃ ἢ ποίοις λόγοις χρώμενος ἐρῶ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 414d.}}
**II** with indefinite words added, ὁποῖός τις {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.38, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.2.2; ὁκοῖόν τι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.158; γιγνομένων ὁποῖοί τινες ἔτυχον 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1286b24; so in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, ὁπποῖ᾽ ἄσσα}} **of what sort,** for ὁποῖά τινα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.218; ὁποῖ᾽ ἄττα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 465a; ὁποιοσοῠν}}**of what kind soever,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 152d, +others}}; ὁποῖος δή, δήποτε, δηποτοῦν, and οὖν δή, as ὁποία δὴ φλέψ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.4.58; τοὺς ὁποιουσδήποτε.. ἐξεπέμπετε στρατηγούς 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.146: _genitive_, ὁποίου τινὸς οὖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.4.10: _accusative_ _feminine_, ὁποιαντινοῦν 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.11; ὁποῖόσπερ 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 669; ὁποιοσποτοῦν 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Physica” 253b23; ὁποιοσδητισοῦν 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus cited in 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 639.30}}; πόλιν.. οὐδ᾽ ὁποίας ἥττω inferior to **none,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 4.65.3; οὔτ᾽ ἄλλους οὐδ᾽ ὁποίους 4th c.BC: Theopompus Historicus 217}} (**c**); μηδὲ καθ᾽ ὁποῖον τρόπον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“SIG” 672.14 (Delph., 2nd c.BC)}}; μηδ᾽ ὁτίη or μηδοτίη, see at {μηδοτίη}.
**III** _neuter_ _plural_ used as _adverb_, **like as,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 915, 1076, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 398.}}
**IV** _adverb_ ὁποίως, **qualiter,** {{Refs|“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”}}

3698:
** ὁπότε**,
_Epic dialect_ **ὁππότε**, both in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}; _Ionic dialect_ **ὁκότε**; Cyrenaic **ὁπόκᾰ** {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1927.164}}; in _Doric dialect_ Poets **ὁππόκᾰ** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.98:}} _adverb_ of Time, correlated to πότε, used much like{ὅτε}, except that the sense is less definite {{Refs 5th c.BC+|compare (5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.3), though the two were frequently used without distinction:}}
**I** Relat., with the _indicative_, mostly with reference to the past, **when,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.399, 3.173, etc.; the _indicative_ ἦστε is omitted, 8.230: in Class. _Attic dialect_ Prose only ὅτε is so used, when referring to a particular time, but later ὁπότε returns, as ὁπότε περιῆν}} **when** she was alive, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 243.10 (1st c.AD)}}: with the _present_ in a simile, ὡς δ᾽ ὁπότε.. ποταμὸς πεδίονδε κάτεισι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.492}}: with _subjunctive_, like{ὁπόταν}, with reference to an _indefinite_ number of occasions in the _present_ or to the future, ὁππότ᾽ Ἀχαιοὶ Τρώων ἐκπέρσωσ᾽ εὖ ναιόμενον πτολίεθρον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1.163, compare 13.817, 21.112, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.170, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 782: sometimes in similes, ὡς ὁπότε νέφεα Ζέφυρος στυφελίξῃ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.305, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.335}}; but ὁπότ᾽ ἄν, _Epic dialect_ ὁπότε or ὁππότε κεν, is more common with _subjunctive_, and in _Attic dialect_ Prose ἄν must be used, see at {ὁπόταν}: Cyrenaic ὁπόκα κα δήληται {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” [prev. cited]}}
**I.2** with _optative_:
**I.2.a** to express an event that occurred often, ὁπότε Κρήτηθεν ἵκοιτο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.233, compare 10.189, 15.284, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.591, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.99, 2.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 220a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.28.}}
**I.2.b** after a verb of waiting, of a time future relatively to the past, ἷζε.. δέγμενος ὁππότε ναῦφιν ἀφορμηθεῖεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.794, compare 4.334, 7.415, 9.191, 18.524.}}
**I.2.c** in indirect speech, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 824 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.20}}; in implied indirect speech, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.344 (of a past promise)}}; ἀποδοτέον.. ὁ. μανεὶς ἀπαιτοῖ we were not [as you remember] to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 332a.}}
**I.2.d** where the principal clause has an _optative_, μηδ᾽ ἀντιάσειας ἐκείνῳ ὁππότε νοστήσειε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.148, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 396c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.6.3.}}
**II** in indirect questions, with _indicative_, ἦ ῥά τι ἴδμεν.. ὁππότε Τηλέμαχος νεῖτα; **when** he is to return, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.633; εἰς ὁ.}}**by what time,** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.99: rarely after a past tense, προσεδέρκετο, δέγμενος αἰεί, ὁππότε δὴ.. ἐφήσει (for ἐφείη, see above 1.2 b) 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.386; εἰς σὲ βλέψαι καὶ τὸν ταμίαν ὁπότ᾽ ἄριστον παραθήσει 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 613.}}
**III** ὁποτεοῦν **at any time whatever,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1049a1.}}
**B** in causal sense, **because, since,** with _indicative_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 749 (assuming variant), 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.125, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 895c, etc.; also ὁπότε γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1699 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.3.7.}}

3699:
** ὅπου**,
_Ionic dialect_ **ὅκου**, Relat., indirect _interrogative_, and _indefinite_ _adverb_ of Place, correlated to ποῦ:
**I** as a Relat., sometimes with _genitive_ loci, τῆς πόλεως ὅπου κάλλιστον στρατοπεδεύσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 415d, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.172; ὅπου βούλοιτο τοῦ δρόμου 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.27; ἔσθ᾽ ὅπου}}**in some places,** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 517 (Lyric poetry), “fragment” 302: standing for the Relat.}} _adjective_, μέλη, ὅπου (i. e. ἐν οἷς){{Refs 5th c.BC+|χελιδὼν ἦν τις ἐμπεποιημένη 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1301}}: with other Particles, ὅκου δή **somewhere or other,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.129}}; ὅπου ἄν **wherever,** with _subjunctive_, {{Refs|“IG” 12.76.11, etc.}}; in Trag. the ἄν may be omitted, as ὅπου δ᾽ Ἀπόλλων σκαιὸς ᾖ, τίνες σοφο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 972, etc. (never in _Attic dialect_ Prose)}}: with _genitive_, ὅπου ἂν τύχῃ τῶν λεγομένων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 342e; ὁπουοῦν}}**anywheresoever,** {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cra.” 403c}}; ὅπουπερ with _optative_, **wherever,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.5; ὅπου ποτέ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 12}}; ὁπουδάν, ={ὅπου δὲ ἄν}, **anywhere,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus “fragment” 109.21}}; ὁπουδήποτε, = **ubicumque,** Dosith.p.410 K.; ὁπουδηποτοῦν **anywheresoever,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “contra Apionem” 2.15.}}
**I.2** in indirect questions, ὄφρα πύθηαι πατρός, ὅπου κύθε γαῖα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.16, compare 16.306, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 924, etc.}}: with Verbs of motion in pregnant sense, ὅκου ἐτράπετο, οὐκέτι εἶχον εἰπεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.119; κεῖνος δ᾽ ὅπου βέβηκεν, οὐδεὶς οἶδε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 40, variant in 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.37, etc.: in many passages editors have in this sense restored ὅποι}} mostly from codices; in repeating a question, ἡ Λακεδαίμων ποῦ 'στι; Answ. ὅπου 'στί; (do you ask) **where** it is? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 214: with _genitive_, εἰδότες ὅκου γῆς εἴη 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.150, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 403e.}}
**II** the strict local sense occasionally passes into,
**II.1** a sense involving Time or Occasion, ὅ. τιν᾽ ἴδῃ {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 922, compare 999, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.3.6; σιγᾶν θ᾽ ὅ. δεῖ καὶ λέγειν τὰ καίρια 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 582, compare “Eu.” 277}}; οὐκ ἔσθ᾽ ὅ. there is no case **in which,** i.e. in no case. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 448, “Aj.” 1069, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 186, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.35}}; so ἔστιν ὅ.; as a question, {{Refs|[prev. author] 18.22.}}
**II.2** of Cause, **whereas,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.68, 4.195, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.7; ὅ. γὰρ ἐγὼ.. ὁμολογῶ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.205}}; ὅκου γε, Latin _quandoquidem_ or **quippe,** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.118; ὅπου γε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.11, etc.}}
**B** later as a _demonstrative_ _adverb_, only in the phrase ὅ. μέν.., ὅ. δέ.. **here.., there..,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.427c, 2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “Πυρρώνειοι ὑποτυπώσεις” 1.53, etc.}}
